/* GENERATED by tools/meta/dissemination/build_microcosm_public_site.py; lazy governed term previews. */
window.__MICROCOSM_TERM_PREVIEWS__ = {"schema_version":"microcosm_term_preview_index_v1","term_count":402,"terms":[{"object_id":"term:above_and_beyond","preferred_label":"above and beyond","reader_preview":"In Plectis, \u201cabove and beyond\u201d means doing the useful follow-through that makes a completed task easy to find, check, and continue.","reader_card":"Finishing a change can leave a broken link, an outdated instruction, or a lesson that exists only in the conversation. This term asks the agent to fix the directly related loose ends while the context is still available.","reader_rule":"The extra work needs a clear stopping point and must fit the user's authorised scope.","reader_deep":"For example, a new feature may need its existing guide updated and its check recorded so the next person can use it."},{"object_id":"term:absolute_convergence","preferred_label":"absolute convergence","reader_preview":"A series converges absolutely if the sum of the absolute values of its terms is finite.","reader_card":"For a real or complex series \u2211 a\u2099, this means \u2211 |a\u2099| converges. It implies convergence of the original series and permits rearranging its terms without changing the sum.","reader_rule":"Ordinary convergence does not imply absolute convergence: the alternating harmonic series is a standard counterexample.","reader_deep":"For nonnegative terms, the two conditions coincide. Absolute convergence is useful in the papers when expanding denominators, regrouping contributions, or bounding an omitted tail by the sum of its magnitudes."},{"object_id":"term:accountability_layer","preferred_label":"accountability layer","reader_preview":"The accountability layer is the evidence and source links that let someone check what the system says it did.","reader_card":"A visible result should lead to the run that produced it, the inputs used, and the claim that was tested. Plectis groups those connections under this name so a reader can inspect the work behind a summary.","reader_rule":"An evidence record supports the particular action or property it captures.","reader_deep":"Keeping the record and its limits together helps expose a claim that has grown broader than the work supporting it."},{"object_id":"term:actor_axes","preferred_label":"actor axes","reader_preview":"Actor axes are separate descriptions of an agent's access, abilities, tools, and role in a task.","reader_card":"Plectis uses several axes because one ranking would mix different things. Type A and Type B describe access to the working system; capability describes what the actor can do; its role describes how it participates in the work.","reader_rule":"A model's reasoning ability does not determine its file permissions or access to evidence.","reader_deep":"The same model can have different authority in two sessions, depending on the tools and material available to it."},{"object_id":"term:admitted_proof_step","preferred_label":"admitted proof step","reader_preview":"An admitted proof step is a gap temporarily accepted as an assumption instead of supplied with a proof.","reader_card":"In Lean, a placeholder such as `sorry` allows unfinished work to compile with a warning. A theorem that depends on it has a proof gap, even if the surrounding steps have been checked.","reader_rule":"Check a theorem's dependencies as well as the file containing its statement.","reader_deep":"Removing visible placeholders is only part of the audit: extra axioms can also introduce unproved assumptions. Ordinary hypotheses are different because the theorem explicitly states that its conclusion depends on them."},{"object_id":"term:affordance_row","preferred_label":"affordance row","reader_preview":"An affordance row is a navigation entry that explains what an item is and what opening it lets you do.","reader_card":"Each row gives enough information to choose a next step: a label, a short explanation, and a route to the relevant material. Plectis can present the same entry as a brief line or a fuller card.","reader_rule":"The row should help a reader choose before loading the underlying files.","reader_deep":"Its source link matters because the menu is a summary; the referenced record or code contains the detail needed to act."},{"object_id":"term:agent","preferred_label":"agent","reader_preview":"An AI agent is software that uses a model and tools to carry out steps towards a task.","reader_card":"An agent may read files, run commands, or propose changes, depending on its tools and permissions. In Plectis, its work is expected to leave inspectable results that another person or check can assess.","reader_rule":"The agent's written account and the evidence of its actions are separate things.","reader_deep":"Type A and Type B describe its access to the working system. They do not rank the model's intelligence or guarantee that its work is correct."},{"object_id":"term:agent_reliability","preferred_label":"agent reliability and safety","reader_preview":"Agent reliability is how consistently an AI agent completes its intended task under the conditions in which it is used.","reader_card":"Plectis studies specific parts of this question, including whether an agent's report matches its actions and whether it respects the limits it was given. Tests deliberately introduce misleading notes or missing work to see whether a check detects them.","reader_rule":"A successful test supports the behaviour and conditions that the test actually covers.","reader_deep":"Safety also concerns harmful actions and permission boundaries. Neither reliability nor safety follows merely from a fluent answer or one passing example."},{"object_id":"term:agent_seed","preferred_label":"agent seed","reader_preview":"An agent seed is a saved record of an agent's observations and interpretations, with its author and sources identified.","reader_card":"Plectis keeps this material separate from raw seed, which preserves the operator's own words. That separation lets useful agent analysis survive a session without being mistaken for something the operator said or intended.","reader_rule":"An agent seed is material to assess and reuse, with authority appropriate to its sources.","reader_deep":"For example, an agent can record a recurring failure and the evidence behind its proposed explanation while preserving the original instruction unchanged."},{"object_id":"term:agent_trace","preferred_label":"agent trace","reader_preview":"An agent trace is a recorded sequence of events from an agent session, such as tool calls, outputs, and file operations.","reader_card":"The trace provides evidence against which to compare the agent's summary. It can show which command was invoked, what result returned, and what happened next, within the events the recording system captures.","reader_rule":"A trace is only as complete as its instrumentation and retention.","reader_deep":"An unrecorded event cannot automatically be treated as an event that never happened. Plectis audits should make that coverage boundary clear when they use traces as evidence."},{"object_id":"term:ai_native","preferred_label":"AI-native","reader_preview":"AI-native describes a system designed from the outset for AI to take part in its normal operation.","reader_card":"In Plectis, agents read structured context, use tools, make bounded changes, and leave records that later work can inspect. The files, interfaces, and checks are arranged to support that participation.","reader_rule":"The term describes a design choice, not a measured level of intelligence or reliability.","reader_deep":"Human direction, permissions, and independent checks still matter. A workflow can be built around AI and still need substantial supervision or correction."},{"object_id":"term:algebraic_number","preferred_label":"algebraic number","reader_preview":"A complex number satisfying some nonzero polynomial equation with integer coefficients.","reader_card":"Every rational number is algebraic because p/q solves qx-p=0. The irrational number \u221a2 is algebraic because it solves x\u00b2-2=0.","reader_rule":"Algebraic does not mean rational. Numbers that satisfy no such integer polynomial, such as \u03c0, are called transcendental.","reader_deep":"The totient discussion explains that a digit-complexity theorem applying to algebraic irrationals cannot be used until algebraicity of the target sum is independently known."},{"object_id":"term:annex","preferred_label":"annex","reader_preview":"An annex is Plectis's source-linked account of ideas or techniques drawn from an outside project.","reader_card":"It records what an external framework, repository, or document offers and how a useful pattern might apply locally. The aim is to make prior work available before an agent invents another implementation.","reader_rule":"The original source remains the authority on what the outside project actually does.","reader_deep":"Adapting a technique requires a separate local decision and check; an annex does not establish that the technique has already been integrated or works here."},{"object_id":"term:anti_principle","preferred_label":"anti-principle","reader_preview":"An anti-principle names a recurring shortcut or failure pattern that the system is meant to avoid.","reader_card":"Where a principle describes a desired way of working, an anti-principle makes a specific bad practice recognisable. It connects the refusal to the requirement it protects, so the rule can be applied to real cases.","reader_rule":"The useful question is which concrete behaviour would violate the rule.","reader_deep":"For example, treating a plausible summary as evidence of a completed action would defeat a requirement to check the actual execution record."},{"object_id":"term:apache_2_0","preferred_label":"Apache-2.0","reader_preview":"Apache-2.0 is an open-source licence allowing use, modification, and redistribution, including commercial use, subject to its conditions.","reader_card":"Redistribution requires a copy of the licence, retention of applicable notices, and prominent notices of changed files. An included NOTICE file can add attribution requirements. The licence also contains a patent grant and its conditions.","reader_rule":"The licence attached to the particular repository or file determines what is covered.","reader_deep":"Apache-2.0 does not grant general trademark rights and provides the work without warranties. The linked licence text controls the exact terms."},{"object_id":"term:appeal_lifecycle","preferred_label":"appeal lifecycle","reader_preview":"An appeal lifecycle is the process for challenging a system decision and recording how the challenge was resolved.","reader_card":"A disputed label or result is examined, its cause is identified, and responsibility for any repair is assigned. The outcome records the reasoning and supporting evidence so later work can tell whether the decision still stands.","reader_rule":"An appeal can confirm a decision, correct it, or reopen the question.","reader_deep":"Plectis uses the record to distinguish a faulty implementation from a faulty expectation: sometimes the check needs repair, and sometimes the rule being checked needs revision."},{"object_id":"term:apply","preferred_label":"apply","reader_preview":"In Plectis, apply is the step that makes a specified change to a file or record.","reader_card":"It follows observation or a proposal by naming the target and performing the permitted operation. The operation should check the conditions for the change and validate the resulting state.","reader_rule":"A proposed change has not taken effect until the apply step succeeds.","reader_deep":"Recording the operation, target, and result makes later inspection possible. Where an operation is reversible, its record can also explain how to undo it without disturbing unrelated work."},{"object_id":"term:arithmetic_progression","preferred_label":"arithmetic progression","reader_preview":"A sequence obtained by repeatedly adding one fixed difference.","reader_card":"The numbers 2,7,12,17,\u2026 form an arithmetic progression with first term 2 and common difference 5; its nth term is 2+5n under zero-based indexing.","reader_rule":"For a nonzero integer common difference d, extending the progression over all integer indices gives one residue class modulo |d|. A geometric progression instead repeatedly multiplies by a fixed ratio.","reader_deep":"Dirichlet's theorem supplies primes in suitable arithmetic progressions, letting the totient argument engineer prescribed congruence and fresh-prime behavior."},{"object_id":"term:artifact","preferred_label":"artifact","reader_preview":"An artifact is a stored item that someone can inspect or use, such as a file, report, dataset, or generated page.","reader_card":"Plectis uses the term for the concrete objects its work produces and maintains. An artifact has an identity and a role; it may be original source material, a record of an action, or a view generated from other data.","reader_rule":"Its role determines how it should be read and changed.","reader_deep":"A report about a test and the test's source code are both artifacts, but they provide different kinds of evidence."},{"object_id":"term:artifact_kind","preferred_label":"artifact kind","reader_preview":"An artifact kind is a category of stored object, such as a standard, paper module, component, or result record.","reader_card":"The category helps Plectis choose the right schema, editing rules, and navigation view for an item. A reader can start with a collection of one kind before choosing an individual object.","reader_rule":"Two artifacts can concern the same subject while belonging to different kinds.","reader_deep":"For example, a component implements a check, a standard states its requirements, and a result record describes one execution of it."},{"object_id":"term:assimilation","preferred_label":"assimilation","reader_preview":"Assimilation is the process of incorporating a useful result into the system so later work can use it.","reader_card":"After a finding has been checked and explained, Plectis puts it into the appropriate source, guide, rule, or record. The result then becomes available through the routes that future work already uses.","reader_rule":"A note left only in a conversation has not completed that process.","reader_deep":"Assimilation includes preserving the finding's scope and evidence. A successful local example should remain a local example unless there is support for a more general rule."},{"object_id":"term:asymptotic_bound","preferred_label":"asymptotic bound","reader_preview":"An asymptotic bound compares the sizes of functions once their input is sufficiently large or approaches a specified limit.","reader_card":"For example, f(n) = O(g(n)) as n tends to infinity means |f(n)| \u2264 C|g(n)| for some fixed C and all sufficiently large n. The notation hides that constant and initial threshold.","reader_rule":"It is a bound on growth, not an exact formula or necessarily an estimate valid at small inputs.","reader_deep":"The papers use such comparisons to constrain potential counterexamples. Constants and parameter dependence must be recovered when an argument needs an explicit finite range."},{"object_id":"term:atlas","preferred_label":"Atlas","reader_preview":"The Atlas is Plectis's navigable map of the system's parts and their relationships.","reader_card":"It helps a reader or agent move from an overview to a particular component, source, work record, or check. Its entries are derived from the system's stored information so the map can be refreshed as that information changes.","reader_rule":"The public site exposes a selected part of the wider system map.","reader_deep":"An Atlas entry is a route to inspect something; appearing on the map alone does not establish that the object is complete, current, or validated."},{"object_id":"term:audit","preferred_label":"audit","reader_preview":"An audit is a systematic examination of records against stated requirements; here it often checks whether an agent's report matches its actions.","reader_card":"For example, a completion audit can compare a claim that tests passed with the commands and results recorded for that session. This is narrower than deciding whether the tests adequately assess the software.","reader_rule":"Read the audit's stated criteria to see which question it answers.","reader_deep":"An accurate report of a poorly tested change can pass a report-faithfulness audit. Assessing the underlying change requires additional evidence and appropriate checks."},{"object_id":"term:authority","preferred_label":"authority","reader_preview":"Authority is the recognised basis for settling a particular question or permitting an action.","reader_card":"Plectis assigns authority by subject. Source code establishes what an implementation contains; an execution record reports a captured run; the operator's instructions determine intended scope. Each source can settle some questions and leave others unanswered.","reader_rule":"Identify both the source and the kind of decision it can support.","reader_deep":"A generated page can help you find authoritative material, but a convenient or confident summary does not acquire the authority of the source it describes."},{"object_id":"term:axiom","preferred_label":"axiom","reader_preview":"An axiom is a starting assumption of a mathematical theory; Plectis also uses the word for a fundamental system rule.","reader_card":"A mathematical proof derives its conclusion from its hypotheses and underlying axioms. On the software pages, a system axiom instead states a design constraint that other rules are expected to preserve.","reader_rule":"The surrounding subject determines which meaning is intended.","reader_deep":"For a Lean theorem, the relevant question is which logical assumptions its proof depends on. For a Plectis design rule, the question is how the implementation enforces it and what evidence shows that enforcement."},{"object_id":"term:band","preferred_label":"band","reader_preview":"A band is a named level of detail in a description, such as a brief label, a short summary, or a full explanation.","reader_card":"Plectis uses bands to let readers choose how much context to open. A compression profile specifies what each level must preserve and how it leads to the fuller source.","reader_rule":"A shorter band should reduce reading effort without changing the claim.","reader_deep":"For example, a glossary preview orients a reader quickly, while the expanded entry supplies the distinctions and examples needed to use the term accurately."},{"object_id":"term:barrier","preferred_label":"barrier","reader_preview":"A mathematical barrier is an obstruction showing why a specified approach cannot establish its intended result under stated conditions.","reader_card":"A barrier theorem proves a limitation of a method or construction. In these papers, such results help identify which attempted routes have failed and what a future approach would have to change.","reader_rule":"Check the hypotheses and the precise class of approaches ruled out.","reader_deep":"A method failing does not show that the original problem is impossible. A barrier can be useful progress by preventing repetition and revealing which assumption caused the obstruction."},{"object_id":"term:base","preferred_label":"base","reader_preview":"A numeral base is the number whose powers determine place values; decimal uses ten and binary uses two.","reader_card":"In an integer base b greater than one, the allowed digits are 0 through b\u22121. Places after the radix point have weights 1/b, 1/b\u00b2, and so on. The papers also use b as the base in expressions such as b\u207f.","reader_rule":"Read the stated restrictions on b before applying an argument about digits or divisibility.","reader_deep":"Changing the base changes an expansion's digits and repetition pattern while leaving the represented number itself unchanged."},{"object_id":"term:basis","preferred_label":"basis","reader_preview":"In linear algebra, a basis is a set of vectors from which every vector in a space has a unique finite linear combination.","reader_card":"The two requirements are spanning and linear independence: the vectors reach the whole space, and none can be expressed using the others. For the ordinary plane, one horizontal and one vertical unit vector form a basis.","reader_rule":"Other fields use \u201cbasis\u201d for different structures, so the surrounding definition matters.","reader_deep":"Plectis also speaks of a named basis for a claim: the stated sources or assumptions supporting it. That use does not assert a linear-algebra structure."},{"object_id":"term:binary_expansion","preferred_label":"binary expansion","reader_preview":"A binary expansion writes a number with digits 0 and 1, using powers of two as place values.","reader_card":"For example, 0.101 in binary is 1/2 + 0/4 + 1/8, or 5/8. The digit positions turn questions about a real number into questions about a sequence of zeros and ones.","reader_rule":"In base two, rational numbers have eventually repeating expansions.","reader_deep":"Numbers with a terminating expansion also have an alternative ending in repeating ones. Arguments about uniqueness or periodicity must account for this familiar ambiguity."},{"object_id":"term:bound","preferred_label":"bound","reader_preview":"A bound is a value that a quantity is proved to stay above or below under stated conditions.","reader_card":"An upper bound M gives x \u2264 M; a lower bound m gives m \u2264 x. Bounds can also depend on a parameter, such as the number of terms in a sum.","reader_rule":"A bound need not equal the quantity it controls or be the best possible bound.","reader_deep":"In these papers, bounds often control the error left after a finite calculation, allowing a statement about an infinite process to follow from finite information."},{"object_id":"term:bridge","preferred_label":"bridge","reader_preview":"A bridge is Plectis's mechanism for sending a bounded task to an outside worker and returning its response.","reader_card":"The controlling agent selects the instructions and context to send. The returned material is then assessed before it is incorporated into the working system. This exchange can support an external reasoning or research pass.","reader_rule":"The bridge itself does not grant the outside worker direct control of the local files.","reader_deep":"The worker's answer remains a proposal or source of evidence until the receiving agent checks it and performs any authorised changes."},{"object_id":"term:bundle","preferred_label":"bundle","reader_preview":"A bundle is a group of related files packaged together for inspection or use.","reader_card":"For a Plectis component, an evidence bundle can include the source code, test inputs, and result record associated with a run. Keeping them together lets a reader inspect which inputs were used, which checks ran, and what those checks returned.","reader_rule":"The bundle's inventory and source versions determine what it actually contains.","reader_deep":"A bundle may document a recorded run or provide material for repeating it. The accompanying instructions should say which of those uses it supports."},{"object_id":"term:canonicalisation","preferred_label":"canonicalisation","reader_preview":"Canonicalisation is the process of putting something into a standard, representative form.","reader_card":"In the mathematical research discussion here, it means consolidating a result into a natural statement and an understood proof that others can reuse. Equivalent formulations and routine supporting lemmas may become part of one clearer account.","reader_rule":"A repository can simplify its own presentation, but it cannot declare that the mathematical community has adopted it.","reader_deep":"This is a later question than whether a proof checks: it concerns which formulation and explanation become useful, recognised tools for other work."},{"object_id":"term:care","preferred_label":"care","reader_preview":"Care means attending to the people, correctness, and future maintenance affected by a piece of work.","reader_card":"Plectis uses the word as a practical standard for an agent's conduct. A change should be understandable, checked to the appropriate extent, and left in a state that another person can continue.","reader_rule":"Care is expressed through decisions and follow-through, rather than through enthusiastic language.","reader_deep":"For example, preserving unrelated edits and updating an instruction made inaccurate by a change both reduce avoidable costs for the next person working on the system."},{"object_id":"term:catalog","preferred_label":"catalog","reader_preview":"A catalog is an organised inventory; on Plectis's architecture page it lists patterns available for use in the system.","reader_card":"Each catalog entry identifies a pattern and provides the information needed to select it. The architecture then connects the selected pattern to requirements, an implementation route, and evidence from its use.","reader_rule":"This particular catalog is distinct from the site's collection of papers or skills.","reader_deep":"Generating the inventory from registered source material makes omissions and stale entries easier to detect, although the generated catalog still needs to be refreshed and checked."},{"object_id":"term:certificate","preferred_label":"certificate","reader_preview":"A certificate is data that a specified checker can use to verify a particular claim.","reader_card":"The data might provide a witness, a calculation trace, or the finite cases needed for a result. Producing the certificate and checking it can be separate tasks, with different costs and trust requirements.","reader_rule":"The guarantee depends on what the checker verifies and how that check connects to the claim.","reader_deep":"A collection of verified cases establishes a general theorem only when a further argument shows that those cases cover everything the theorem asserts."},{"object_id":"term:challenge_solution_pair","preferred_label":"Challenge/Solution pair","reader_preview":"A Challenge/Solution pair packages a precise Lean problem with a proposed solution that can be checked against it.","reader_card":"The challenge states the required result and allowed assumptions. The solution supplies the implementation or proof intended to meet that requirement. Plectis uses the pair as an independently inspectable unit in its Comparator and Palomar material.","reader_rule":"The solution must satisfy the actual challenge, including its hypotheses and restrictions.","reader_deep":"Checking one pair establishes that result within its stated setting. It does not automatically settle neighbouring statements or the original open problem from which the challenge was derived."},{"object_id":"term:check","preferred_label":"check","reader_preview":"A check is an examination of whether something satisfies a stated condition.","reader_card":"On this site, a check usually has a runnable command, specified inputs, and a recorded result. It may test a behaviour, validate a file, compare copies, or verify a proof.","reader_rule":"The result should say what was examined and which condition passed or failed.","reader_deep":"Useful checks can distinguish relevant failures from success. Reading a recorded result tells you about that run; repeating the command supplies evidence about your own copy and environment."},{"object_id":"term:checker","preferred_label":"checker","reader_preview":"A checker is a program or procedure that evaluates an input against specified rules.","reader_card":"A syntax checker, link checker, and proof checker each answer a different question. Their outputs are useful when the rules, inputs, and failure conditions are clear enough to inspect.","reader_rule":"A pass establishes satisfaction of the implemented rules, subject to the checker's own correctness.","reader_deep":"The rules may still omit something important to the wider task. Plectis therefore connects checker results to specific claims rather than treating every pass as general approval."},{"object_id":"term:chinese_remainder_theorem","preferred_label":"Chinese remainder theorem","reader_preview":"The Chinese remainder theorem solves simultaneous congruences with pairwise coprime positive moduli.","reader_card":"It says that any prescribed remainders occur together in exactly one class modulo the product of the moduli. For example, x \u2261 1 modulo 2 and x \u2261 2 modulo 3 combine to x \u2261 5 modulo 6.","reader_rule":"Without coprimality, the prescribed remainders must satisfy compatibility conditions.","reader_deep":"The papers use the theorem to arrange several divisibility conditions at once. It gives a controlled arithmetic construction rather than merely evidence that a computer found one suitable integer."},{"object_id":"term:circuit_break","preferred_label":"circuit-break","reader_preview":"To circuit-break a process is to interrupt a loop that would otherwise keep running or reinforcing itself.","reader_card":"In Plectis's research discussion, the loop is self-confirmation: the same people or tools produce work and judge its value. An outside reader can interrupt that loop by questioning the evidence, interpretation, or direction.","reader_rule":"An interruption prompts reassessment; it does not itself decide which conclusion is true.","reader_deep":"Automated checks remain useful for errors they can detect. Independent judgement addresses questions of meaning and value that repeating the internal checks may never raise."},{"object_id":"term:claim","preferred_label":"claim","reader_preview":"A claim is an assertion that can be examined for truth or support.","reader_card":"Plectis records what a component or mathematical result is said to establish and links that wording to evidence. This makes it possible to compare the public explanation with the source, proof, or recorded test.","reader_rule":"The strength of the wording should match the strength and scope of the evidence.","reader_deep":"For example, a successful test on specified inputs supports that tested behaviour. A claim covering every possible input requires a different argument or a suitable proof."},{"object_id":"term:claim_registry","preferred_label":"claim registry","reader_preview":"A claim registry is a structured list of assertions and the evidence, scope, and status attached to each.","reader_card":"For the formal mathematics, it connects reader-facing statements to particular declarations or other supporting material. This gives reviewers a way to check that the explanation says what the mathematics actually establishes.","reader_rule":"Registration records how a claim is presented and supported; it is not peer review.","reader_deep":"When the wording or proof changes, their connection must be checked again. A large number of formal declarations does not by itself imply an equally large number of distinct research contributions."},{"object_id":"term:clone","preferred_label":"clone","reader_preview":"To clone a repository is to create a local copy of its files and Git history, with options to limit what is downloaded.","reader_card":"A normal full clone lets you inspect earlier revisions and work on the project locally. A shallow clone downloads limited history; other options can defer file contents or restrict the working checkout.","reader_rule":"The public repositories linked here can be cloned for local inspection and checks.","reader_deep":"A clone records a particular state. To compare your results with a published example, use the stated revision and environment rather than assuming that every copy contains the same files."},{"object_id":"term:closed_route","preferred_label":"closed route","reader_preview":"A closed route is a recorded approach that has been ruled out within a stated set of assumptions or methods.","reader_card":"The working records keep the reason for closure, such as a counterexample or a proved obstruction. This lets later researchers understand the failure before trying a similar idea.","reader_rule":"Closure applies to the described approach, not automatically to the original problem.","reader_deep":"A materially different method or hypothesis may avoid the obstruction. The record should make clear what would have to change, and whether the closure rests on proof, exact calculation, or weaker evidence."},{"object_id":"term:closed_set","preferred_label":"closed set","reader_preview":"A set containing all of its limit points; equivalently, its complement is open.","reader_card":"The closed unit disc {z:|z|\u22641} includes its boundary circle. If a sequence of its points converges, the limit remains in the disc.","reader_rule":"Closed does not mean bounded: the entire real line is closed. A set can be both open and closed, depending on the surrounding space.","reader_deep":"Closed discs and closed coefficient classes retain limiting configurations, which is essential when compactness arguments pass from approximating polynomials to a limit."},{"object_id":"term:closeout","preferred_label":"closeout","reader_preview":"A closeout is the final record of a unit of work: what changed, what was checked, and what remains unresolved.","reader_card":"It gives the next person enough information to continue without reconstructing the session. Plectis expects the record to distinguish completed work, deliberate scope limits, and unfinished obligations.","reader_rule":"Claims of completion should point to the resulting files or execution evidence.","reader_deep":"A useful closeout also explains any condition that should trigger another pass. That makes a bounded stopping point understandable without implying that every related problem has been solved."},{"object_id":"term:code_map","preferred_label":"Code Map","reader_preview":"Code Map is a generated view of the code's parts and their dependencies.","reader_card":"It helps readers see which modules belong together and which other modules they use. Plectis derives this view from source information so the diagram can be refreshed when the implementation changes.","reader_rule":"Open the linked source when a dependency matters to a proposed change.","reader_deep":"The map reflects the relationships its analysis captures. Dynamic behaviour or indirect connections may require further inspection, and the view can become stale if it has not been rebuilt."},{"object_id":"term:coding_agent","preferred_label":"coding agent","reader_preview":"A coding agent is an AI agent equipped to work with source files and development tools.","reader_card":"Depending on its permissions, it can inspect a repository, edit code, run commands, and examine results. Plectis provides routes and checks that let a person use such an agent to work on a bounded task.","reader_rule":"Its report should distinguish actions it performed from actions it merely recommends.","reader_deep":"Tool access makes execution possible; it does not guarantee a sound solution. The resulting change still needs appropriate validation, and the agent's permissions remain separate from its reasoning ability."},{"object_id":"term:coefficient","preferred_label":"coefficient","reader_preview":"A number multiplying a variable, power, basis element, or series term.","reader_card":"In 3z\u00b2-7z+4, the coefficients are 3, -7, and 4. A missing power has coefficient zero: the coefficient of z\u00b3 in this example is 0.","reader_rule":"A coefficient may be an integer, rational, real, or complex number depending on context. In a series, coefficients form a sequence and need not be polynomial coefficients.","reader_deep":"Plectis uses coefficients both to describe polynomial families and to weight infinite series, so the surrounding formula determines which meaning applies."},{"object_id":"term:cofinal","preferred_label":"cofinal","reader_preview":"A subset of an ordered set is cofinal if it reaches at least as far as every element of that set.","reader_card":"More precisely, for each x there is an element y in the subset with x \u2264 y. For indices in the natural numbers, this means that qualifying indices occur arbitrarily far out.","reader_rule":"Cofinality does not mean that every sufficiently large index qualifies.","reader_deep":"For example, the even natural numbers are cofinal among all natural numbers. This distinction matters when a proof needs infinitely many suitable indices rather than a property that eventually holds everywhere."},{"object_id":"term:cold_agent","preferred_label":"cold agent","reader_preview":"A cold agent starts a task without the earlier conversation or session context.","reader_card":"It must recover the task's meaning and current state from available files, instructions, and navigation routes. Plectis uses this situation to judge whether knowledge has been preserved well enough for work to continue.","reader_rule":"\u201cCold\u201d describes missing context, not a lack of reasoning ability.","reader_deep":"A source link, clear status, and recorded next step can let a new agent resume correctly. A conclusion kept only in a previous conversation cannot do that."},{"object_id":"term:cold_clone","preferred_label":"cold clone","reader_preview":"A cold clone is a fresh repository checkout used to test whether someone new can follow the published instructions.","reader_card":"The test starts without the author's local caches, private files, or unrecorded setup. It checks whether the stated dependencies and commands are enough to reproduce the advertised example.","reader_rule":"Any additional setup must be stated for the test to be meaningful.","reader_deep":"A successful cold-clone run supports portability to that tested environment. It does not promise identical behaviour on every operating system, dependency version, or machine."},{"object_id":"term:collaboration","preferred_label":"collaboration","reader_preview":"Collaboration is people working together on a shared task or result.","reader_card":"Here it can take the form of a proof, correction, explanation, implementation, or useful account of a failed approach. The public records give contributors something concrete to inspect and improve together.","reader_rule":"Attribution should identify the people responsible for the work and describe tool assistance accurately.","reader_deep":"Agreement with the project is not required to help it: a well-supported correction or objection can be as useful as extending an existing result."},{"object_id":"term:combinatorics","preferred_label":"combinatorics","reader_preview":"Combinatorics studies how discrete objects can be selected, arranged, counted, and related.","reader_card":"Typical questions concern subsets, permutations, graphs, and patterns that must occur in sufficiently large collections. Some problems ask for an exact count; others ask whether a configuration exists at all.","reader_rule":"The objects and allowed arrangements must be specified before a counting argument applies.","reader_deep":"In the papers here, combinatorial structure can turn a broad number-theory question into a finite collection of cases or expose patterns that an arithmetic argument must address."},{"object_id":"term:common_sense","preferred_label":"common sense","reader_preview":"In Plectis, common sense means choosing a proportionate action using the task, available evidence, and relevant constraints.","reader_card":"It applies when no instruction spells out every detail. An agent should infer routine next steps, inspect what already exists, and choose an action that advances the user's aim without silently widening it.","reader_rule":"Judgement remains accountable to evidence, permissions, and the consequences of being wrong.","reader_deep":"For example, updating a link broken by one's own change is ordinary follow-through; starting an unrelated redesign requires a different justification."},{"object_id":"term:compact","preferred_label":"compact","reader_preview":"In the software descriptions, compact means short enough to scan while preserving the information needed for its purpose.","reader_card":"A compact card may omit implementation detail while keeping the claim, its limits, and a route to the full source. Its usefulness depends on which information the reader needs to make a decision.","reader_rule":"Shorter wording is helpful only if it preserves the relevant distinctions.","reader_deep":"Mathematics also has a precise topological meaning of compact, concerning finite subcovers of open covers. That is a separate meaning, defined by the mathematical setting."},{"object_id":"term:compactness","preferred_label":"compactness","reader_preview":"A space is compact if every collection of open sets covering it has a finite subcollection that still covers it.","reader_card":"In Euclidean space, a set is compact exactly when it is closed and bounded. For metric spaces, compactness is also equivalent to every sequence having a subsequence converging to a point in the set.","reader_rule":"These equivalent tests require their stated setting; boundedness alone is not enough in general.","reader_deep":"The papers use compactness to extract limits from families of candidates. A separate continuity or closure argument is needed to show that the limit retains the required property."},{"object_id":"term:comparator","preferred_label":"Comparator","reader_preview":"Comparator is a Lean tool for checking a submitted solution against a separately specified challenge.","reader_card":"Keeping the challenge separate helps detect a submission that proves a different or weakened statement. The challenge and checking configuration determine the requirements, including any restrictions on assumptions.","reader_rule":"Read the checked pair and configuration to see exactly what was accepted.","reader_deep":"Plectis uses this separation to support external inspection of formal results. A successful comparison concerns the specified challenge; it does not establish the importance of the result or its agreement with an informal claim."},{"object_id":"term:compatibility_route","preferred_label":"compatibility route","reader_preview":"A compatibility route keeps an old address or identifier usable after an item has moved or been renamed.","reader_card":"It may be a redirect, alias, or explanatory page leading to the current location. Plectis keeps such routes so existing citations and bookmarks can still reach the intended material.","reader_rule":"The destination identifies the current item; the old address preserves continuity.","reader_deep":"A migration record can state the old route, new route, and expected behaviour. This makes it possible to test the transition instead of relying on readers to discover the move themselves."},{"object_id":"term:complex_number","preferred_label":"complex number","reader_preview":"A number a+bi with real a,b and i\u00b2=-1.","reader_card":"The complex number 3+2i is represented by the point (3,2) in the complex plane. Its modulus |3+2i|=\u221a13 is its distance from 0.","reader_rule":"Real numbers are the special case b=0. Addition works coordinatewise, while multiplication uses i\u00b2=-1 and combines rotation with scaling.","reader_deep":"Every nonconstant polynomial with complex coefficients has a complex root and factors fully into linear factors. Its modulus level sets form the lemniscate geometry studied here."},{"object_id":"term:component","preferred_label":"component","reader_preview":"A component is a distinct part of a system with a defined job and interfaces to other parts.","reader_card":"Plectis presents public components with their inputs, outputs, source, and checks. This lets a reader inspect one mechanism without first understanding the entire system.","reader_rule":"The component's description should make clear which behaviour it implements and tests.","reader_deep":"Combining components can introduce additional interactions. Evidence for each part helps assess the combination, but the combined behaviour may require its own checks and an explanation of how the parts fit together."},{"object_id":"term:compression","preferred_label":"compression","reader_preview":"Compression reduces the amount of information needed to store, transmit, or read something.","reader_card":"In Plectis's navigation, it means producing a shorter view of source material under rules about what must be preserved. The view keeps a route to the fuller source and makes important omissions or limits recoverable.","reader_rule":"This use allows deliberate loss of detail, so preserving meaning requires judgement and checks.","reader_deep":"A glossary preview, for example, should retain the defining idea while leaving examples and qualifications to the expanded entry."},{"object_id":"term:compression_profile","preferred_label":"compression profile","reader_preview":"A compression profile specifies how one kind of material should be presented at different levels of detail.","reader_card":"It defines what a short view must include, what it may omit, and how it links to the fuller account. Plectis uses profiles to make summaries of the same artifact kind follow consistent expectations.","reader_rule":"The profile governs the shape of a summary; the source governs its facts.","reader_deep":"For example, a result card may always need its claim and evidence status even when a very short version leaves out the supporting calculation."},{"object_id":"term:computer_science","preferred_label":"computer science","reader_preview":"Computer science is the study of computation, algorithms, information, and the systems that process them.","reader_card":"It includes theoretical questions about what can be computed as well as practical questions about languages, software, hardware, and reliable execution. CS is the usual abbreviation.","reader_rule":"Formal verification is one part of this wider field.","reader_deep":"On this site, computer-science contributions may concern the architecture, tools, checks, or reproducibility of the research workflow. They can improve how the mathematical work is produced and examined without themselves proving a mathematical claim."},{"object_id":"term:concept","preferred_label":"concept","reader_preview":"A concept is an idea or category used to understand and organise a subject.","reader_card":"Plectis also uses Concept as a structured record type: a recurring idea receives a stable identifier, definition, sources, and relationships. Other records can then refer to it consistently.","reader_rule":"The record explains what the idea denotes; procedures describe how to act on it.","reader_deep":"This separation helps detect vocabulary drift. If two mechanisms use the same name for different things, their shared concept reference provides a place to examine and resolve the mismatch."},{"object_id":"term:conditional_reduction","preferred_label":"conditional reduction","reader_preview":"A conditional reduction proves that a target statement follows if a specified further condition holds.","reader_card":"The implication can be a complete theorem even while the condition remains unresolved. The reduction is useful when it exposes a more tractable question or clarifies exactly what additional evidence is needed.","reader_rule":"The target is still open until the required condition is established or another proof is found.","reader_deep":"A one-way implication need not give an equivalent problem. The new condition may be stronger than the target, so its difficulty and plausibility need separate assessment."},{"object_id":"term:conditional_theorem","preferred_label":"conditional theorem","reader_preview":"A conditional theorem proves a conclusion under stated hypotheses.","reader_card":"Mathematical theorems commonly have hypotheses. Here the label is especially useful when an important hypothesis remains unproved, so the theorem establishes an implication without yet establishing the desired conclusion on its own.","reader_rule":"Read the assumptions together with the conclusion, including assumptions supplied as parameters in Lean.","reader_deep":"A checked proof of \u201cif H, then C\u201d can be fully valid while H and C remain open. It is different from a proof containing an admitted step."},{"object_id":"term:congruence","preferred_label":"congruence","reader_preview":"Two integers are congruent modulo m if their difference is divisible by m.","reader_card":"For a positive modulus, this is equivalent to having the same remainder. Thus 17 \u2261 2 modulo 5 because 17 \u2212 2 = 15 is divisible by 5.","reader_rule":"Congruence is weaker than equality: infinitely many different integers can be congruent to one another.","reader_deep":"The papers use congruences to keep only the remainder information relevant to an argument. A nonzero remainder can rule out divisibility even when the original expression is too large to inspect directly."},{"object_id":"term:conjecture","preferred_label":"conjecture","reader_preview":"A conjecture is a precise mathematical claim proposed as true but not yet established by proof.","reader_card":"Examples, calculations, and partial results may make a conjecture plausible. A proof must cover its full statement; a counterexample must satisfy its hypotheses while violating its conclusion.","reader_rule":"Evidence from many cases does not by itself settle a claim about all cases.","reader_deep":"The papers can give equivalent formulations or sufficient conditions for a conjecture. Those are useful results, but the conjecture stays unresolved unless the additional work actually establishes or refutes it."},{"object_id":"term:connected_component","preferred_label":"connected component","reader_preview":"A connected component is a connected subset that cannot be enlarged while remaining connected within the surrounding space.","reader_card":"Connected means that the part cannot be separated into two disjoint, nonempty relatively open pieces. Each point belongs to exactly one connected component.","reader_rule":"Connectedness need not provide a continuous path between every pair of points in an arbitrary space.","reader_deep":"In the lemniscate papers, components distinguish separate regions around polynomial roots. Statements requiring an actual path or a length bound need more than merely identifying which connected component contains a point."},{"object_id":"term:connected_set","preferred_label":"connected set","reader_preview":"A set that cannot be split into two disjoint nonempty open pieces relative to that set.","reader_card":"An interval is connected, while two separated intervals are not. Connectedness says the set is one topological piece even when no particular path has yet been supplied.","reader_rule":"Path connected implies connected, but the converse can fail in general spaces. Do not silently replace a connected-set claim by a stronger path claim.","reader_deep":"The lower-bound argument applies to every connected set containing two selected roots, so it is stronger than checking only straight segments or one chosen curve."},{"object_id":"term:context_packet","preferred_label":"context packet","reader_preview":"A context packet is a selected collection of information supplied for a particular task.","reader_card":"Plectis uses packets to give an agent the relevant question, source routes, constraints, and current evidence without requiring it to read the whole repository. Public reader packets serve a similar orientation purpose.","reader_rule":"A packet should make its scope and source versions clear.","reader_deep":"Selection necessarily leaves things out. Links to the underlying material let a reader recover missing detail and check whether a summary still agrees with the sources it describes."},{"object_id":"term:contradiction","preferred_label":"contradiction","reader_preview":"A contradiction is an impossibility obtained from assumptions, such as a statement and its negation both being true.","reader_card":"In proof by contradiction, one assumes the opposite of the desired conclusion and derives such an impossibility. Classical reasoning then rules out that opposite assumption.","reader_rule":"Each inference must follow from the assumptions actually available at that point.","reader_deep":"For example, an irrationality argument may assume a number equals a fraction in lowest terms, then show both numerator and denominator must share a factor. That conflicts with the lowest-terms assumption."},{"object_id":"term:contrapositive","preferred_label":"contrapositive","reader_preview":"In classical logic, the contrapositive of \u201cif P, then Q\u201d is the equivalent statement \u201cif not Q, then not P\u201d.","reader_card":"For example, \u201cevery multiple of 4 is even\u201d gives \u201can odd integer is not a multiple of 4\u201d. Either version expresses the same implication through a different route.","reader_rule":"The converse, \u201cif Q, then P\u201d, is a different statement and need not hold.","reader_deep":"The papers often prove that rationality would force a property, then establish failure of that property to deduce irrationality. The reasoning must negate the exact consequence, with its quantifiers intact."},{"object_id":"term:contribute","preferred_label":"contribute","reader_preview":"To contribute is to supply work that helps a shared project advance or correct itself.","reader_card":"Here useful contributions include proofs, counterexamples, clearer explanations, software fixes, and reproducible reports of failed approaches. The public repositories provide concrete material against which a contribution can be inspected.","reader_rule":"Acceptance and attribution should follow the work and evidence, not the confidence of its presentation.","reader_deep":"A mathematical result remains attributable to the people who established it. Using Plectis as a tool or starting point does not transfer priority for their result to the project."},{"object_id":"term:contributor_credit","preferred_label":"contributor credit","reader_preview":"Contributor credit records who supplied a piece of work and what that contribution was.","reader_card":"Plectis aims to preserve attribution for accepted results, corrections, and other useful work, including when later work builds on them. Describing the contribution makes the credit more informative than simply listing a name.","reader_rule":"Human authorship and the use of AI or other tools should be recorded distinctly.","reader_deep":"The project's contribution record is not a substitute for scholarly priority or publication conventions. A result's authors retain credit for establishing it, including when this system helped them work."},{"object_id":"term:convergence","preferred_label":"convergence","reader_preview":"Convergence means approaching a specified limit; for an infinite series, it means that its partial sums approach a finite value.","reader_card":"For a real sequence, every chosen positive tolerance must eventually contain all later terms around the limit. For a series, apply that condition to the sums of the first n terms.","reader_rule":"Terms tending to zero are necessary for a series to converge, but are not sufficient.","reader_deep":"For example, 1 + 1/2 + 1/3 + \u22ef diverges. Proving convergence also leaves separate questions about whether the sum is rational, algebraic, or transcendental."},{"object_id":"term:coprime","preferred_label":"coprime","reader_preview":"Two integers are coprime if their greatest common divisor is 1.","reader_card":"They have no positive common divisor other than 1, or equivalently no common prime factor. For example, 8 and 15 are coprime even though neither is prime.","reader_rule":"A fraction is in lowest terms when its numerator and denominator are coprime.","reader_deep":"In modular arithmetic, an integer has a multiplicative inverse modulo m exactly when it is coprime to m. This is why coprimality often appears among the hypotheses of statements about multiplicative order."},{"object_id":"term:corpus","preferred_label":"corpus","reader_preview":"A corpus is a collection of material studied or managed as a body of work.","reader_card":"The site uses the term for collections such as formal declarations, papers, or recorded examples. A particular revision or manifest can fix which items belong to the collection being described.","reader_rule":"Any reported count or check should identify the corpus and version it covers.","reader_deep":"A corpus can grow or change over time. Results about one snapshot should not silently be treated as results about every later version or every related repository."},{"object_id":"term:counterexample","preferred_label":"counterexample","reader_preview":"A counterexample is a case that satisfies a claim's assumptions but makes its conclusion false.","reader_card":"One counterexample disproves a universal statement. For instance, 2 is a counterexample to \u201cevery prime number is odd\u201d, because it is prime and even.","reader_rule":"An example outside the assumptions does not refute the statement.","reader_deep":"Counterexamples in the working records may target an attempted lemma or method rather than the original open problem. Naming the exact statement they refute preserves that distinction and explains what a revised approach must avoid."},{"object_id":"term:critic_posture","preferred_label":"critic posture","reader_preview":"Critic posture is a deliberate way of examining an idea for weaknesses before relying on it.","reader_card":"Plectis uses it to ask concrete questions: whether an abstraction fits the evidence, whether a proposed route can be executed, and whether a necessary rule or check is missing.","reader_rule":"A useful criticism identifies the affected claim and the evidence that would resolve it.","reader_deep":"This turns an objection into work that can be assessed. The critic's role is to test the idea, while any resulting change still needs an owner and validation."},{"object_id":"term:critical_point","preferred_label":"critical point","reader_preview":"For a smooth real-valued function, a critical point is an input where all first partial derivatives vanish.","reader_card":"For a complex polynomial p, a critical point c satisfies p\u2032(c) = 0. For p(z) = z\u00b2 \u2212 1, the critical point is 0, whereas the roots are 1 and \u22121. In several real variables, all first partial derivatives must vanish together.","reader_rule":"A critical point and a root need not be the same point.","reader_deep":"Some elementary conventions also include points of nondifferentiability. The polynomial setting here is differentiable everywhere, and critical points matter because the Newton expression \u2212p/p\u2032 is undefined there."},{"object_id":"term:critical_value","preferred_label":"critical value","reader_preview":"A critical value is the output of a function at one of its critical points.","reader_card":"For p(z) = z\u00b2 \u2212 1, the critical point 0 has critical value \u22121. The point and value lie on different sides of the function: one is an input and the other is its image.","reader_rule":"The modulus |p(c)| is the magnitude of a critical value, not the value itself.","reader_deep":"The lemniscate arguments compare these magnitudes and directions to study how regions can meet. Distinct critical points can share a critical value, and different values can have the same magnitude."},{"object_id":"term:declaration","preferred_label":"declaration","reader_preview":"A declaration introduces a named item in a programming or proof language, such as a definition, theorem, or structure.","reader_card":"In Lean, the declaration's source states its type and, where applicable, its value or proof. Later declarations can use that name, creating a chain of dependencies.","reader_rule":"A declaration count combines different kinds of items and is not a count of distinct mathematical discoveries.","reader_deep":"To assess a theorem, inspect its exact statement, proof dependencies, and assumptions. Supporting definitions and routine lemmas may be necessary without being separate research contributions."},{"object_id":"term:demo_take","preferred_label":"Demo Take","reader_preview":"Demo Take is the system's recording tool for capturing a demonstration and its accompanying material.","reader_card":"It brings screen recording, transcript capture, and still frames into one workflow. The resulting files can then support a prepared demonstration without reconstructing the session from separate recordings.","reader_rule":"A recorded demonstration shows the particular run and material that were captured.","reader_deep":"It is useful for explanation and review, while repeatability still depends on the source, inputs, environment, and commands behind what appears on screen."},{"object_id":"term:denominator","preferred_label":"denominator","reader_preview":"The denominator is the divisor in a fraction: the b in a/b, where b is nonzero.","reader_card":"In 3/4, the denominator is 4. For a positive fraction interpreted as equal-sized parts, it specifies that the whole has been divided into four parts.","reader_rule":"Cancelling a common factor changes the written denominator without changing the fraction's value.","reader_deep":"Number-theory arguments often require the denominator in lowest terms. For example, whether a fraction terminates in a chosen numeral base depends on the prime factors of that reduced denominator."},{"object_id":"term:denominator_clearing","preferred_label":"clearing denominators","reader_preview":"Clearing denominators multiplies an equation or expression by a suitable nonzero common denominator to remove its fractions.","reader_card":"For example, multiplying x/2 + y/3 = 1 by 6 gives 3x + 2y = 6. Every term must receive the same multiplier for the equation to remain equivalent.","reader_rule":"If a denominator contains a variable, its nonzero condition must be preserved.","reader_deep":"The papers use this operation to obtain integer equations from rational quantities. Divisibility and congruence arguments can then study the resulting integers without losing the original denominator assumptions."},{"object_id":"term:derivation","preferred_label":"derivation","reader_preview":"A derivation is a sequence of justified steps leading from stated starting points to a result.","reader_card":"In mathematics, the steps use definitions, assumptions, and valid inference rules. In the software descriptions, a derivation can instead describe how a generated object was obtained from its sources.","reader_rule":"The meaning of \u201cjustified\u201d depends on the setting and the rules being used.","reader_deep":"Keeping the starting material and steps inspectable helps locate an error or repeat the process. A final answer alone may hide the assumption or transformation on which it depends."},{"object_id":"term:derivation_certificate","preferred_label":"derivation certificate","reader_preview":"A derivation certificate is an inspectable record that lets a checker verify how a particular claim follows from its stated basis.","reader_card":"Its form depends on the claim: it might be a formal proof object, a calculation trace, or structured data checked against source inputs. The checker must implement the connection between that record and the claimed result.","reader_rule":"Simply recording that a process ran does not certify every conclusion drawn from it.","reader_deep":"Plectis separates the producer, supporting record, and acceptance check so the basis for accepting a result can itself be examined."},{"object_id":"term:derivative","preferred_label":"derivative","reader_preview":"A derivative is the linear map that approximates a function's change to first order near a point.","reader_card":"For a function of one real or complex variable, the derivative at x is f\u2032(x) = lim_{h\u21920}(f(x+h)\u2212f(x))/h when this limit exists. For f(x) = x\u00b2, expanding the quotient gives f\u2032(x) = 2x. In several variables, the derivative acts on a direction of movement to give the corresponding first-order change.","reader_rule":"The approximation error must become negligible compared with the size of the input change. For complex functions, the difference quotient must approach the same limit from every complex direction.","reader_deep":"Polynomial critical points satisfy f'(c)=0, and the Newton vector field uses the quotient -f/f'."},{"object_id":"term:diebold_mariano_test","preferred_label":"Diebold-Mariano test","reader_preview":"The Diebold\u2013Mariano test assesses whether two forecasting methods have equal expected loss under a chosen measure of error.","reader_card":"It compares the loss differences between paired forecasts and accounts for time dependence through an estimate of their long-run variance. Squared error and absolute error can lead to different comparisons.","reader_rule":"Interpret the result under the test's sampling and dependence assumptions.","reader_deep":"The test addresses average predictive loss in the analysed setting. Statistical significance neither identifies the cause of a difference nor guarantees that the better-performing method will remain better on future data."},{"object_id":"term:digest","preferred_label":"digest","reader_preview":"A cryptographic digest is a fixed-size value computed from data, used to detect whether the data has changed.","reader_card":"The same bytes produce the same digest under the same algorithm. Plectis uses hashes to connect generated material and result records to particular file contents.","reader_rule":"A mismatch proves the contents differ; a matching secure hash provides strong practical evidence of equality.","reader_deep":"Different inputs can theoretically share a digest, so it is not a mathematical guarantee of identical bytes. A digest also says nothing about the truth or quality of the contents."},{"object_id":"term:digestion","preferred_label":"digestion","reader_preview":"Mathematical digestion is the process of understanding a result well enough to place it in the field and use it in further work.","reader_card":"It includes identifying the main idea, checking how the hypotheses matter, connecting the result to earlier work, and finding useful applications or reformulations. The site's discussion follows the distinction between verifying a proof and making its mathematics usable.","reader_rule":"A checked proof and a published paper can support this process without completing it.","reader_deep":"Clear explanations, failed approaches, and precise open questions help other mathematicians assess and continue the work. Community uptake cannot be awarded by a repository's own checks."},{"object_id":"term:discrete_mathematics","preferred_label":"discrete mathematics","reader_preview":"Discrete mathematics studies objects with separate elements, such as integers, graphs, and finite arrangements.","reader_card":"Its questions include counting configurations, finding paths in networks, and proving properties of integer sequences. The objects may be finite or infinite; \u201cdiscrete\u201d does not mean \u201cfinite\u201d.","reader_rule":"The relevant structure is determined by the definitions of the problem.","reader_deep":"Number theory and combinatorics supply many of the discrete methods used in these papers. Arguments may still involve continuous tools, such as real limits or analytic estimates, to study discrete objects."},{"object_id":"term:divergence","preferred_label":"divergence","reader_preview":"A sequence or series diverges when it does not converge in the sense being considered.","reader_card":"For real sequences, failure to approach a finite limit can mean growing without bound or oscillating indefinitely. A series diverges when its sequence of partial sums fails to converge.","reader_rule":"Individual terms tending to zero do not ensure that their sum converges.","reader_deep":"The harmonic series is a standard example: its terms shrink to zero while its partial sums grow without bound. Papers should specify whether \u201cdiverges\u201d means unbounded growth or another failure of convergence."},{"object_id":"term:divisibility","preferred_label":"divisibility","reader_preview":"Divisibility is the relation in which one integer is an exact integer multiple of another.","reader_card":"The notation a \u2223 b means that b = ak for some integer k. For example, 3 divides 12 because 12 = 3\u00b74, while 5 does not divide 12.","reader_rule":"The direction matters: a \u2223 b does not generally imply b \u2223 a.","reader_deep":"With this algebraic definition, every integer divides zero and zero divides only zero. The papers often impose positivity to exclude such cases, then use divisibility to track denominator cancellation, congruences, and prime factors."},{"object_id":"term:divisor","preferred_label":"divisor","reader_preview":"An integer d divides an integer n if n = d\u00b7k for some integer k.","reader_card":"For example, 3 divides 12 because 12 = 3\u00b74. Number-theory statements usually specify positive divisors, while the divisibility relation itself can also involve negative integers or zero.","reader_rule":"The notation d \u2223 n means \u201cd divides n\u201d.","reader_deep":"A divisor is an exact factor, so there is no remainder. With the stated algebraic definition, every integer divides zero, and zero divides only zero; additional positivity hypotheses often exclude these edge cases."},{"object_id":"term:doctrine","preferred_label":"doctrine","reader_preview":"In Plectis, doctrine is the organised set of ideas and rules explaining how the system is intended to work.","reader_card":"It includes principles, defined concepts, mechanisms, and guidance connected to source material. These records let agents recover the reasons behind an implementation rather than infer them from code alone.","reader_rule":"A doctrine statement expresses a rule or interpretation; implementation evidence shows whether it is enacted.","reader_deep":"Keeping the two connected makes disagreements visible. A rule can be clear while its software support is incomplete, or code can change while the explanation remains outdated."},{"object_id":"term:doi","preferred_label":"DOI","reader_preview":"A DOI is a persistent identifier for an object such as a paper, dataset, or software release.","reader_card":"It resolves through the DOI system to information maintained by the registering organisation. That destination can be updated when the object's web location changes, allowing the identifier to remain stable.","reader_rule":"A DOI identifies material; it does not certify correctness, peer review, or permanence of access.","reader_deep":"When citing work, use the DOI for the intended object or version. Different versions and related artifacts may have separate identifiers or explicit relationships in their metadata."},{"object_id":"term:domain_expert","preferred_label":"domain expert","reader_preview":"A domain expert is someone with substantial knowledge and judgement in a particular field.","reader_card":"For this project, relevant expertise may concern number theory, proof assistants, statistics, software architecture, or another subject addressed by a component. The useful expertise depends on the claim being examined.","reader_rule":"Expertise in one area does not automatically settle questions in another.","reader_deep":"An expert can help by assessing significance, detecting a missing assumption, relating a result to prior work, or explaining what would make the work easier for the field to use."},{"object_id":"term:drift","preferred_label":"drift","reader_preview":"Drift is a mismatch that develops between things expected to agree, such as a generated page and its source.","reader_card":"A source can change while a copied count, summary, or diagram continues to describe an older state. Plectis uses comparisons and refresh records to detect this kind of disagreement.","reader_rule":"Identify the two objects, their versions, and the relationship being checked.","reader_deep":"A drift report locates a mismatch; deciding which side needs correction requires knowing the authoritative source. Regenerating a page helps only if its builder is using the right inputs."},{"object_id":"term:drilldown","preferred_label":"drilldown","reader_preview":"A drilldown opens a more detailed view of something shown in a summary.","reader_card":"For example, a component count can lead to the component list, then to one component's source and recorded checks. Each step narrows the question and exposes more evidence.","reader_rule":"The detailed view should preserve the identity of the item selected in the summary.","reader_deep":"Plectis uses these routes to make a large system readable in stages. A summary remains useful when readers can recover the detail that was deliberately left out."},{"object_id":"term:dyadic","preferred_label":"dyadic","reader_preview":"Dyadic means related to powers of two, often fractions or intervals built from them.","reader_card":"A dyadic rational has the form m/2\u207f for integers m and nonnegative n. Examples include 1/2, 3/8, and 5; each has a terminating binary expansion.","reader_rule":"The exact object follows the noun: a dyadic interval or partition is not itself a number.","reader_deep":"Dyadic scales are useful when repeatedly halving a range or relating binary digits to arithmetic. They provide a common structure for estimates, subdivisions, and expansions in base two."},{"object_id":"term:endogenous","preferred_label":"endogenous","reader_preview":"Endogenous means originating within the system or process being discussed.","reader_card":"An internal score, feedback signal, or judgement may be endogenous if the process being assessed also produces it. The term always depends on where the system boundary is drawn.","reader_rule":"An internally produced measure can still be informative, but its dependencies matter.","reader_deep":"In the site's research discussion, the concern is self-confirmation: repeated agreement among connected internal checks may provide less independent support than the number of agreeing outputs suggests."},{"object_id":"term:engine_room","preferred_label":"engine room","reader_preview":"\u201cEngine room\u201d is the site's informal label for components that operate and maintain the system itself.","reader_card":"These include routing, work queues, gates, and checks for mismatches between source and generated state. They support the workflow in which other components or mathematical work are developed.","reader_rule":"The label groups a function within the architecture rather than naming a separate product.","reader_deep":"An individual component in this group should still have a specific job, source, and evidence. The broad label alone does not explain what any particular mechanism has been shown to do."},{"object_id":"term:entry","preferred_label":"entry","reader_preview":"In the navigation descriptions, an entry is either a starting route or an individual item in a collection.","reader_card":"A command described as an entry route helps a reader choose the next relevant action. A glossary or catalog entry instead refers to one stored item with its own identity and detail.","reader_rule":"The surrounding noun distinguishes these two common uses.","reader_deep":"Plectis tries to make entry routes short enough for first contact while preserving links to the sources needed for the task. An entry should orient the reader before asking them to load deeper context."},{"object_id":"term:epistemic_control_plane","preferred_label":"epistemic control plane","reader_preview":"The epistemic control plane is a proposed system layer that uses evidence and uncertainty to govern which actions may proceed.","reader_card":"\u201cEpistemic\u201d concerns what is known and how it is justified. The design connects a claim's evidence status to permitted actions, review requirements, and conditions that should stop a change.","reader_rule":"The phrase names a design target associated with the Workingness Instrument; the relevant implementation evidence must be checked separately.","reader_deep":"Its central idea is that changed evidence should change decisions. Recording uncertainty is useful only when the actions that depend on it respond appropriately."},{"object_id":"term:equality","preferred_label":"equality","reader_preview":"An equality states that two expressions represent the same value or mathematical object.","reader_card":"For example, 1/2 = 2/4 compares different expressions for the same number. Equality depends on the setting: two functions are equal when they agree as functions, not merely at a few tested inputs.","reader_rule":"Distinguish equality from an approximation or a one-way implication.","reader_deep":"In Lean, equality is itself a proposition that can require a proof. Two expressions that simplify to the same form may be equal for a more direct reason than expressions requiring a substantial theorem."},{"object_id":"term:equation","preferred_label":"equation","reader_preview":"An equation is a mathematical statement that two expressions are equal.","reader_card":"It can specify a relation, define a quantity, or ask which values make the equality true. For example, x\u00b2 = 4 has solutions x = 2 and x = \u22122 over the real numbers.","reader_rule":"The permitted domain and assumptions determine what counts as a solution.","reader_deep":"Some equations are identities that hold for every allowed value; others hold only for particular values. A displayed equality in a proof may express a justified transformation rather than a problem to solve."},{"object_id":"term:equivalence","preferred_label":"equivalence","reader_preview":"Two statements are logically equivalent when each implies the other under the same assumptions.","reader_card":"To establish P \u2194 Q, one proves both P \u2192 Q and Q \u2192 P. Either statement can then replace the other within that setting, although one may be easier to understand or use.","reader_rule":"A single implication gives a reduction in one direction, not an equivalence.","reader_deep":"The papers use equivalences to compare reformulations of open problems. An equivalent reformulation preserves the unresolved question; proving the equivalence alone does not establish either side unconditionally."},{"object_id":"term:erdos","preferred_label":"Paul Erd\u0151s","reader_preview":"Paul Erd\u0151s was a mathematician known for work in number theory, combinatorics, probability, and related fields.","reader_card":"He posed and collected many problems, often through extensive collaboration. The site studies selected questions identified by their numbers in the independently maintained Erd\u0151s Problems database.","reader_rule":"A database number identifies the question; it does not describe its current solution status.","reader_deep":"Read the linked problem statement and references for the intended formulation. A paper may establish a related theorem, special case, or obstruction while the numbered question remains open."},{"object_id":"term:erdos_problem","preferred_label":"Erd\u0151s problem","reader_preview":"An Erd\u0151s problem is a mathematical question associated with Paul Erd\u0151s; this site uses the numbering of the Erd\u0151s Problems database.","reader_card":"For example, \u201cErd\u0151s #249\u201d identifies a particular database entry rather than the 249th theorem in this repository. The entry supplies the question and references needed to understand its origin and context.","reader_rule":"Compare a paper's exact statement with the original question before interpreting a result as a solution.","reader_deep":"An equivalent reformulation, a finite computation, and an unconditional proof are different forms of progress and should retain their own descriptions."},{"object_id":"term:eulers_theorem_modular","preferred_label":"Euler's theorem (modular arithmetic)","reader_preview":"Euler's theorem says that if a is coprime to a positive integer n, then a raised to \u03c6(n) is congruent to 1 modulo n.","reader_card":"The totient \u03c6(n) counts integers from 1 through n that are coprime to n. For example, \u03c6(10) = 4 and 3\u2074 = 81 leaves remainder 1 modulo 10.","reader_rule":"The coprimality hypothesis is essential.","reader_deep":"The exponent \u03c6(n) gives a period but need not be the smallest one. The papers use such exponents to arrange modular repetition while separately proving any claim about the exact multiplicative order."},{"object_id":"term:evaluator","preferred_label":"evaluator","reader_preview":"An evaluator is a tool or procedure that assesses something against chosen criteria.","reader_card":"In Plectis's component model, an evaluator observes inputs and reports its assessment without changing the material being assessed. A separate action is responsible for any repair or update.","reader_rule":"The criteria determine what its result can establish.","reader_deep":"For example, an evaluator may detect that a generated page disagrees with source data. Its report identifies the mismatch; it does not by itself decide that the page, source, or generation rule should be changed."},{"object_id":"term:eventual_property","preferred_label":"eventual property","reader_preview":"An eventual property holds at every index after some finite threshold.","reader_card":"For a sequence indexed by natural numbers, \u201ceventually P(n)\u201d means that there is an N such that P(n) holds for all n \u2265 N. Any failures must occur before that threshold.","reader_rule":"This is stronger than holding infinitely often or at arbitrarily large indices.","reader_deep":"For example, being positive holds eventually for n \u2212 10, whereas being even never holds eventually for all integers n. The papers use this distinction when comparing recurring obstructions with consequences of rationality."},{"object_id":"term:evidence","preferred_label":"evidence","reader_preview":"Evidence is information that supports or challenges a particular claim.","reader_card":"It may come from a source, observation, proof, calculation, or recorded test. Plectis links claims to their evidence so readers can examine the basis and decide whether it supports the wording used.","reader_rule":"Evidence should be interpreted within the conditions under which it was obtained.","reader_deep":"A test result, for instance, concerns the version, inputs, and environment tested. A proof has a different scope, determined by its precise statement and assumptions."},{"object_id":"term:evidence_band","preferred_label":"evidence band","reader_preview":"An evidence band labels the kind of support attached to a statement in a mathematical working record.","reader_card":"The records use labels such as Lean for formal checking, Cert for a checked certificate, Math for mathematical argument, Cited for a referenced result, and Open for an unresolved obligation.","reader_rule":"These labels describe different sources of support, not interchangeable levels of approval.","reader_deep":"A separate scope description indicates whether the statement covers listed cases, a bounded range, arbitrarily large parameters, or all allowed parameters. Both the evidence and its reach matter."},{"object_id":"term:evidence_class","preferred_label":"evidence class","reader_preview":"An evidence class identifies how a claim is supported, such as by a proof, test run, calculation, or citation.","reader_card":"The classification helps readers distinguish what has actually been checked from what has been inferred or proposed. Each class comes with its own assumptions and limits.","reader_rule":"The class must match the evidence attached to the particular claim.","reader_deep":"For example, exact arithmetic avoids rounding error in a calculation but does not automatically cover infinitely many cases. A formal proof can cover all cases in its statement while still depending on explicit hypotheses."},{"object_id":"term:exact_rational_arithmetic","preferred_label":"exact rational arithmetic","reader_preview":"Exact rational arithmetic calculates with integer numerators and denominators instead of rounded decimal values.","reader_card":"For example, 1/3 + 1/6 is stored and simplified to 1/2 using integer operations. Provided the implementation supports the required integer sizes, these operations introduce no rounding error.","reader_rule":"Exact calculation still depends on correct inputs, algorithms, and interpretation.","reader_deep":"The papers use it when a tiny difference or precise equality matters. It settles the computed rational expression, while an argument about an infinite limit may also need a proved bound on the omitted terms."},{"object_id":"term:executable_cross_section","preferred_label":"executable cross-section","reader_preview":"An executable cross-section is a selected part of a larger system that can be run and inspected on its own.","reader_card":"Plectis publishes components with the source, inputs, checks, and records needed to examine their behaviour. The selection aims to make a working mechanism available without requiring the entire private environment.","reader_rule":"Its scope is the included material and the dependencies declared for it.","reader_deep":"A successful run provides evidence about that public selection. Claims about the larger system need their own support, especially where unpublished components or interactions are involved."},{"object_id":"term:exogenous","preferred_label":"exogenous","reader_preview":"Exogenous means originating outside the system or process under discussion.","reader_card":"An external observation, event, or judgement can be exogenous relative to the process being studied. The classification depends on the chosen boundary: a signal can be external to one component but internal to the organisation containing it.","reader_rule":"Outside origin does not by itself guarantee accuracy or independence.","reader_deep":"Plectis uses the term when discussing evidence and criticism that do not arise solely from the workflow producing the work. The source's expertise and dependencies still need assessment."},{"object_id":"term:exogenous_truth","preferred_label":"exogenous truth","reader_preview":"\u201cExogenous truth\u201d is this project's phrase for judgement brought in from outside the process being judged.","reader_card":"It refers to an independent reader examining the public evidence and forming their own assessment, including disagreement with the project's account. The phrase stresses an external source of criticism or correction.","reader_rule":"It does not mean that an outsider's judgement is automatically true.","reader_deep":"The value lies in different evidence, expertise, or assumptions entering the discussion. A claim still needs to be assessed on its merits, whether it comes from inside or outside the project."},{"object_id":"term:exponent","preferred_label":"exponent","reader_preview":"An exponent specifies a power: in b\u207f, b is the base and n is the exponent.","reader_card":"For a positive integer n, b\u207f means multiplying n copies of b. With nonzero b, b\u2070 = 1 and b\u207b\u207f = 1/b\u207f. Fractional and real exponents require further definitions and domain conditions.","reader_rule":"In digit expansions and arithmetic series, check whether the exponent is restricted to integers.","reader_deep":"The expression 2\u207f \u2212 1 subtracts one after taking the power. It differs from 2\u207f\u207b\u00b9, which lowers the exponent before calculating the power."},{"object_id":"term:exposition","preferred_label":"exposition","reader_preview":"Mathematical exposition explains a result, its proof, and why the argument works for its intended readers.","reader_card":"Good exposition states the assumptions, introduces notation, identifies the main idea, and distinguishes new work from earlier results. It makes a proof easier to assess and use without weakening its precise claim.","reader_rule":"Clear writing supports verification but cannot replace missing mathematical reasoning.","reader_deep":"The site treats exposition as a separate task from obtaining a checked proof. A formal declaration may be valid while still needing an explanation that lets another mathematician understand or continue the work."},{"object_id":"term:factor","preferred_label":"factor","reader_preview":"A factor is one of the quantities multiplied together to make a product.","reader_card":"In 12 = 3\u00b74, both 3 and 4 are factors of 12. For integers, saying that d is a factor of n usually means that d divides n exactly.","reader_rule":"Factorisation expresses an object as a product of its factors.","reader_deep":"In algebra, factors may be expressions: x\u00b2 \u2212 1 = (x \u2212 1)(x + 1). The setting determines which factors count as irreducible and what uniqueness of factorisation means. Factoring can expose roots or divisibility conditions that are hidden in the expanded expression."},{"object_id":"term:factorial","preferred_label":"factorial","reader_preview":"The factorial of a nonnegative integer n is the product of the integers from 1 through n, with the value at zero defined as 1.","reader_card":"For example, 4! = 24. Factorials count arrangements of n distinct objects and also provide rapidly growing, highly divisible denominators in number-theory problems.","reader_rule":"The exclamation mark here is mathematical notation, not punctuation indicating emphasis.","reader_deep":"Expressions such as n! \u2212 1 and (n \u2212 1)! have very different values and divisibility properties. The paper's parentheses and order of operations are therefore part of the statement being studied."},{"object_id":"term:factorisation","preferred_label":"factorisation","reader_preview":"Factorisation writes a number or algebraic expression as a product of simpler factors.","reader_card":"For example, 60 = 2\u00b2\u00b73\u00b75 is the prime factorisation of 60. Every integer greater than 1 has a unique prime factorisation, apart from the order in which its factors are written.","reader_rule":"Uniqueness depends on the mathematical setting when the objects are more general than integers.","reader_deep":"The papers use factorisation to study divisibility, common factors, and the denominators of sums. A convenient factorisation can reveal constraints that are hard to see from a decimal value."},{"object_id":"term:fail_closed","preferred_label":"fail-closed","reader_preview":"Fail-closed means withholding an action when a required condition cannot be established.","reader_card":"For example, a publication gate may stop when a required check is missing or its result cannot be read. Plectis uses this behaviour where proceeding without evidence would wrongly treat uncertainty as permission.","reader_rule":"The required conditions and the action being withheld must be explicit.","reader_deep":"Failing closed can prevent an unsupported action, but it does not prove the underlying artifact is faulty. The next step may be to obtain the missing evidence or repair the check."},{"object_id":"term:failure_class_propagation","preferred_label":"failure class propagation","reader_preview":"Failure-class propagation checks whether a discovered fault also affects other cases for the same reason.","reader_card":"Plectis starts with the local failure, identifies its cause, and inspects related surfaces that share the relevant mechanism. Confirmed instances can then be repaired together through the common source or rule.","reader_rule":"Similarity alone is not enough to assume that another case has the same fault.","reader_deep":"The aim is to fix a recurring cause without expanding the repair to unrelated work. A useful record states which cases were examined and where the explanation stops applying."},{"object_id":"term:failure_modes","preferred_label":"failure modes","reader_preview":"Failure modes are the distinct ways something can fail to meet its intended purpose.","reader_card":"A check might accept bad input, reject good input, use stale data, or fail to run. These are different failure modes because their causes, evidence, and repairs can differ.","reader_rule":"Name the mechanism of failure, not just the fact that a result is wrong.","reader_deep":"Plectis uses negative cases and recorded incidents to make such failures inspectable. Covering known modes improves a check, while new situations may still reveal modes that were not anticipated."},{"object_id":"term:farey_sequence","preferred_label":"Farey sequence","reader_preview":"The Farey sequence of order N lists all reduced fractions between 0 and 1 whose positive denominators are at most N.","reader_card":"The fractions appear in increasing order, including 0/1 and 1/1. For example, order 3 gives 0/1, 1/3, 1/2, 2/3, 1/1.","reader_rule":"\u201cReduced\u201d means that numerator and denominator have no common factor greater than 1.","reader_deep":"The denominator bound makes the sequence finite. Its neighbouring fractions have useful arithmetic relationships, making Farey sequences a way to organise questions about rational approximation and the gaps between fractions."},{"object_id":"term:feedback_loop","preferred_label":"feedback loop","reader_preview":"A feedback loop occurs when a process's output influences its later behaviour.","reader_card":"Feedback can reinforce a change, counteract it, or create more complicated dynamics. In software, test results can guide revisions; in research, an interpretation can shape which evidence is sought next.","reader_rule":"Feedback is not inherently beneficial or self-confirming; its effect depends on what is measured and how it is used.","reader_deep":"Plectis records evidence and invites external criticism to help distinguish correction from a loop that merely repeats the assumptions of its own earlier outputs."},{"object_id":"term:finite","preferred_label":"finite","reader_preview":"A set is finite if its elements can be counted by a nonnegative integer.","reader_card":"In related uses, a finite calculation or sequence has only finitely many steps or terms. The count can be extremely large; finite does not mean practically small or easy to inspect.","reader_rule":"Distinguish one large finite case from a statement about every possible size.","reader_deep":"The papers may verify many finite instances exactly. Extending that result to all instances requires a general argument, unless a theorem has already reduced the problem to those particular cases."},{"object_id":"term:finite_computation","preferred_label":"finite computation","reader_preview":"A finite computation is a calculation that completes after finitely many steps.","reader_card":"It can examine one input or an explicitly bounded collection of cases, producing an exact result or an approximation with stated error. The algorithm and arithmetic determine which kind of result it gives.","reader_rule":"The number of cases checked does not by itself extend the result beyond those cases.","reader_deep":"A finite computation can support an infinite theorem when combined with a proof of coverage or a suitable error bound. The connection must be stated rather than inferred from the amount of computation."},{"object_id":"term:finite_truncation","preferred_label":"finite truncation","reader_preview":"A finite truncation keeps a specified finite part of an otherwise longer or infinite object.","reader_card":"For a series, truncating after N terms gives its Nth partial sum and leaves a tail. For a power series, it produces a polynomial retaining a chosen range of powers.","reader_rule":"A finite expression can be calculated exactly while still differing from the full object.","reader_deep":"The papers connect calculations to infinite claims through bounds or identities controlling what was omitted. The truncation point and error estimate determine whether the finite calculation is adequate for the stated conclusion."},{"object_id":"term:fixture","preferred_label":"fixture","reader_preview":"A test fixture supplies the inputs, state, or environment used for a test.","reader_card":"In Plectis's public examples, fixtures are often small stored datasets or files chosen to exercise particular behaviours. A negative fixture deliberately represents a case the mechanism should reject or report.","reader_rule":"The fixture's role is to make the tested conditions explicit and repeatable.","reader_deep":"Using the same fixture helps comparison, but repeatability also depends on the program and environment. A fixture can test one failure mode well without representing the full range of real-world inputs."},{"object_id":"term:formal","preferred_label":"formal","reader_preview":"Formal means expressed within explicitly specified rules; in mathematics, it usually concerns a precise logical language and inference system.","reader_card":"A formal statement identifies its objects and conditions in that language. A formal proof follows the system's rules to derive a conclusion from assumptions.","reader_rule":"Precision of form does not ensure that the statement expresses the intended question.","reader_deep":"The papers connect formal statements with ordinary mathematical explanations so readers can examine both the validity of the derivation and whether the encoded result matches the claim being made."},{"object_id":"term:formal_mathematics","preferred_label":"formal mathematics","reader_preview":"Formal mathematics expresses definitions, statements, and proofs in a language governed by explicit logical rules.","reader_card":"Proof assistants such as Lean can check the resulting derivations mechanically. Authors may use tactics and automation to construct proofs, while the accepted proof must satisfy the system's checking rules.","reader_rule":"The exact statement and assumptions determine what has been established.","reader_deep":"Formal checking reduces dependence on unchecked inference steps. It leaves separate work in choosing the right formulation, explaining the ideas, assessing significance, and ensuring that the formal statement corresponds to the intended mathematics."},{"object_id":"term:formal_proof","preferred_label":"formal proof","reader_preview":"A formal proof is a derivation expressed precisely enough to be checked against a formal system's inference rules.","reader_card":"In Lean, a proof establishes a proposition using definitions, earlier results, hypotheses, and any permitted axioms. Automation can construct the proof, but the final object is checked against the claimed type.","reader_rule":"A theorem with unresolved hypotheses proves an implication; a theorem using an admission contains an additional proof gap.","reader_deep":"The check establishes the encoded statement within its logical setting. Readers still need to compare that statement with the informal result described in the paper."},{"object_id":"term:formal_verification","preferred_label":"formal verification","reader_preview":"Formal verification uses mathematical methods to establish that a system or proof satisfies a precise specification.","reader_card":"It can concern software behaviour, hardware, protocols, or mathematical arguments. In the Lean work here, the central check is whether a supplied proof establishes the encoded theorem under its stated assumptions.","reader_rule":"The specification and trusted checking machinery are part of the guarantee.","reader_deep":"A formally verified result may still address the wrong specification or an incomplete model of the intended problem. Explaining and reviewing that correspondence remains necessary alongside the mechanical check."},{"object_id":"term:formalisation","preferred_label":"formalisation","reader_preview":"Formalisation translates mathematical definitions, statements, and arguments into a precise formal language.","reader_card":"The work includes choosing representations, making implicit assumptions explicit, and filling in steps required by the proof assistant. The resulting objects can then be checked by the formal system.","reader_rule":"A faithful formalisation must preserve the meaning and scope of the original mathematics.","reader_deep":"Sometimes the process exposes a missing hypothesis or an ambiguous argument. It can also prove useful supporting results, which should be distinguished from the main theorem when describing what the project contributes."},{"object_id":"term:fraction","preferred_label":"fraction","reader_preview":"A fraction writes a quotient as a/b; for a rational number, a and b are integers and b is nonzero.","reader_card":"The numerator a is divided by the denominator b. Different fractions can represent the same number: 2/4 and 1/2 are equal because the common factor 2 can be cancelled.","reader_rule":"\u201cIn lowest terms\u201d means that numerator and denominator are coprime.","reader_deep":"An expression written as a quotient is not necessarily a rational number if its numerator or denominator is non-integer. For example, \u221a2/1 is still irrational."},{"object_id":"term:freshness","preferred_label":"freshness","reader_preview":"Freshness describes whether stored information still reflects the source or state it is meant to represent.","reader_card":"A generated page, cached result, or copied file may have been accurate when created and become outdated after its source changes. Plectis uses source versions and refresh rules to assess that relationship.","reader_rule":"A recent timestamp alone does not show that the right source was read.","reader_deep":"A useful freshness record identifies the source, the version examined, and the condition that requires another check. Different kinds of information can have very different refresh needs."},{"object_id":"term:front_end","preferred_label":"frontend","reader_preview":"A front end is the part of a software system through which a user interacts with it.","reader_card":"It may present screens, controls, and views over data supplied by a back end. In the site's \u201cWhy\u201d walkthrough, the term refers to the larger system's operator interface shown in the videos.","reader_rule":"That demonstrated interface has a different scope from the public website you are reading.","reader_deep":"Both can present views of related source objects. Their appearance alone does not establish which actions or private data are available through each interface."},{"object_id":"term:frontier","preferred_label":"frontier","reader_preview":"Here, the mathematical frontier is the stated boundary between established results and unresolved questions for a problem.","reader_card":"A useful account identifies the strongest relevant results, the assumptions still needed, and the approaches that have been ruled out. It helps another researcher choose a next question without reconstructing all earlier work.","reader_rule":"A convenient intermediate target is not automatically equivalent to the original problem.","reader_deep":"Moving the frontier requires a meaningful mathematical change, such as removing a hypothesis or proving a new obstruction. More declarations or a new presentation alone do not show that change."},{"object_id":"term:function","preferred_label":"function","reader_preview":"A function assigns exactly one output to each input in its domain.","reader_card":"It comes with a domain of allowed inputs and a codomain in which outputs lie. For example, f(n) = n\u00b2 defines a function from the integers to the integers.","reader_rule":"Different inputs may have the same output; one input cannot have two outputs under the same function.","reader_deep":"In the papers, domain restrictions are part of the definition. A formula involving division, logarithms, or roots may define different functions depending on which inputs are permitted."},{"object_id":"term:gate","preferred_label":"gate","reader_preview":"A gate is a checkpoint that permits an action only when specified conditions are satisfied.","reader_card":"Plectis uses gates before changes or publication that require evidence, such as a successful check or a matching source version. The gate records which requirement determined its decision.","reader_rule":"A gate's pass means that its particular conditions were met.","reader_deep":"It cannot establish requirements that it never examines. A blocked gate can indicate missing evidence, a failing check, or an unavailable dependency, and those cases may require different next steps."},{"object_id":"term:generalise_up_propagate","preferred_label":"generalise-up-propagate","reader_preview":"Generalise-up-propagate means turning a supported local lesson into guidance that future relevant work will actually encounter.","reader_card":"Plectis first identifies what the case teaches and how broadly the lesson applies. It then places the refinement in an existing rule, skill, standard, or other source used by the affected workflow.","reader_rule":"A single example should not become a universal rule without an argument for that scope.","reader_deep":"The change should preserve the source case and its limits. Updating the shared owner can prevent repeated mistakes more effectively than adding another isolated note."},{"object_id":"term:geometric_majorant","preferred_label":"geometric majorant","reader_preview":"A geometric majorant bounds terms or their magnitudes by a geometric sequence with a fixed ratio.","reader_card":"If |a_N\u208a\u2096| \u2264 Cr\u1d4f for k \u2265 0 and 0 \u2264 r < 1, the entire tail's absolute sum is at most C/(1 \u2212 r). This turns infinitely many bounds into a usable finite estimate.","reader_rule":"The same ratio and initial bound must work throughout the claimed tail.","reader_deep":"Rapidly growing factorial denominators can supply such comparisons. The starting index matters because a ratio bound may fail for early terms even when it holds for all later ones."},{"object_id":"term:geometric_series","preferred_label":"geometric series","reader_preview":"A geometric series adds terms formed by repeatedly multiplying an initial value by a fixed number.","reader_card":"Its terms are a, ar, ar\u00b2, and so on, where a is the initial value and r is the common multiplier. When |r| < 1, the infinite sum is a/(1\u2212r); for example, 1 + 1/2 + 1/4 + \u2026 = 2.","reader_rule":"For a nonzero initial value, the infinite sum converges exactly when |r| < 1. With initial value zero, every term and the sum are zero.","reader_deep":"Geometric series and their differentiated forms give explicit bounds for analytic tails and their first and second derivatives in the lemniscate paper."},{"object_id":"term:git","preferred_label":"Git","reader_preview":"Git is a version-control system that records snapshots of files and the history connecting them.","reader_card":"A commit identifies a stored snapshot, its parent history, and a message. Branches name lines of development, and differences between commits show how tracked content changed.","reader_rule":"Uncommitted edits are part of a working copy, not yet part of a recorded commit.","reader_deep":"The repositories use Git revisions to identify the source behind a paper, check, or generated page. Sharing a revision lets another person obtain the same tracked content rather than an unspecified current copy."},{"object_id":"term:github","preferred_label":"GitHub","reader_preview":"GitHub is a service for hosting Git repositories and tools for collaboration around them.","reader_card":"It provides interfaces for browsing files and history, discussing issues, reviewing changes, and publishing releases. GitHub Pages can serve static websites from a repository's published content.","reader_rule":"Git is the version-control system; GitHub is one service that hosts repositories using it.","reader_deep":"The links on this site lead to public source and related artifacts. A hosted file's presence establishes availability, while its correctness and evidence status must be assessed from the work itself."},{"object_id":"term:govern","preferred_label":"govern","reader_preview":"To govern a process is to define how its decisions are made, authorised, and checked.","reader_card":"In Plectis, this includes assigning source authority, setting rules for changes, and specifying evidence required before an action proceeds. The relevant rule should be discoverable where the action is performed.","reader_rule":"A written rule and an enforced rule are different states of implementation.","reader_deep":"To assess a governed process, inspect both its stated contract and the mechanism applying it. Recorded decisions can then show whether the rule had the intended effect in a particular case."},{"object_id":"term:greatest_common_divisor","preferred_label":"greatest common divisor","reader_preview":"For integers not all zero, the greatest common divisor is the largest positive integer dividing every one of them.","reader_card":"The common positive divisors of 12 and 18 are 1,2,3,6, so gcd(12,18)=6. Two integers are coprime exactly when their gcd is 1.","reader_rule":"Signs do not affect the nonnegative gcd. By convention gcd(0,0) = 0, and gcd(a,0) = |a|; these cases require care when describing the gcd as a largest positive divisor.","reader_deep":"The factorial paper uses gcds to measure cancellation, reduce denominators, and control how prime factors overlap across many terms."},{"object_id":"term:grounding","preferred_label":"grounding","reader_preview":"Grounding connects a statement or decision to the information that gives it meaning and support.","reader_card":"For a factual claim, that may be a source or observation. For a system term, it may be the code and records defining its local use. Plectis expects important explanations to retain those connections.","reader_rule":"The source must be relevant to the actual claim, not merely related to its subject.","reader_deep":"A source link improves inspectability but does not settle interpretation by itself. The reader still needs to check that the source supports the wording and scope."},{"object_id":"term:guinea_pig","preferred_label":"guinea pig","reader_preview":"\u201cGuinea pig\u201d is an informal term for a person or case on which an experimental method is tried.","reader_card":"The phrase draws attention to who bears the uncertainty, inconvenience, or consequences of the trial. In descriptions of this project, it concerns the experience of trying an unfinished workflow or method.","reader_rule":"An experimental use should be understood through its actual conditions and limits.","reader_deep":"The metaphor does not establish that a method works or that its effects generalise. A trial can reveal useful failures while leaving wider effectiveness and suitability unresolved."},{"object_id":"term:harness","preferred_label":"harness","reader_preview":"A test harness is the code and setup that runs a test and captures its result.","reader_card":"It supplies inputs, prepares the environment, invokes the thing being tested, and records or compares outputs. Plectis uses harnesses to make a component's advertised check executable.","reader_rule":"The harness is separate from both the component and the claim being tested.","reader_deep":"Its own behaviour matters: a harness that runs the wrong command or ignores a failure can produce a misleading pass. Negative cases help check that relevant failures are actually detected."},{"object_id":"term:hologram","preferred_label":"hologram","reader_preview":"In Plectis, \u201chologram\u201d means a structured, compressed view that preserves routes into the material it summarises.","reader_card":"Public JSON packets carry selected content, identities, and relationships so an assistant or reader can examine the collection without reconstructing the website page by page. Internal navigation uses the same idea at different levels of detail.","reader_rule":"The term is a project metaphor, not a claim about optical holography.","reader_deep":"The packet's declared coverage determines what it contains. Source links and preserved relationships make deeper inspection possible even when the packet deliberately omits large bodies of detail."},{"object_id":"term:html","preferred_label":"HTML","reader_preview":"HTML is the markup language used to describe the structure and meaning of web-page content.","reader_card":"Elements identify headings, paragraphs, links, lists, forms, and other parts that browsers and assistive technology can interpret. CSS controls presentation, while JavaScript can add behaviour.","reader_rule":"HTML structure affects how a page works as well as how it looks.","reader_deep":"The site's glossary references are links to definitions, enhanced with interactive previews. Keeping meaningful links in the page lets readers still reach an entry when the preview script is unavailable."},{"object_id":"term:hypothesis","preferred_label":"hypothesis","reader_preview":"A mathematical hypothesis is an assumption under which a statement is being considered or proved.","reader_card":"For example, \u201cif n is prime and greater than 2, then n is odd\u201d assumes both primality and the size condition. The conclusion is asserted for cases satisfying those assumptions.","reader_rule":"A hypothesis in a theorem need not be an unproved conjecture; it may simply define the cases covered.","reader_deep":"In conditional research results, a particular hypothesis may remain unresolved. The proof then establishes what follows from it, rather than establishing that it holds."},{"object_id":"term:ide","preferred_label":"IDE","reader_preview":"An IDE, or integrated development environment, is an application combining tools for working on software.","reader_card":"It commonly provides a code editor, project navigation, diagnostics, and ways to build or run programs. Support for a proof language can also show goals, errors, and declaration information while a proof is developed.","reader_rule":"An IDE is useful for local work but is not required to read the website.","reader_deep":"The repositories' command-line checks describe the reproducible operations. An editor interface can invoke or help inspect those operations without replacing their recorded inputs and results."},{"object_id":"term:implication","preferred_label":"implication","reader_preview":"An implication says that if one statement holds, another follows.","reader_card":"The notation P \u2192 Q means \u201cif P, then Q\u201d. P is a sufficient condition for Q, and Q is a necessary condition for P. This alone does not say that Q implies P.","reader_rule":"Proving the implication does not establish P itself.","reader_deep":"The papers use implications to connect hypotheses and target results. To claim an equivalent reformulation, the reverse implication must also be proved under compatible assumptions."},{"object_id":"term:inequality","preferred_label":"inequality","reader_preview":"An inequality compares quantities using an order relation such as <, \u2264, >, or \u2265.","reader_card":"The symbol \u2264 means \u201cless than or equal to\u201d, so x \u2264 M allows x to equal M. The strict inequality x < M excludes equality.","reader_rule":"The difference between strict and non-strict bounds can determine whether an endpoint is included.","reader_deep":"Inequalities in the papers often control an error or bound a sum. Each manipulation must respect sign conditions: multiplying both sides by a negative number reverses the order."},{"object_id":"term:infinite","preferred_label":"infinite","reader_preview":"An infinite set has more elements than any finite set; no finite count exhausts it.","reader_card":"Infinite sequences and series specify a term for every index in an unending index set. Statements about them are expressed through definitions, quantifiers, and limits rather than by carrying out infinitely many steps.","reader_rule":"\u201cInfinitely many\u201d is weaker than \u201call\u201d and can also be weaker than \u201call sufficiently large\u201d.","reader_deep":"For example, there are infinitely many even integers, but arbitrarily large odd integers remain. This distinction matters when interpreting the scope of a theorem."},{"object_id":"term:inspection","preferred_label":"inspection","reader_preview":"Inspection means examining the actual material closely enough to assess a specific question about it.","reader_card":"Here that can involve reading a theorem statement, tracing its proof dependencies, examining a test, or comparing a result record with the command that produced it. The relevant depth depends on the question.","reader_rule":"Reading a summary is useful orientation but may leave the decisive detail unexamined.","reader_deep":"Plectis provides source and evidence routes to make that deeper reading possible. Inspection can reveal uncertainty as well as confirm a claim; it need not end in approval."},{"object_id":"term:integer","preferred_label":"integer","reader_preview":"An integer is one of the numbers \u2026, \u22122, \u22121, 0, 1, 2, \u2026 .","reader_card":"Integers have no fractional part. They include positive and negative whole numbers and zero, and are usually denoted collectively by \u2124.","reader_rule":"Addition, subtraction, and multiplication preserve integers; division need not do so.","reader_deep":"Number-theory statements often restrict integers further, for example to positive or nonnegative values. Those restrictions matter for divisibility, factorials, and sequence indices, so they are part of the mathematical claim rather than optional wording."},{"object_id":"term:integral_curve","preferred_label":"integral curve","reader_preview":"An integral curve of a vector field is a parametrised curve whose velocity agrees with that field at every point.","reader_card":"It solves x\u2032(t) = V(x(t)). For the constant field V = 1 on the real line, the curves are x(t) = t + c, with the starting value determined by c.","reader_rule":"Existence and uniqueness require conditions on the field, such as local Lipschitz continuity for a standard uniqueness theorem.","reader_deep":"In the Newton-flow papers, the field is regular away from zeros of p\u2032. A trajectory approaching an excluded point needs separate analysis before further continuation can be asserted."},{"object_id":"term:intelligent","preferred_label":"intelligent","reader_preview":"Intelligent ordinarily describes an ability to learn, reason, understand, or adapt to a task.","reader_card":"The word covers several capacities rather than one universally agreed measure. Performance can vary with the problem, available context, tools, and time.","reader_rule":"Plectis's Type A and Type B labels describe access and authority, not intelligence.","reader_deep":"An actor may reason well while lacking access to the evidence needed for a particular claim. Conversely, permission to run commands gives an opportunity to gather evidence without guaranteeing good judgement about the results."},{"object_id":"term:invariant","preferred_label":"invariant","reader_preview":"An invariant is a quantity or property preserved by specified transformations or steps of a process.","reader_card":"For example, repeatedly adding 2 preserves an integer's parity. The unchanged property can connect distant stages without examining every intermediate operation separately.","reader_rule":"An invariant is preserved exactly in the stated sense; a quantity merely increasing or decreasing is instead monotone.","reader_deep":"The papers use preserved arithmetic relationships to constrain possible trajectories of a recurrence. Which transformation is allowed matters: a property invariant under one update need not survive a different operation or change of coordinates."},{"object_id":"term:irrational","preferred_label":"irrational","reader_preview":"A real number is irrational if it cannot equal a/b for any integers a and nonzero b.","reader_card":"For example, \u221a2 is irrational. In any fixed integer base greater than one, an irrational number's digit expansion never becomes permanently periodic.","reader_rule":"An infinite expansion alone does not imply irrationality: 1/3 has an infinite repeating decimal expansion.","reader_deep":"An irrationality proof must rule out every possible rational representation. Computing many digits or checking many candidate denominators can provide evidence, but it does not on its own exclude all fractions."},{"object_id":"term:irrationality_criterion","preferred_label":"irrationality criterion","reader_preview":"An irrationality criterion is a proved condition that can be used to establish that a number is irrational.","reader_card":"It may require a pattern of rational approximations, denominators, or scaled tails that a rational number cannot have. The criterion gives a route to irrationality when its requirements are verified.","reader_rule":"Some criteria are sufficient conditions; stronger characterisations give both necessary and sufficient conditions.","reader_deep":"The papers state these logical roles separately. Proving a criterion is useful, but applying it to an open problem still requires establishing the condition for the particular number under study."},{"object_id":"term:kernel","preferred_label":"kernel","reader_preview":"A kernel is a system's central core; this site uses the word for both Lean's proof checker and Plectis's command router.","reader_card":"The Lean kernel checks proof objects against the formal language's rules. Plectis's `kernel.py` instead answers questions about the repository and directs agents towards relevant files, records, and actions.","reader_rule":"The surrounding discussion of proofs or navigation identifies which meaning applies.","reader_deep":"The two programs have different responsibilities and guarantees. A route returned by Plectis's kernel is navigation information, while acceptance by the Lean kernel concerns the validity of a specific encoded proof."},{"object_id":"term:laboratory","preferred_label":"laboratory","reader_preview":"In Plectis, a laboratory is a controlled setting for trying a proposed mechanism before relying on it in live work.","reader_card":"It runs candidate checks, rules, or workflows against selected fixtures and records what happens. The small, explicit setting makes it easier to investigate failures and compare proposed changes.","reader_rule":"Laboratory evidence covers the experiment that was actually run.","reader_deep":"A successful trial can justify further testing or a proposed integration. It does not automatically change a governing rule or establish that the mechanism will behave the same way on every live input."},{"object_id":"term:lambert_series","preferred_label":"Lambert series","reader_preview":"A Lambert series has the form \u2211 a\u2099x\u207f/(1 \u2212 x\u207f), usually summed over positive integers n where it converges.","reader_card":"At x = 1/b, its terms become a\u2099/(b\u207f \u2212 1). Expanding the denominators as geometric series connects the coefficients to sums over divisors, under conditions that justify rearranging the terms.","reader_rule":"A power series \u2211 a\u2099x\u207f is a different expression and is not automatically a Lambert series.","reader_deep":"That distinction matters here: the totient power series and the sums with denominators b\u207f \u2212 1 are related number-theory objects, but their definitions and convergence arguments must be read separately."},{"object_id":"term:latex","preferred_label":"LaTeX","reader_preview":"LaTeX is a system for writing structured documents that a typesetting engine turns into formatted output, usually a PDF.","reader_card":"Authors write text together with commands for sections, equations, references, and layout. This is widely used for mathematical papers because complex notation and cross-references can be maintained in source form.","reader_rule":"The source file and rendered document serve different purposes and should correspond to the same version.","reader_deep":"The site publishes readable paper views alongside source material where available. A successful typesetting run checks document construction, not the correctness of the mathematics."},{"object_id":"term:lattice","preferred_label":"lattice","reader_preview":"In Plectis, the lattice is the network of relationships between rules, skills, concepts, code, and evidence.","reader_card":"Its connections state how items relate: a standard may govern a skill, a component may implement a mechanism, and a record may support a claim. This lets a reader follow meaning across different kinds of artifact.","reader_rule":"This project usage does not assert the axioms of a mathematical lattice.","reader_deep":"In order theory, a lattice instead requires a greatest lower bound and least upper bound for every pair. The local term is a name for the system's relationship graph."},{"object_id":"term:lattice_transposition","preferred_label":"lattice transposition","reader_preview":"Lattice transposition moves a reusable lesson into the kind of system record that can apply it to future work.","reader_card":"A local failure may reveal a missing instruction, validation rule, or concept. Plectis retains the original case and places the general refinement in the narrowest existing skill, standard, or other owner that can carry it.","reader_rule":"The lesson's scope must survive the move.","reader_deep":"For example, a faulty glossary preview may justify changing the shared glossary rule. It does not automatically justify changing how every kind of document is written."},{"object_id":"term:launch_scope_decision","preferred_label":"launch-scope decision","reader_preview":"A launch-scope decision determines what is ready and authorised to be published or made available.","reader_card":"It considers the intended audience, included material, evidence, and unresolved limitations. In Plectis, component checks provide information for this decision but do not independently grant publication authority.","reader_rule":"A passing technical check answers its own conditions, not every question about release readiness.","reader_deep":"The person responsible for the release must make the decision within the authorised scope. A published selection should then match the material and evidence on which that decision was based."},{"object_id":"term:lay_person","preferred_label":"lay person","reader_preview":"A lay person is someone who is not a specialist in the subject being discussed.","reader_card":"The term concerns subject knowledge, not intelligence or general ability. A mathematician can be a lay reader of software architecture, just as an experienced programmer can be new to number theory.","reader_rule":"The glossary aims to explain unfamiliar terms without requiring specialist training.","reader_deep":"Readers can still contribute useful questions, corrections, or work with tools. Specialist judgement may be needed for particular claims, and the project should make that need clear rather than assume a reader's background."},{"object_id":"term:lean","preferred_label":"Lean","reader_preview":"Lean is a programming language and proof assistant used to state mathematics and check formal proofs.","reader_card":"Authors supply definitions, theorem statements, and proofs, often with tactics that help construct the proof object. Lean then checks that the object establishes the encoded proposition under its assumptions.","reader_rule":"Successful checking applies to that precise formal statement.","reader_deep":"Admissions, added axioms, or an incorrectly translated problem can limit what acceptance establishes. The papers therefore need readable statements and evidence of the relevant checks alongside the Lean source."},{"object_id":"term:lean_kernel","preferred_label":"Lean kernel","reader_preview":"The Lean kernel is the core that checks whether a formal proof object has the type claimed for it.","reader_card":"Proof-writing tools and tactics help construct objects that the kernel checks according to Lean's logical rules. This separates proof search from the final checking operation.","reader_rule":"Acceptance depends on the encoded statement, definitions, and axioms used by the proof.","reader_deep":"The kernel does not judge whether that statement captures an informal conjecture or whether a result is significant. Those questions require mathematical interpretation and review of the correspondence with the paper."},{"object_id":"term:lean_standard_axioms","preferred_label":"Lean's standard axioms","reader_preview":"Lean's usual mathematical foundations include propositional extensionality, quotient soundness, and classical choice.","reader_card":"Their names are `propext`, `Quot.sound`, and `Classical.choice`. They support standard mathematical reasoning in Lean and Mathlib. An axiom audit identifies which assumptions a particular declaration depends on.","reader_rule":"\u201cNo added axioms\u201d here means no assumptions beyond the allowed mathematical foundations, not that the proof uses no axioms at all.","reader_deep":"Lean also exposes mechanisms such as `sorryAx` for unfinished proofs. These must be distinguished from the permitted foundations when checking whether a result has proof gaps."},{"object_id":"term:least_common_multiple","preferred_label":"least common multiple","reader_preview":"For positive integers, the least common multiple is the smallest positive integer divisible by all of them.","reader_card":"For example, the least common multiple of 4 and 6 is 12. In prime factorisation, it keeps the highest exponent of each prime appearing in the inputs.","reader_rule":"Positivity matters because zero is also a common multiple but is not the intended minimum here.","reader_deep":"Least common multiples combine periodic conditions: an event repeating every 4 steps and one repeating every 6 steps align every 12 steps. Definitions involving zero or empty collections need their stated conventions."},{"object_id":"term:lebesgue_measure","preferred_label":"Lebesgue measure","reader_preview":"Lebesgue measure generalises length on the real line, area in the plane, and volume in higher dimensions.","reader_card":"For an interval it gives the usual length, but it also applies to many fragmented or irregular sets. A set can contain infinitely many points and still have measure zero.","reader_rule":"Claims using Lebesgue measure apply to measurable sets in the stated space.","reader_deep":"The papers use it to distinguish the size of a set from its number of points or its topological shape. Positive measure, containing an interval, and being infinite are different properties."},{"object_id":"term:lemma","preferred_label":"lemma","reader_preview":"A lemma is a proved statement introduced mainly to support another argument.","reader_card":"It has the same need for a valid proof as a theorem. The label describes its role in the exposition, not a lower standard of correctness or a guarantee that it is easy.","reader_rule":"Read its hypotheses before applying it to a later step.","reader_deep":"A lemma may isolate the main difficulty, provide a reusable technical fact, or organise a long proof. Counting lemmas therefore gives little information by itself about the number of distinct mathematical ideas."},{"object_id":"term:lemniscate","preferred_label":"lemniscate","reader_preview":"A polynomial lemniscate is a set defined by the size of a complex polynomial, commonly the curve |p(z)| = c.","reader_card":"The term is also used, with qualification, for a sublevel region such as |p(z)| < c or |p(z)| \u2264 c. These regions can have several components surrounding the polynomial's roots.","reader_rule":"Check whether the paper means the level curve, the open region, or the filled closed region.","reader_deep":"In the Erd\u0151s #1041 material, paths inside the open unit region |p(z)| < 1 are central. Being on its boundary is different from being inside it."},{"object_id":"term:level_set","preferred_label":"level set","reader_preview":"A level set consists of all points where a function has one specified value.","reader_card":"For a real-valued function g, the level set at c is {x : g(x) = c}. In the plane, g(x,y) = x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 has a unit circle as its level set at 1.","reader_rule":"A level set need not be a smooth curve; critical points can produce singularities or changes of shape.","reader_deep":"Polynomial lemniscates use g(z) = |p(z)|. Their level curves are different from the regions defined by |p(z)| < c or |p(z)| \u2264 c."},{"object_id":"term:limit","preferred_label":"limit","reader_preview":"\u201cLimit\u201d can mean a restriction or, in mathematics, the value approached by a sequence or function.","reader_card":"On the system pages, a scope limit states how far a claim or action reaches. A service limit instead restricts use of a resource. Mathematical limits describe behaviour as an input approaches a point or infinity.","reader_rule":"The surrounding phrase determines which meaning applies.","reader_deep":"For a mathematical example, 1/n tends to the limit zero as n increases, although every term is positive. The separate mathematical-limit entry explains that precise sense in more detail."},{"object_id":"term:lineage","preferred_label":"lineage","reader_preview":"Lineage records where an item came from and which transformations led to its present form.","reader_card":"For a generated page, it may identify source records, the builder, and the relevant versions. For a claim, it may connect an interpretation to earlier results or observations.","reader_rule":"Origin and correctness are separate questions: a traceable item can still contain an error.","reader_deep":"Plectis uses lineage to make copies and summaries inspectable. When a source changes, those connections help identify which derived artifacts may need to be rebuilt or reconsidered."},{"object_id":"term:living_system_posture","preferred_label":"living system posture","reader_preview":"Living system posture means treating claims about a changing system as statements about an identifiable state or version.","reader_card":"A report can remain a valid record of an earlier run after the implementation has changed. Plectis distinguishes that historical record from evidence about the system as it exists now.","reader_rule":"The relevant source and refresh rule determine whether an old observation still applies.","reader_deep":"This does not make every mathematical theorem time-dependent. It concerns facts such as which proof version was checked, which files were published, or which behaviour a particular run demonstrated."},{"object_id":"term:locus","preferred_label":"locus","reader_preview":"A locus is a place where something occurs or is defined; in mathematics, it can mean all points satisfying a condition.","reader_card":"In the system documentation, a code locus identifies the file or function implementing a behaviour. In geometry, the locus of points at distance one from the origin is the unit circle.","reader_rule":"The subject determines whether the word means a source location or a mathematical set.","reader_deep":"Both uses help make a description precise: they replace an unspecified \u201csomewhere\u201d with a location or condition that can be examined directly."},{"object_id":"term:logarithm","preferred_label":"logarithm","reader_preview":"For a positive base b other than 1, the real logarithm log_b(x) is the exponent y satisfying b\u02b8 = x, for x > 0.","reader_card":"For example, log\u2082(8)=3 because 2\u00b3=8. In advanced mathematics, log without a base usually means the natural logarithm with base e.","reader_rule":"For positive inputs, logarithms turn products into sums and real powers into multipliers: log(xy) = log x + log y and log(x\u1d43) = a log x. Complex logarithms require separate branch choices.","reader_deep":"The papers take logarithms of products and least common multiples to compare multiplicative growth efficiently using additive estimates and asymptotic scales."},{"object_id":"term:machine_checked","preferred_label":"machine-checked","reader_preview":"Machine-checked means software has verified a stated property of the supplied material.","reader_card":"The strength of that verification depends on the checker. A Lean kernel check concerns a formal proof; a schema check concerns a file's structure; a test run concerns behaviour on particular inputs.","reader_rule":"Always identify what was checked and by which method.","reader_deep":"The phrase alone does not mean that every surrounding claim is true. The encoded statement, assumptions, source version, and connection to the informal explanation determine how far a mathematical checking result reaches."},{"object_id":"term:manifest","preferred_label":"manifest","reader_preview":"A manifest is a structured inventory of the files or objects included in a build, package, or release.","reader_card":"It may record paths, versions, content digests, sizes, and relationships. Plectis uses such inventories to describe what a published collection contains and to support consistency checks.","reader_rule":"The manifest's listed content should agree with the actual files.","reader_deep":"A manifest helps detect missing, unexpected, or changed artifacts. It does not itself establish the correctness of their contents, and its own source and generation process need to remain identifiable."},{"object_id":"term:map","preferred_label":"map","reader_preview":"A map presents a collection and the relationships that help someone navigate it.","reader_card":"Plectis's software maps are generated views of stored objects, such as components and their dependencies. The view lets a reader choose a part before opening its detailed sources.","reader_rule":"In a mathematical passage, \u201cmap\u201d commonly means a function instead.","reader_deep":"For a generated system map, completeness depends on what the underlying analysis captures. Links to source and a refresh record help readers understand both the represented relationships and the limits of the view."},{"object_id":"term:mathematical_continuity","preferred_label":"mathematical continuity","reader_preview":"A function is continuous when sufficiently close inputs always produce sufficiently close outputs.","reader_card":"For a function between metric spaces, continuity at x means that every output tolerance \u03b5 > 0 has an input tolerance \u03b4 > 0: whenever y is less than \u03b4 from x, f(y) is less than \u03b5 from f(x). For real or complex functions, these distances are written |y\u2212x| and |f(y)\u2212f(x)|.","reader_rule":"Continuity allows bending and changing speed but forbids jumps. A continuous path is a continuous function from an interval into the space being studied.","reader_deep":"The lemniscate problem requires a path whose every point stays inside {|f|<1}; continuity makes it one unbroken connection between the selected roots."},{"object_id":"term:mathematical_induction","preferred_label":"mathematical induction","reader_preview":"Mathematical induction proves a claim for every natural-number index by establishing a starting case and a valid step to the next case.","reader_card":"The step assumes the claim for an arbitrary index n and proves it for n + 1. Together with the base case, that gives the claim throughout the stated range.","reader_rule":"The starting index and induction hypothesis must match the statement being proved.","reader_deep":"Strong induction allows the step to use all earlier cases. The papers use these methods to establish recurrence formulas, divisibility propagation, and finite identities later used in arguments about limits."},{"object_id":"term:mathematical_limit","preferred_label":"mathematical limit","reader_preview":"A mathematical limit describes the value a sequence or function approaches as its index or input approaches a specified point or infinity.","reader_card":"For a real sequence with limit L, every positive tolerance around L eventually contains all later terms. The terms need not ever equal L; 1/n tends to zero while staying positive.","reader_rule":"The limit and the value at a particular input are different concepts.","reader_deep":"For functions, the relevant domain and direction of approach matter. An argument about an infinite sum uses the limit of its finite partial sums."},{"object_id":"term:mathematical_priority","preferred_label":"mathematical priority","reader_preview":"Mathematical priority concerns who first established or discovered a result, as supported by the scholarly record.","reader_card":"It can involve dated manuscripts, publications, communications, and careful comparison of what each source actually proved. Closely related results may have different hypotheses, conclusions, or forms of contribution.","reader_rule":"The project's own contribution records cannot settle an unresolved priority question by declaration.","reader_deep":"When this site says it asks for no priority on someone else's solution, it means using the project as a starting point does not give the project authorship of that person's result."},{"object_id":"term:mathematician","preferred_label":"mathematician","reader_preview":"A mathematician studies mathematical structures and questions through definitions, reasoning, and proof.","reader_card":"The term covers many specialisms and forms of work, including research, teaching, exposition, and applications. Expertise relevant to one paper may differ from the expertise needed for another.","reader_rule":"The site invites mathematical judgement about correctness, context, significance, and useful next questions.","reader_deep":"A contribution may clarify a proof, identify prior work, refute a proposed step, or find a new result. Its value depends on the work itself rather than on agreement with the project's current direction."},{"object_id":"term:mathematics","preferred_label":"mathematics","reader_preview":"Mathematics studies patterns, quantities, structures, and relationships through precise definitions and logical reasoning.","reader_card":"Its results are established by proof, while examples, computation, and conjectures help discover what might be true. The mathematical pages here focus on selected problems associated with Erd\u0151s and related formal work in Lean.","reader_rule":"That collection is one research programme within a much wider subject.","reader_deep":"The site distinguishes proved results, finite calculations, conditional arguments, and open questions so readers can understand what each artifact contributes without treating every mathematical-looking output as a theorem."},{"object_id":"term:mathlib","preferred_label":"Mathlib","reader_preview":"Mathlib is the community-maintained library of mathematical definitions, theorems, and tools for Lean.","reader_card":"It supplies reusable material across many fields, along with notation and tactics that help authors build further proofs. A local project can import the relevant modules instead of developing all foundations itself.","reader_rule":"The Mathlib version is part of a formal project's reproducible environment.","reader_deep":"Using a theorem from the library requires matching its assumptions and types. New declarations built on Mathlib should distinguish the project's contribution from results already supplied by the library."},{"object_id":"term:mechanism","preferred_label":"mechanism","reader_preview":"A mechanism explains how an outcome is produced through particular inputs, steps, and interactions.","reader_card":"In Plectis, a useful mechanism description names what enters, what happens, what comes out, and which conditions constrain the process. This connects a conceptual explanation to implementation and evidence.","reader_rule":"A label for a capability is not yet an account of how it works.","reader_deep":"For example, \u201cdetects drift\u201d becomes inspectable when the description identifies the two sources compared, the comparison rule, and the record produced when they disagree."},{"object_id":"term:mechanism_contract","preferred_label":"mechanism contract","reader_preview":"A mechanism contract states what a component is expected to do and how that expectation can be checked.","reader_card":"It identifies the component's purpose, inputs or conditions, outputs, evidence, and scope limits. Plectis uses this information on component cards so readers can inspect different mechanisms with a consistent set of questions.","reader_rule":"The contract states the expectation; source and execution evidence show whether it is met.","reader_deep":"If the implementation or supported use changes, the contract needs to be reviewed with it. A broader description should not inherit evidence collected for an earlier, narrower behaviour."},{"object_id":"term:mersenne_number","preferred_label":"Mersenne number","reader_preview":"A Mersenne number has the form 2\u207f \u2212 1 for a positive integer n.","reader_card":"The sequence begins 1, 3, 7, 15, 31. Some are prime, but many are composite; \u201cMersenne prime\u201d is the more specific term for a prime of this form.","reader_rule":"In base two, 2\u207f \u2212 1 is written as n consecutive ones.","reader_deep":"The Erd\u0151s #257 material studies reciprocal sums involving these denominators. Expressions b\u207f \u2212 1 use a similar construction at another base, but the unqualified name Mersenne conventionally refers to base two."},{"object_id":"term:metabolism","preferred_label":"metabolism","reader_preview":"\u201cMetabolism\u201d is Plectis's name for the recurring work that keeps stored knowledge and generated views in step with their sources.","reader_card":"The process identifies changes, queues bounded work, refreshes affected artifacts, and records the checks. It covers upkeep such as rebuilding an index or incorporating a supported lesson into an existing rule.","reader_rule":"The biological word is a metaphor for maintenance and renewal in the software.","reader_deep":"The process should have explicit owners and stopping conditions. A refresh that runs successfully still needs to preserve source meaning and satisfy the checks governing its output."},{"object_id":"term:metadata_only","preferred_label":"metadata-only","reader_preview":"Metadata-only means a record contains information about material or a run without including the underlying bodies themselves.","reader_card":"It may list counts, identifiers, classifications, digests, and outcomes while omitting the source text or inputs. This can make a record smaller or limit what it discloses.","reader_rule":"The omitted content may still be necessary to independently inspect or repeat the work.","reader_deep":"A digest can identify a file but cannot reconstruct it. A metadata-only result therefore needs an appropriate source route if readers are expected to examine the evidence behind its verdict."},{"object_id":"term:microcosm","preferred_label":"Microcosm","reader_preview":"Microcosm is an earlier public name for Plectis that remains in some technical paths and compatibility identifiers.","reader_card":"Existing package names, schemas, files, and links may retain the old spelling to preserve compatibility and traceability. Current reader-facing descriptions use Plectis for the project.","reader_rule":"The presence of the old name does not by itself identify a separate project or release.","reader_deep":"An ordinary phrase such as \u201ca microcosm of a larger system\u201d has a different meaning: a small representative example. The glossary keeps that everyday sense separate from this historical project identifier."},{"object_id":"term:microcosm_instance","preferred_label":"a microcosm","reader_preview":"A microcosm is a small example that reflects features of a larger system or situation.","reader_card":"Calling a local case a microcosm suggests that it makes a wider pattern easier to see. The comparison is useful only to the extent that the relevant relationships are actually shared.","reader_rule":"A representative example does not automatically establish every claim about the larger whole.","reader_deep":"This ordinary sense is distinct from Microcosm, the project's earlier name. When a component illustrates a general mechanism, its evidence still applies first to the particular case that was built and checked."},{"object_id":"term:mining","preferred_label":"mining","reader_preview":"Here, \u201cmining\u201d means sustained searching through candidate arguments and experiments for useful mathematical results or failures.","reader_card":"A person or agent follows a research direction, tries approaches, checks candidates, and preserves useful outcomes. The term describes repeated exploration rather than a single answer to a prompt.","reader_rule":"The amount of computation does not determine the value of what is found.","reader_deep":"Results still need verification, explanation, and attribution. A failed approach can be useful when it records a precise obstruction, while many unchecked drafts may add little to the understanding of a problem."},{"object_id":"term:model","preferred_label":"model","reader_preview":"A model is a representation used to describe, predict, or reason about something.","reader_card":"Here the word often refers to an AI model used by an agent. In mathematics, a model may instead be a structure satisfying a theory's axioms; in software, a data model specifies how information is organised.","reader_rule":"The surrounding qualifier identifies the intended meaning.","reader_deep":"An AI model is only one part of an agent's working setup. Tools, instructions, available evidence, and permissions also affect what the agent can do and what its answers can establish."},{"object_id":"term:module","preferred_label":"module","reader_preview":"A module is a unit of code or written material organised around a subject or responsibility.","reader_card":"A code module groups related definitions and exposes interfaces to other code. A Plectis paper module explains a subsystem and links its concepts, implementation, and evidence.","reader_rule":"The module's dependencies determine what else is needed to understand or use it.","reader_deep":"The same word also has a separate algebraic meaning, generalising a vector space from a field to a ring. That mathematical use must be read from its own definitions and context."},{"object_id":"term:modulus","preferred_label":"modulus","reader_preview":"In modular arithmetic, the modulus is the positive integer that determines which remainders are distinguished.","reader_card":"Modulo 5, integers fall into five classes represented by 0, 1, 2, 3, and 4. Changing the modulus changes these classes and the divisibility information they retain.","reader_rule":"In a complex-number passage, \u201cmodulus\u201d instead means magnitude: |z| is the distance of z from zero.","reader_deep":"Both meanings occur in these papers. A phrase such as \u201ccritical-value modulus\u201d refers to a complex magnitude, while \u201cmodulo m\u201d refers to arithmetic with integer remainders."},{"object_id":"term:monic","preferred_label":"monic","reader_preview":"A polynomial is monic if its leading coefficient is 1.","reader_card":"The leading coefficient multiplies its highest-degree nonzero term. For example, z\u00b3 + 2z \u2212 7 is monic, while 3z\u00b3 + 2z \u2212 7 is not.","reader_rule":"This normalisation concerns the polynomial's scale, not the positions of its roots alone.","reader_deep":"In lemniscate questions, multiplying a polynomial by a constant changes sets defined by |p(z)| < 1 or |p(z)| = 1. A monic hypothesis therefore matters to geometric statements about those sets."},{"object_id":"term:morse_saddle","preferred_label":"Morse saddle","reader_preview":"A Morse saddle is a critical point of a smooth real-valued function whose second-order form is nondegenerate and has both positive and negative directions.","reader_card":"In two dimensions, the local behaviour resembles x\u00b2 \u2212 y\u00b2 after a suitable change of coordinates: the value rises in some directions and falls in others. Nondegeneracy rules out flat second-order directions.","reader_rule":"At a planar saddle, the level set through the point has four local half-branches.","reader_deep":"The lemniscate arguments use this local geometry. It must be distinguished from the behaviour of a singular flow or a degenerate critical point, where additional analysis is required."},{"object_id":"term:multiple","preferred_label":"multiple","reader_preview":"In arithmetic, a multiple of an integer d is a number d\u00b7k for some integer k.","reader_card":"For example, 12 is a multiple of 3 because 12 = 3\u00b74. A common multiple of several positive integers is divisible by every one of them.","reader_rule":"The adjective \u201cmultiple\u201d also means \u201cmore than one\u201d in ordinary prose.","reader_deep":"In phrases such as \u201cmultiple root\u201d, it has a more specific algebraic role: a root occurs with multiplicity greater than one. The surrounding noun therefore matters when reading this word in a paper."},{"object_id":"term:multiple_root","preferred_label":"multiple root","reader_preview":"A multiple root of a nonzero polynomial is a root appearing with multiplicity at least two.","reader_card":"For example, z\u00b2 \u2212 2z + 1 = (z \u2212 1)\u00b2 has 1 as a double root. In general, multiplicity k means (z \u2212 a)\u1d4f divides the polynomial but the next power does not.","reader_rule":"Over the real or complex numbers, every multiple root is also a root of the derivative.","reader_deep":"This changes local geometry and root counting. A polynomial's degree counts roots over the complex numbers with multiplicity, while the number of distinct root locations can be smaller."},{"object_id":"term:multiplicative_order","preferred_label":"multiplicative order","reader_preview":"For b coprime to a positive modulus Q, the multiplicative order is the least positive k for which b\u1d4f \u2261 1 modulo Q.","reader_card":"It measures the repetition period of powers of b in modular arithmetic. For example, the order of 2 modulo 7 is 3, since 2\u00b3 leaves remainder 1 and no smaller positive exponent does.","reader_rule":"Finding an exponent with remainder 1 gives a period, which need not be the least one.","reader_deep":"Exact order requires a minimality argument. The papers use this distinction when connecting denominator arithmetic with periodic digit patterns and finite certificates."},{"object_id":"term:named_basis","preferred_label":"named basis","reader_preview":"A named basis identifies the exact inputs, assumptions, or sources on which a claim rests.","reader_card":"Plectis uses the phrase to make a result reproducible and inspectable: the supporting material should be specified precisely enough for another checker or reader to examine it.","reader_rule":"This is a statement of support, distinct from a vector-space basis in linear algebra.","reader_deep":"A path without a version may not identify the bytes used in an earlier run. Source revisions, content digests, and explicit hypotheses help preserve the intended basis as files and explanations change."},{"object_id":"term:natural_number","preferred_label":"natural number","reader_preview":"A natural number is a counting number; in Lean, the natural numbers include zero: 0, 1, 2, 3, \u2026 .","reader_card":"Mathematical authors differ on whether their notation \u2115 starts at zero or one. Lean's type `Nat` starts at zero, and positivity must be stated separately when needed.","reader_rule":"Check the convention in a paper rather than inferring it from the phrase alone.","reader_deep":"This matters for factorials, denominators, and sequence indices. A theorem valid for positive n may need a separate hypothesis or a separate zero case when encoded over `Nat`."},{"object_id":"term:navigable","preferred_label":"navigable","reader_preview":"Material is navigable when a reader has usable routes from its summary to the relevant detail or next action.","reader_card":"In Plectis, these routes can be links, anchors, source references, or commands. They help a person or agent move from a term or result to the object being described.","reader_rule":"A route must resolve to the intended material to be useful.","reader_deep":"The presence of a link alone does not ensure good navigation. Its label, destination, access requirements, and level of detail should fit the reader's question."},{"object_id":"term:navigation","preferred_label":"navigation","reader_preview":"Navigation is the process of finding and moving between relevant parts of a body of information.","reader_card":"Plectis provides collections, summaries, search, links, and command routes to help a reader reach the source needed for a task. Different levels of detail let the reader narrow the question before opening large files.","reader_rule":"Navigation should preserve the identity and meaning of the selected object across views.","reader_deep":"A good route reduces unnecessary reading while keeping evidence accessible. It cannot replace the underlying material when the task requires checking a claim or changing an implementation."},{"object_id":"term:necessary_and_sufficient","preferred_label":"necessary and sufficient","reader_preview":"A condition is necessary and sufficient for a statement when each implies the other.","reader_card":"Necessary means the statement cannot hold without the condition. Sufficient means the condition guarantees the statement. Together they give an equivalence, often written \u201cif and only if\u201d or \u2194.","reader_rule":"A condition can have one of these roles without having the other.","reader_deep":"For example, divisibility by 4 is sufficient for an integer to be even but is not necessary: 6 is even. Distinguishing the directions prevents a stronger research target from being mistaken for the original problem."},{"object_id":"term:negative_case","preferred_label":"negative case","reader_preview":"A negative case is a test input designed to exercise a failure or refusal that the system should detect.","reader_card":"It accompanies valid inputs so a checker cannot appear successful simply by accepting everything. Plectis may plant a forged result, missing action, or invalid condition and verify that the appropriate finding is produced.","reader_rule":"The expected response must be stated for the particular case.","reader_deep":"Rejecting one planted error supports that detection path. It does not establish that every possible error will be detected, and valid inputs are also needed to check for inappropriate refusals."},{"object_id":"term:neighbourhood","preferred_label":"neighbourhood","reader_preview":"A set containing an open set around a specified point or subset.","reader_card":"For a point x in the plane, any set containing a small open disc centred at x is a neighbourhood of x. Its size may depend on x.","reader_rule":"Authors sometimes say neighbourhood for the open disc itself and sometimes for any set containing one; the common content is local room around the target.","reader_deep":"The paper needs neighbourhoods where analytic expansions converge and saddle geometry has a controlled local form before connecting it to global paths."},{"object_id":"term:newton_flow","preferred_label":"Newton flow","reader_preview":"For a complex polynomial p, the Newton flow studied here follows the differential equation z\u2032 = \u2212p(z)/p\u2032(z) where p\u2032(z) \u2260 0.","reader_card":"It is a continuous analogue of Newton's root-finding iteration. Along a regular trajectory, the chain rule gives d(p(z(t)))/dt = \u2212p(z(t)), so the polynomial value decays exponentially.","reader_rule":"The displayed vector field is undefined at critical points where p\u2032 vanishes.","reader_deep":"This limitation matters in the lemniscate arguments: local trajectories and their behaviour near critical points require separate justification. The decay identity applies only along intervals where the trajectory is defined and p\u2032 does not vanish."},{"object_id":"term:newton_vector_field","preferred_label":"Newton vector field","reader_preview":"The Newton vector field assigns the velocity \u2212p(z)/p\u2032(z) to points where the polynomial derivative p\u2032 is nonzero.","reader_card":"A vector field prescribes a direction and speed at each allowed point. Its integral curves solve the Newton-flow equation; the field and the resulting trajectories are related but distinct objects.","reader_rule":"The quotient is undefined where p\u2032 vanishes, even if a special case allows a separately defined extension.","reader_deep":"Along a regular trajectory, the polynomial value satisfies w\u2032 = \u2212w. This explains its exponential decay, while the existence, endpoints, and length of a global trajectory require additional arguments in the lemniscate papers."},{"object_id":"term:normal_form","preferred_label":"normal form","reader_preview":"A normal form is a specified standard shape in which an object or statement can be represented.","reader_card":"For example, a rational number can be written in lowest terms with a positive denominator. Some normal forms are unique; others still allow several representations, so uniqueness requires its own definition or theorem.","reader_rule":"Putting a problem in normal form can clarify its structure without solving it.","reader_deep":"The papers use normal forms to state exact remaining obligations. When equivalence is claimed, the transformation must preserve the original statement in both directions under the given assumptions."},{"object_id":"term:normalized_vanishing","preferred_label":"normalised vanishing","reader_preview":"Normalised vanishing means that a quantity tends to zero after division by a specified nonzero scale.","reader_card":"For example, n divided by n\u00b2 tends to zero although n itself grows. The property describes relative size, often written a\u2099/b\u2099 \u2192 0, with the denominator's allowed values and sign specified.","reader_rule":"Changing the normalising scale changes the statement.","reader_deep":"In the reciprocal-tail work, a centred error is compared with an arithmetic scale. This relative smallness can combine with discrete constraints or lower bounds, but it does not alone imply that the unscaled error eventually equals zero."},{"object_id":"term:nowhere_dense","preferred_label":"nowhere dense","reader_preview":"A set is nowhere dense if its closure has empty interior.","reader_card":"On the real line, this means that even after adding all limit points, the set contains no nonempty open interval. Every open interval therefore contains a smaller open interval avoiding the closure.","reader_rule":"Having empty interior alone is weaker: the rational numbers contain no interval, but their closure is the whole real line.","reader_deep":"The papers distinguish this topological property from measure. A nowhere-dense set can have positive Lebesgue measure, so the word does not simply mean \u201csmall in length\u201d."},{"object_id":"term:number_theory","preferred_label":"number theory","reader_preview":"Number theory studies integers and their arithmetic relationships, including primes, divisibility, and congruences.","reader_card":"It also studies related objects such as rational numbers, algebraic numbers, and arithmetic functions. Problems about integer patterns can require tools from analysis, algebra, geometry, or combinatorics.","reader_rule":"The precise assumptions determine which arithmetic setting a theorem concerns.","reader_deep":"The papers here use number-theory ideas to examine reciprocal sums, prime factors, digit expansions, and related questions. A common subject does not make their separate hypotheses or open problems interchangeable."},{"object_id":"term:numerator","preferred_label":"numerator","reader_preview":"The numerator is the quantity being divided in a fraction: the a in a/b.","reader_card":"For example, 3 is the numerator of 3/4. For rational numbers written as integer fractions, cancelling a factor common to numerator and denominator can give a simpler expression for the same value.","reader_rule":"The numerator depends on the representation unless a normalisation is specified.","reader_deep":"Thus 1/2 and 2/4 have different written numerators but represent the same number. Arithmetic arguments often use the reduced numerator, with the fraction in lowest terms and a positive denominator."},{"object_id":"term:obs_studio","preferred_label":"OBS Studio","reader_preview":"OBS Studio is a free, open-source application for video recording and live streaming.","reader_card":"It can combine sources such as a screen, camera, and microphone into a recorded scene. The project uses recording tools to present demonstrations and explanations of work that readers can inspect elsewhere.","reader_rule":"A video shows the captured session rather than every possible behaviour of the system.","reader_deep":"Source links and runnable instructions complement a demonstration by letting viewers inspect the implementation and repeat the relevant steps in their own environment."},{"object_id":"term:observe","preferred_label":"observe","reader_preview":"In Plectis, observe is the step that gathers and records information without changing the material being examined.","reader_card":"It can read files, run a read-only probe, or inspect a response and turn the findings into a structured record. That record supplies evidence for a later decision or proposed change.","reader_rule":"Observation and application have different responsibilities: collecting evidence does not itself authorise a write.","reader_deep":"The observation should state its source, scope, and time or version where relevant. Those details determine whether a later action can still rely on it."},{"object_id":"term:omission_receipt","preferred_label":"omission receipt","reader_preview":"An omission receipt records what a shortened view leaves out and how to recover the fuller material.","reader_card":"Plectis uses it when a summary or packet intentionally excludes detail to fit a reading or context budget. The record explains the omission's reason and provides a route to the source.","reader_rule":"Omitting detail must not silently change the meaning of the retained claim.","reader_deep":"For example, a short result card can omit a lengthy proof while preserving the theorem's assumptions. The reader should be able to open that proof when its details become relevant."},{"object_id":"term:open_obligation","preferred_label":"open obligation","reader_preview":"An open obligation is a precise requirement that remains to be established in an argument or workflow.","reader_card":"In the mathematical records, it is often a lemma or hypothesis needed by a conditional result. Naming it makes the remaining work available for scrutiny and further attempts.","reader_rule":"An obligation needed by one approach may be stronger than, or unrelated to solving, the original problem directly.","reader_deep":"The record should state its logical role: equivalent target, sufficient condition, or supporting task. Proving a conditional theorem does not automatically discharge the obligation it assumes."},{"object_id":"term:open_problem","preferred_label":"open problem","reader_preview":"An open problem is a mathematical question for which no accepted resolution is known in the relevant literature.","reader_card":"Its statement specifies what would count as a solution or refutation. Related theorems, special cases, and computations can clarify the problem while leaving the full question unresolved.","reader_rule":"Open status is a fact about current knowledge and should be checked against up-to-date sources.","reader_deep":"The project distinguishes its own unfinished work from the wider field's open questions. A result missing from this repository may already be known elsewhere, and a newly published claim may still require review."},{"object_id":"term:open_set","preferred_label":"open set","reader_preview":"A set containing a small neighbourhood around each of its points.","reader_card":"The open unit disc {z:|z|<1} is open: every point inside has some positive-radius disc still contained in it. Boundary points with |z|=1 are excluded.","reader_rule":"Openness is relative to the surrounding space. In a metric space, each point must have a whole ball of some radius lying in the set.","reader_deep":"Strict inequalities such as |f(z)|<1 define open regions in which the paper seeks paths between polynomial roots."},{"object_id":"term:open_world","preferred_label":"open-world","reader_preview":"An open-world approach treats missing information as unknown rather than automatically false.","reader_card":"If a record does not contain a proof or observation, the system cannot conclude that none exists. Plectis uses this distinction when reasoning over incomplete evidence and public or private collections.","reader_rule":"A negative conclusion requires an appropriate completeness argument or a separate source of evidence.","reader_deep":"For a declared finite collection that has been fully checked, absence can settle a bounded question. Outside that boundary, \u201cnot found here\u201d and \u201cdoes not exist\u201d remain different claims."},{"object_id":"term:operator_proxy","preferred_label":"operator proxy","reader_preview":"An operator proxy carries out a bounded task on the operator's behalf using supplied instructions and context.","reader_card":"Plectis uses the term for an outside worker receiving a prepared packet. That worker can produce an answer or artifact within the assigned task, while the receiving side checks the result and decides how to incorporate it.","reader_rule":"Acting on someone's behalf does not confer all of that person's authority.","reader_deep":"The packet should state the purpose, limits, and expected output. Any access to tools or live material remains a separate part of the worker's permissions."},{"object_id":"term:operator_voice","preferred_label":"operator voice","reader_preview":"Operator voice is the preserved record of what the human operator actually said or wrote.","reader_card":"Plectis keeps it distinct from an agent's paraphrase, inference, or interpretation. That distinction makes it possible to revisit the original intent when a later summary is unclear or disputed.","reader_rule":"Preserving the words does not remove the need to interpret context and changes of mind carefully.","reader_deep":"Agent-authored analysis belongs in its own attributed record. Keeping both layers allows the system to learn from interpretation while retaining evidence of which claims and expressions came from the person."},{"object_id":"term:option_surface","preferred_label":"option surface","reader_preview":"An option surface is a structured view of the items or actions available within a category.","reader_card":"It presents selectable rows with enough identity, purpose, and source information to choose a next step. Plectis uses these views to browse kinds such as components, skills, and standards.","reader_rule":"The view should distinguish items that exist from actions currently permitted or supported.","reader_deep":"Selecting a row opens further detail or an authoritative source. A collection can be visible at overview level while still lacking the richer rows needed for reliable item-by-item navigation."},{"object_id":"term:orbit","preferred_label":"orbit","reader_preview":"An orbit is the path of an object under repeated application of a rule or continuous evolution through time.","reader_card":"For iteration, it starts at x and continues f(x), f(f(x)), and so on. For a flow, the parameter is continuous and the orbit follows a trajectory.","reader_rule":"The starting point and evolution rule determine which orbit is meant.","reader_deep":"An orbit may repeat, approach a limit, or behave otherwise. In modular arithmetic the state space is finite, but further hypotheses determine whether repetition starts immediately or only after an initial segment."},{"object_id":"term:organ","preferred_label":"organ (source identifier)","reader_preview":"Organ is the source-code term for a bounded unit that the public site usually calls a component.","reader_card":"It may appear in registry names and paths retained by the implementation. Each unit has a particular job, interfaces, and evidence describing its behaviour.","reader_rule":"The biological word is internal terminology; it does not add a separate guarantee or capability.","reader_deep":"When following a component card into source code, an organ identifier can refer to that same unit. The registry and linked source establish the identity and scope more precisely than the choice of label."},{"object_id":"term:orientation","preferred_label":"orientation","reader_preview":"Orientation gives a new reader the context needed to understand what they are looking at and where to go next.","reader_card":"It introduces the subject, scope, main objects, and available routes. On this site, overviews and reader packets help visitors choose a paper, component, or evidence record without first reading everything.","reader_rule":"Orientation should point to evidence while keeping its own summary role clear.","reader_deep":"A useful introduction answers the reader's first questions and leaves deeper detail accessible. It should not require learning the project's internal vocabulary before the subject itself makes sense."},{"object_id":"term:orphan","preferred_label":"orphan","reader_preview":"An orphan is an item that exists but lacks the expected connection to its index, parent, or related records.","reader_card":"In Plectis's navigation, this can mean source material that the generated map fails to place. The item may be valid and useful while remaining difficult to discover.","reader_rule":"The expected relationship determines whether the missing connection is actually a fault.","reader_deep":"Repair may involve adding the appropriate source relationship or correcting an index builder. Removing the orphaned item is not automatically the right response, because absence from a map does not imply that the source is unwanted."},{"object_id":"term:p_adic_valuation","preferred_label":"p-adic valuation","reader_preview":"The p-adic valuation of a nonzero integer counts how many factors of the prime p divide it.","reader_card":"For example, v\u2082(12) = 2 because 12 = 2\u00b2\u00b73. For a nonzero rational a/b, the valuation is v\u209a(a) \u2212 v\u209a(b), which can be negative.","reader_rule":"Valuations add under multiplication; addition can involve cancellation and needs a separate rule.","reader_deep":"In a finite sum, a unique term with strictly smallest p-adic valuation determines the valuation of the sum. The papers use this to show when a prime factor in a denominator cannot disappear through cancellation."},{"object_id":"term:package","preferred_label":"package","reader_preview":"A package groups related material so it can be distributed, installed, or used as a unit.","reader_card":"In software, it may contain code and dependency information. A research or release package may instead collect source, documents, checks, and metadata needed for a particular submission or public version.","reader_rule":"The package's manifest and instructions determine what is included and how to use it.","reader_deep":"Packaging makes material easier to transfer, but it does not verify its contents. Its evidence, licences, and dependencies still need to match the intended use."},{"object_id":"term:packet","preferred_label":"packet","reader_preview":"A packet is a selected bundle of information prepared for a particular reader or task.","reader_card":"On the public site, a packet is often a downloadable JSON file containing summaries, identities, links, and selected content. An assistant can read it without reconstructing the site through separate page requests.","reader_rule":"The packet's declared scope determines which parts of the public record it includes.","reader_deep":"It remains a generated view and can become stale. Source references let a reader verify the underlying material and recover detail that was not included in the download."},{"object_id":"term:palomar","preferred_label":"Palomar","reader_preview":"Palomar is an external registry used to present formally checked mathematical results with identifiable source and checking records.","reader_card":"Plectis prepares selected result packages for this route. A package identifies the statements and repository version being submitted so an external record can refer to a concrete object.","reader_rule":"Preparation, submission, successful checking, and public registration are distinct states.","reader_deep":"A registry record supports inspection of the statements it names. It does not automatically establish novelty, mathematical importance, peer review, or correctness of every other claim in the source repository."},{"object_id":"term:paper","preferred_label":"paper","reader_preview":"A mathematical paper presents a question, argument, or result in a form intended for readers to study and assess.","reader_card":"It normally states definitions and hypotheses, explains the proof or method, and identifies relevant prior work. This site offers papers as PDFs and web reading views, with source links where available.","reader_rule":"The file format does not determine the work's evidence or review status.","reader_deep":"A paper may contain formal results, ordinary mathematical arguments, experiments, or unresolved questions. Those roles should remain clear in the text rather than be inferred from the presence of equations or a polished layout."},{"object_id":"term:paper_module","preferred_label":"paper module","reader_preview":"A paper module is Plectis's maintained explanation of one subsystem or recurring mechanism.","reader_card":"It connects the subsystem's purpose, structure, rules, source locations, and evidence. The aim is to give a reader enough understanding to act without reconstructing the design from scattered files.","reader_rule":"A paper module is system documentation and need not be a scholarly research paper.","reader_deep":"Its explanation should remain linked to the implementation it describes. Source changes can require a refresh, while historical notes may remain useful if their earlier scope and version are explicit."},{"object_id":"term:partial_sum","preferred_label":"partial sum","reader_preview":"A partial sum adds finitely many terms from the beginning of a series.","reader_card":"For a series \u2211 a\u2099 starting at n = 1, its Nth partial sum is S_N = a\u2081 + \u22ef + a_N. The infinite sum, if it exists, is the limit of these finite sums.","reader_rule":"The unadded terms form the tail of the series.","reader_deep":"Computing a partial sum gives an approximation only when the remaining tail is controlled. An exact value for the finite sum does not by itself give the exact infinite sum. A proved error estimate connects the approximation with the limiting value."},{"object_id":"term:passion","preferred_label":"passion","reader_preview":"In Plectis's description of judgement, passion means keeping the purpose and human importance of the work in view.","reader_card":"It asks an agent to understand why a task matters, so routine execution does not lose the aim that made the work worth doing. It works alongside care and proportionate judgement.","reader_rule":"Motivation does not relax requirements for evidence, permissions, or truthful reporting.","reader_deep":"The practical effect should be a better choice of action or useful follow-through. Enthusiastic prose alone adds nothing, and commitment to an idea must leave room for correction when the evidence changes."},{"object_id":"term:path_connected","preferred_label":"path connected","reader_preview":"A set is path connected if any two of its points can be joined by a continuous path entirely within the set.","reader_card":"Formally, the path is a continuous map from the closed interval [0,1], with the chosen points as its endpoints. Every intermediate point of the path must remain in the set.","reader_rule":"Path connectedness implies connectedness, but the converse fails for some spaces.","reader_deep":"It also does not give a bound on path length. The lemniscate problem asks for paths satisfying both a location condition and a quantitative length requirement."},{"object_id":"term:path_handle","preferred_label":"path handle","reader_preview":"A path handle is a stable short name that resolves to a file or other source location.","reader_card":"Plectis uses it to refer to an item without repeating a long or changeable path. The associated record can identify the current location, role, and route for inspecting it.","reader_rule":"The handle preserves identity only if its resolution is maintained when the target moves.","reader_deep":"This helps keep packets compact and migrations traceable. A short name that no longer resolves correctly is a broken reference, even when the name itself has remained unchanged."},{"object_id":"term:pdf","preferred_label":"PDF","reader_preview":"PDF is a document format designed to preserve a page's layout across different devices and applications.","reader_card":"It can contain text, images, fonts, links, and accessibility information. Mathematical papers commonly use it to keep equations and pagination stable for reading and citation.","reader_rule":"A PDF's appearance does not establish its correctness or whether its text is accessible.","reader_deep":"The site pairs papers with other reading and source routes where available. When comparing a web view with a PDF, the relevant version matters: two readable forms can otherwise describe different revisions."},{"object_id":"term:peer","preferred_label":"peer","reader_preview":"A peer is another participant working at a comparable level or within a shared activity.","reader_card":"In Plectis's agent documentation, it often means another agent that can use recorded work and lessons. The participants may have different tools, permissions, or context even when they use the same model.","reader_rule":"Being peers does not imply shared memory or identical authority.","reader_deep":"Durable records provide the connection between sessions. A later agent can inherit an attributed lesson through source material, while remaining responsible for checking that the lesson fits its current task."},{"object_id":"term:peer_propagate","preferred_label":"peer-propagate","reader_preview":"A peer-propagate record passes an attributed operating lesson from one agent to later agents.","reader_card":"It states what was learned, the source case, where it applies, and any uncertainty or limits. Plectis uses this form for useful guidance that is not yet established as a governing rule.","reader_rule":"The record remains candidate knowledge rather than operator instruction or active doctrine.","reader_deep":"Later agents can test it against new situations. Repeated support may justify refining a shared skill or standard, while a counterexample can narrow or retire the lesson without rewriting its original evidence."},{"object_id":"term:perfect_set","preferred_label":"perfect set","reader_preview":"A perfect set is closed and has no isolated points.","reader_card":"Every point of the set can be approached arbitrarily closely by other points in the set, and the set contains all its limit points. The usual Cantor set is a standard example.","reader_rule":"Perfect does not mean that the set contains an interval or has positive measure.","reader_deep":"These are separate properties in analysis. Under the usual definition, the empty set is also perfect; statements about nonempty perfect sets must include that extra condition when it is needed."},{"object_id":"term:periodic","preferred_label":"periodic","reader_preview":"A sequence is periodic if shifting it by some fixed positive number of positions leaves every term unchanged.","reader_card":"Thus a\u2099\u208a\u209a = a\u2099 for all relevant n, where p is a period. It is eventually periodic if this equality holds only after a finite initial segment.","reader_rule":"A period need not be the smallest period.","reader_deep":"Rational numbers have eventually periodic digit expansions in any integer base greater than one. The distinction between repetition from the start and repetition after an initial segment matters in the papers' arithmetic arguments."},{"object_id":"term:phase","preferred_label":"phase","reader_preview":"A phase is a defined stage of work; in Plectis, it carries the objective and state of a resumable work programme.","reader_card":"It groups the relevant sources, planned steps, current activity, and closeout information. This makes it possible to stop and resume work without relying on one agent's memory.","reader_rule":"A phase's current state should be read from its live record.","reader_deep":"The phase organises work rather than proving that the objective has been achieved. Completed steps, remaining obligations, and the conditions for moving on still need explicit evidence."},{"object_id":"term:pip","preferred_label":"pip","reader_preview":"pip is a package installer for Python.","reader_card":"It installs software distributions and their declared dependencies into a Python environment, using a package index or another specified source. Projects often provide requirement files or package metadata to describe what they need.","reader_rule":"The chosen environment and dependency versions affect what code is installed.","reader_deep":"For reproducible checks, follow the repository's stated setup and version constraints. A successful installation makes the packages available; it does not establish that a project's tests pass or that its results are correct."},{"object_id":"term:planted_error","preferred_label":"planted error","reader_preview":"A planted error is a deliberate fault added to a test to see whether a check detects it.","reader_card":"It creates a known negative case, such as an invalid claim or mismatched output. The test specifies which response should occur and compares that expectation with the checker's actual result.","reader_rule":"Detecting the planted error is evidence about that failure mode.","reader_deep":"A useful test suite also includes valid cases, because rejecting everything would catch the error without being a useful checker. More varied negative cases can expose different blind spots."},{"object_id":"term:planted_forgery","preferred_label":"planted forgery","reader_preview":"A planted forgery is deliberately false evidence or reporting placed in a controlled test input.","reader_card":"For example, a test note might claim that a command ran when the recorded actions show it did not. Plectis uses such cases to assess whether an audit distinguishes the false account from supported work.","reader_rule":"The forgery belongs to the test setup and should be identified as such.","reader_deep":"Successful detection supports the tested audit behaviour. It does not establish resistance to every possible misleading report, and the expected finding should identify what was actually false."},{"object_id":"term:plectis","preferred_label":"Plectis","reader_preview":"Plectis is this project's public name, covering its mathematical research, formal proofs, and supporting software.","reader_card":"The website connects papers and working records with Lean source, reusable software components, and checks. It aims to make both the results and the process accessible for inspection and further work.","reader_rule":"Each paper or component has its own scope and evidence status.","reader_deep":"The project name is not a certification that every question is solved or every mechanism is complete. Some older technical identifiers retain the name Microcosm for compatibility with earlier files and links."},{"object_id":"term:polynomial","preferred_label":"polynomial","reader_preview":"A finite sum of constant multiples of nonnegative whole-number powers of a variable.","reader_card":"For example, f(z)=z\u00b3+2z-5 is a polynomial: its terms use powers 3, 1, and 0, and its coefficients are 1, 2, and -5. Expressions such as 1/z or \u221az are not polynomials in z.","reader_rule":"The degree is the largest exponent with nonzero coefficient. Evaluating the polynomial means substituting a number for the variable and carrying out the indicated arithmetic.","reader_deep":"The lemniscate papers study complex polynomials through their zeros, derivative, critical values, and sets where |f(z)| stays below a threshold."},{"object_id":"term:polynomial_degree","preferred_label":"degree of a polynomial","reader_preview":"The degree of a nonzero polynomial is the highest exponent with a nonzero coefficient.","reader_card":"For example, 3z\u2074 + 2z \u2212 1 has degree 4, while a nonzero constant has degree 0. Over the complex numbers, a degree-n polynomial has n roots counted with multiplicity.","reader_rule":"The zero polynomial needs a separate degree convention because it has no nonzero coefficient.","reader_deep":"Degree constrains possible algebraic behaviour without determining the locations or distinctness of roots. The lemniscate papers use degree in quantitative estimates and sometimes prove results only for particular degree ranges."},{"object_id":"term:polynomial_root","preferred_label":"root of a polynomial","reader_preview":"A number r for which a polynomial f has value f(r)=0.","reader_card":"For f(z)=z\u00b2-1, both 1 and -1 are roots because substituting either gives zero. The factorization f(z)=(z-1)(z+1) displays them directly.","reader_rule":"A nonconstant complex polynomial of degree n has n roots when repetitions are counted. A root can have multiplicity greater than one, as 0 does for z\u00b2.","reader_deep":"Problem 1041 asks whether two root occurrences can be joined by a short path staying in a prescribed polynomial sublevel set."},{"object_id":"term:positive_integer","preferred_label":"positive integer","reader_preview":"A positive integer is one of 1, 2, 3, \u2026 .","reader_card":"It is an integer strictly greater than zero. This differs from a nonnegative integer, which can also be zero, and from a positive real number, which need not be whole.","reader_rule":"The restriction often excludes an undefined or exceptional case.","reader_deep":"For example, n! is defined at zero, but 1/(n! \u2212 1) has zero denominator there and at n = 1. A series using that expression therefore needs an appropriate starting index."},{"object_id":"term:prime","preferred_label":"prime","reader_preview":"A prime number is an integer greater than 1 whose only positive divisors are 1 and itself.","reader_card":"The primes begin 2, 3, 5, 7, 11. The number 1 is not prime. Every integer greater than 1 factors uniquely into primes, apart from the order of the factors.","reader_rule":"Being prime is different from being coprime to another number.","reader_deep":"The papers use prime factors to analyse denominators, periods, and reciprocal sums. Claims about consecutive primes also depend on excluding any other prime between the two named values."},{"object_id":"term:prime_gap","preferred_label":"prime gap","reader_preview":"A prime gap is the difference between consecutive prime numbers.","reader_card":"For example, the gap from 7 to 11 is 4 because there is no prime between them. Differences between arbitrary primes are not necessarily prime gaps in this sense.","reader_rule":"The endpoint primes and the absence of an intervening prime are both part of the definition.","reader_deep":"Questions about prime gaps ask how these differences behave as primes grow. A computed range establishes the gaps in that range, while a claim about infinitely many gaps requires a general argument."},{"object_id":"term:prime_power","preferred_label":"prime power","reader_preview":"A prime power is a number p\u1d4f where p is prime and k is a positive integer.","reader_card":"For example, 8 = 2\u00b3 and 9 = 3\u00b2 are prime powers, while 12 is not because it has two different prime factors. Some contexts also allow exponent zero, so that convention must be stated.","reader_rule":"Having a prime-power divisor does not make the whole number a prime power.","reader_deep":"Prime powers record how often a prime factor occurs. This finer information is needed when the papers ask whether a denominator factor survives cancellation at a particular exponent."},{"object_id":"term:principle","preferred_label":"principle","reader_preview":"A principle is a general rule or commitment that guides decisions.","reader_card":"Plectis stores principles with stable identities, explanations, and relationships to more specific standards and mechanisms. They express why the system should behave in a certain way across multiple tasks.","reader_rule":"A broad principle still needs interpretation in the conditions of a particular case.","reader_deep":"Its implementation is assessed through the rules and actions that enact it. Recording a principle makes the commitment inspectable, but does not establish that every agent or component already follows it."},{"object_id":"term:problem_note","preferred_label":"problem note","reader_preview":"A problem note is a focused mathematical write-up about one problem and the work done on it.","reader_card":"It states the question, relevant definitions, established results, and remaining uncertainty, with references to supporting proof or calculation. It may be shorter than a full research paper while still needing precise claims and attribution.","reader_rule":"A note about an open problem is not necessarily a claimed solution.","reader_deep":"The notes here help readers distinguish original targets from reformulations, conditional results, and finite evidence. The accompanying source provides further detail where the short account leaves it out."},{"object_id":"term:producer","preferred_label":"producer","reader_preview":"A producer is the person, model, or program that generates an output.","reader_card":"In Plectis's verification descriptions, the output may be a proposed claim, certificate, proof, or artifact. A separate checking step decides whether it satisfies the relevant requirements.","reader_rule":"Acceptance should depend on the output and evidence rather than on confidence in the producer's presentation.","reader_deep":"In mathematical research plans, \u201cproducer\u201d can also name a missing construction that would supply a required object. Its exact target and logical role must then be stated in the surrounding argument."},{"object_id":"term:product","preferred_label":"product","reader_preview":"In mathematics, a product is the result of multiplication; in software discussions, it can mean an offering intended for users.","reader_card":"For example, 12 is the product of 3 and 4. The notation \u220f can indicate multiplication over a family of factors, just as \u2211 indicates addition over terms.","reader_rule":"The surrounding subject distinguishes the arithmetic meaning from the software meaning.","reader_deep":"For an infinite product, convergence needs a definition and proof appropriate to that setting. A long finite multiplication does not automatically determine the behaviour of the infinite expression."},{"object_id":"term:profile_gap","preferred_label":"profile gap","reader_preview":"A profile gap marks missing support for presenting a kind of system object at the promised levels of detail.","reader_card":"The underlying files or registry may exist while the navigation lacks reliable summaries, cards, or item-level routes. Plectis records the gap so an overview can acknowledge the collection without pretending its browsing support is complete.","reader_rule":"Existing source and complete navigation are separate states.","reader_deep":"Repair should connect the artifact kind to a suitable profile and builder, then verify that the resulting rows preserve identity, meaning, and access to the relevant source."},{"object_id":"term:program","preferred_label":"program","reader_preview":"A computer program is a set of instructions that a computing system can execute.","reader_card":"It can transform inputs, control a process, or provide an interactive service. In other contexts, \u201cprogramme\u201d can mean an organised initiative or course of work; the site uses that ordinary sense too.","reader_rule":"An executable program's behaviour depends on its code, inputs, and environment.","reader_deep":"Source code is available for inspection, while a recorded run supplies evidence about a particular execution. Neither the name of a program nor its successful launch establishes every claim about its behaviour."},{"object_id":"term:projection","preferred_label":"projection","reader_preview":"In Plectis, a projection is a view derived from source material for reading, querying, or navigation.","reader_card":"A builder may select, reorganise, or summarise records into a page, index, or card. The projection should retain the identities and source routes needed to inspect its claims.","reader_rule":"The source remains authoritative for the facts the projection represents.","reader_deep":"A projection can become stale when its inputs change. Its source version and refresh process therefore matter alongside the quality of its presentation. Mathematical uses of \u201cprojection\u201d have separate definitions in their own settings."},{"object_id":"term:projection_drift","preferred_label":"projection drift","reader_preview":"Projection drift is a mismatch between a generated view and the source it is meant to represent.","reader_card":"It can arise when the source changes without a rebuild, or when the builder transforms it incorrectly. A page may then display an outdated count, missing item, or inaccurate summary.","reader_rule":"The relevant source and generation rules determine what agreement should mean.","reader_deep":"Rebuilding fixes some forms of drift, but a faulty transformation needs repair in the builder. Plectis records the comparison so the mismatch can lead to an appropriate correction rather than another unsupported status label."},{"object_id":"term:proof","preferred_label":"proof","reader_preview":"A proof is a logically valid argument establishing a statement from definitions, assumptions, and previously justified results.","reader_card":"It must cover the statement's full scope, including every case quantified by the claim. Examples and calculations can be parts of a proof when their role and coverage are justified.","reader_rule":"A proof of an implication establishes the conclusion under its hypotheses.","reader_deep":"Formal proof assistants check an encoded derivation, while readers also assess whether the encoded statement matches the intended mathematics. Exposition explains the main reasoning without replacing any of the obligations that make the argument valid."},{"object_id":"term:proof_assistant","preferred_label":"proof assistant","reader_preview":"A proof assistant is software for constructing and checking arguments in a formal logical system.","reader_card":"It provides a language for definitions and statements, tools for developing proofs, and a checking mechanism. Lean is the proof assistant used for the formal mathematics linked from this site.","reader_rule":"The assistant checks what is formally stated, including the assumptions and dependencies used.","reader_deep":"It does not choose the research question or automatically assess significance. Authors remain responsible for faithful formalisation, readable explanation, attribution, and identifying any gaps or extra assumptions in the proof."},{"object_id":"term:proof_boundary","preferred_label":"proof boundary","reader_preview":"A proof boundary specifies exactly what a proof establishes and which surrounding claims need additional justification.","reader_card":"For formal work, it is determined by the encoded theorem, its definitions, and its assumptions. A paper or README can accidentally describe something broader than that statement.","reader_rule":"Compare the informal claim with the formal statement rather than relying on the presence of a checked proof.","reader_deep":"For example, a theorem about a bounded range does not prove the same assertion for every integer. Making that boundary explicit lets useful partial results be understood without overstating them."},{"object_id":"term:proof_checker","preferred_label":"proof checker","reader_preview":"A proof checker verifies that a supplied formal proof obeys a logical system's rules and establishes the claimed statement.","reader_card":"It is concerned with checking the proof object, which may have been constructed by a person, tactic, search procedure, or other software. The rules and accepted assumptions determine the result's logical basis.","reader_rule":"Proof search and proof checking are different tasks.","reader_deep":"A correct checker can still accept a proof of a statement that was translated incorrectly from an informal problem. Reviewing that translation and explaining the theorem's significance remain mathematical tasks outside the checking operation."},{"object_id":"term:proposition","preferred_label":"proposition","reader_preview":"A proposition is a statement that can be asserted or denied once its terms and parameters are specified.","reader_card":"For example, \u201cn is prime\u201d becomes a particular proposition when n is fixed. In Lean, propositions have proofs as their inhabitants; constructing an appropriate proof establishes the proposition in the formal system.","reader_rule":"Calling a statement a proposition does not mean it has been proved.","reader_deep":"Papers also use \u201cProposition\u201d as a label for a proved result, often between a lemma and a theorem in presentation. The label describes its expository role rather than a different logical standard."},{"object_id":"term:provenance","preferred_label":"provenance","reader_preview":"Provenance records where an item came from and the steps or sources that produced it.","reader_card":"It can identify a source repository, revision, original author, transformation, or recorded run. Plectis uses these connections to make copied and generated material traceable.","reader_rule":"Provenance establishes origin and process, not correctness by itself.","reader_deep":"A current matching check may also be needed to show that a copy still agrees with its source. Retaining both the origin and the comparison evidence helps distinguish an authentic historical copy from an up-to-date projection."},{"object_id":"term:pytest","preferred_label":"pytest","reader_preview":"pytest is a framework for writing and running automated tests in Python.","reader_card":"It discovers tests, provides fixtures and assertions, and reports which tests pass or fail. Projects can also use plugins to extend its behaviour.","reader_rule":"A passing run concerns the selected tests in the environment and source version used.","reader_deep":"Plectis links runnable checks and result records so readers can inspect what was tested. A test suite may miss behaviours outside its coverage, so its pass should support specific claims rather than be treated as proof of complete correctness."},{"object_id":"term:queryable","preferred_label":"queryable","reader_preview":"Information is queryable when a reader or program can retrieve relevant items by asking a structured question or searching.","reader_card":"Plectis uses identifiers, fields, indexes, and relationships so material can be found without already knowing its exact file path. A query may return a summary with a route to fuller evidence.","reader_rule":"The query's coverage and matching rules affect what its results mean.","reader_deep":"No result can mean \u201cnot present in this indexed collection\u201d rather than \u201cdoes not exist\u201d. Source routes and clear coverage help a reader decide when a broader search is needed."},{"object_id":"term:quickstart","preferred_label":"quickstart","reader_preview":"A quickstart gives the shortest supported sequence of steps to obtain a first useful result.","reader_card":"For a repository, that usually means getting the source, installing the declared dependencies, running an example or check, and inspecting its output. It should state any prerequisites needed for those steps.","reader_rule":"The example establishes an initial working route, not mastery of the whole project.","reader_deep":"A good quickstart leads onward to the explanation and reference material needed for further work. Its commands should be tested from a clean environment so hidden local setup does not become the reader's problem."},{"object_id":"term:radix","preferred_label":"radix","reader_preview":"Radix is another word for the base of a positional numeral system.","reader_card":"Decimal has radix ten and binary has radix two. In radix b, successive places have weights that are powers of b; the radix point separates whole-number places from fractional places.","reader_rule":"The familiar decimal point is the radix point in base ten.","reader_deep":"For integer bases greater than one, rational numbers have eventually periodic expansions. The value is independent of its chosen radix, although its digits and period can change substantially when written in another base."},{"object_id":"term:rational","preferred_label":"rational","reader_preview":"A rational number is a number equal to a/b for integers a and nonzero b.","reader_card":"Examples include 3/4, \u22122, and 0. Fractions differing by a common nonzero factor can represent the same rational number; a positive denominator and lowest terms provide a standard representation.","reader_rule":"In any integer base greater than one, rational numbers have terminating or eventually repeating digit expansions.","reader_deep":"An infinite series of rational terms can have a rational or irrational sum. The type of each term alone does not determine the type of the limit."},{"object_id":"term:rational_approximation","preferred_label":"rational approximation","reader_preview":"Rational approximation studies how closely fractions can approach a number, especially in relation to their denominators.","reader_card":"For example, 22/7 approximates \u03c0 with a small error, but the denominator 7 matters when comparing its quality with other approximations. A useful estimate states both the error and how it depends on q in p/q.","reader_rule":"A small decimal error alone does not establish irrationality.","reader_deep":"Arguments in these papers combine structured approximations with denominator restrictions or tail bounds. The theorem must explain why their combined behaviour cannot occur if the target number is rational."},{"object_id":"term:raw_seed","preferred_label":"raw seed","reader_preview":"Raw seed is Plectis's preserved, append-only record of the operator's original words and developing ideas.","reader_card":"Later summaries, interpretations, and rules can refer back to it, while the original material remains available for checking intent and context. Corrections or changes of mind are preserved as further input rather than silently replacing the earlier record.","reader_rule":"Agent-authored synthesis belongs in a separate attributed record.","reader_deep":"Raw seed is authoritative about what the operator said. Claims about software behaviour, mathematics, or external facts still require the appropriate implementation or factual evidence rather than authority from a quotation alone."},{"object_id":"term:reader","preferred_label":"reader","reader_preview":"A reader is the person or software consuming a document or other published material.","reader_card":"The reader may be new to the subject, seeking one fact, reviewing a proof, or preparing to run a check. Different purposes call for different amounts of context and detail.","reader_rule":"Plectis's summaries and source routes should make those different reading paths possible.","reader_deep":"Defining specialist terms helps a non-specialist proceed without interrupting an expert. The surrounding explanation should still be understandable enough that a glossary is optional support rather than a prerequisite for every sentence."},{"object_id":"term:reader_packet","preferred_label":"reader packet","reader_preview":"A reader packet gathers selected explanations, links, source references, and scope information for someone examining the project.","reader_card":"It can be downloaded or supplied to an assistant as a portable starting point. The selection helps the reader orient and identify which original material deserves closer attention.","reader_rule":"The packet is a generated guide with declared coverage, not a replacement for all its sources.","reader_deep":"Its version matters when the project changes. A useful packet preserves routes to current source and evidence while identifying which summaries or copied contents belong to the packet's own snapshot."},{"object_id":"term:real_number","preferred_label":"real number","reader_preview":"A real number is a point on the real number line, including rational and irrational values.","reader_card":"Integers, fractions, \u221a2, and \u03c0 are examples. The real numbers provide a setting for limits, lengths, and continuous quantities; they are commonly denoted by \u211d.","reader_rule":"Real does not mean that a number has a finite decimal representation or can be computed exactly by a program.","reader_deep":"In a paper, a real-valued series can converge without its sum being rational. Establishing the existence of the sum and classifying that value are separate questions."},{"object_id":"term:receipt","preferred_label":"receipt","reader_preview":"A receipt is a saved record of an action and its reported outcome.","reader_card":"Plectis uses receipts to identify what ran or changed, the relevant inputs, and the checks performed. The record gives later readers something concrete to compare with a completion claim.","reader_rule":"The strength of the evidence depends on how the receipt was produced and what it captures.","reader_deep":"A receipt is not automatically proof that every reported fact is true. Source versions, execution traces, and independent checks can help establish whether it accurately describes the action."},{"object_id":"term:reciprocal","preferred_label":"reciprocal","reader_preview":"The reciprocal of a nonzero number x is 1/x.","reader_card":"Multiplying a number by its reciprocal gives 1. For example, the reciprocal of 4 is 1/4, and the reciprocal of 2/3 is 3/2.","reader_rule":"Zero has no reciprocal in the real or rational numbers.","reader_deep":"The papers study sums of reciprocals of structured integer sequences. The denominator's growth and arithmetic properties both matter: shrinking terms alone do not prove that a reciprocal series converges or determine whether its sum is rational."},{"object_id":"term:reciprocal_series","preferred_label":"reciprocal series","reader_preview":"A reciprocal series is a sum whose terms have the form 1/a\u2099 for specified nonzero numbers a\u2099.","reader_card":"For example, \u2211 1/2\u207f starting at n = 1 sums to 1, while \u2211 1/n diverges. The way the denominators grow influences convergence, but a precise comparison or other argument is needed.","reader_rule":"Convergence does not determine whether the sum is rational or irrational.","reader_deep":"The papers study denominators built from factorials, primes, or powers minus one. Their divisibility structure can constrain cancellation and rationality even when convergence follows from a simple size estimate."},{"object_id":"term:record","preferred_label":"record","reader_preview":"A record is stored information about an object, event, decision, or result.","reader_card":"In Plectis it may be a structured row, file, or packet that can be reopened after a session ends. Its fields should make clear what it describes and where the information came from.","reader_rule":"Different records have different authority and evidence roles.","reader_deep":"A result record may describe one test run, while a registry record defines an object's identity. Keeping those roles clear prevents a summary of an action from being confused with the source or execution evidence behind it."},{"object_id":"term:recurrence","preferred_label":"recurrence","reader_preview":"A recurrence defines terms of a sequence through a relation involving other terms, usually earlier ones.","reader_card":"Initial values together with the rule determine the sequence when the recurrence is well-posed. For example, a\u2080 = 1 and a\u2099\u208a\u2081 = 2a\u2099 produce 1, 2, 4, 8, \u2026 .","reader_rule":"The initial conditions are part of the definition.","reader_deep":"The same recurrence can produce different sequences from different starting values. A direct formula may be proved equivalent to the recurrence, making it easier to establish growth, divisibility, or periodicity."},{"object_id":"term:reduction","preferred_label":"reduction","reader_preview":"A reduction shows how solving one problem would allow another problem to be solved.","reader_card":"If the target is P and the proposed replacement is Q, proving Q \u2192 P gives a route from a solution of Q to a solution of P. It can reveal a useful sufficient condition.","reader_rule":"That direction alone does not show that P and Q are equivalent.","reader_deep":"The replacement may be harder, stronger, or even impossible under its own conditions. The papers should state the direction and any reverse implication before treating a reduction as the same unresolved problem."},{"object_id":"term:reference","preferred_label":"reference","reader_preview":"A reference identifies another source, object, or location relevant to the current material.","reader_card":"It can be a citation, link, stable identifier, file path, or declaration name. Plectis uses references to connect summaries with the material needed to inspect them.","reader_rule":"A useful reference identifies both the destination and why it is relevant.","reader_deep":"Its presence alone does not show that the destination supports the claim. Readers may need a particular section, theorem, version, or execution record to check the precise relationship being asserted."},{"object_id":"term:reformulation","preferred_label":"reformulation","reader_preview":"A reformulation expresses a question or statement in a different way, ideally making a useful aspect of its structure clearer.","reader_card":"When two formulations are claimed to be equivalent, a proof must show that each implies the other under the stated assumptions. A one-way relationship instead gives a sufficient or necessary condition.","reader_rule":"Changing notation or coordinates does not by itself resolve an open question.","reader_deep":"The papers use reformulations to expose exact obligations and compare attempted approaches. The relationship to the original target should remain visible so a reader can tell what proving the new statement would accomplish."},{"object_id":"term:registry","preferred_label":"registry","reader_preview":"A registry is a structured collection of identified records about a particular kind of object.","reader_card":"It provides stable names and fields that tools can query, validate, and use to build other views. Plectis has registries for objects such as terms, components, and claims.","reader_rule":"A registry's schema and ownership rules determine what its entries mean.","reader_deep":"Registration can establish that an item has been recorded without establishing that it is complete, approved, or correct. Those statuses require explicit fields and the appropriate evidence or decision process."},{"object_id":"term:rename_migration_receipt","preferred_label":"rename migration receipt","reader_preview":"A rename migration receipt records the plan, outcome, and checks for moving or renaming stored material.","reader_card":"It identifies the affected paths and the references that may need updating, together with any rollback or remaining obligations. Plectis uses it to keep a migration understandable across sessions.","reader_rule":"The receipt's status distinguishes a planned or blocked change from one that completed.","reader_deep":"A completed record should be backed by the relevant checks. Recording success alone does not guarantee that every reference was found, so the tested scope and any omissions should remain explicit."},{"object_id":"term:replay","preferred_label":"replay","reader_preview":"A replay repeats a recorded scenario to examine whether it produces the expected behaviour.","reader_card":"Plectis replays provide defined inputs, actions, and expected outcomes for a component or workflow. They can exercise interactions across several steps rather than only a single isolated function.","reader_rule":"The scenario and environment determine what the replay covers.","reader_deep":"A matching result supports repeatability of that case. A replay may deliberately model only part of a live process, so its success should not be described as verification of unrepresented inputs or wider system behaviour."},{"object_id":"term:repository","preferred_label":"repository","reader_preview":"A repository stores a project's tracked files and, in Git, the history of their recorded revisions.","reader_card":"It can exist locally or be hosted for sharing. A working checkout exposes files for a selected revision, possibly with local edits that have not been committed.","reader_rule":"A repository need not be public, and a local clone may contain only part of its history or contents.","reader_deep":"The public links here identify source collections that readers can inspect or obtain. Reproducing a result requires the appropriate revision and setup, not just a repository with the same name."},{"object_id":"term:reproducible","preferred_label":"reproducible","reader_preview":"A result is reproducible when someone can use the stated materials and method to obtain the same relevant outcome.","reader_card":"For software checks, this usually requires a source version, inputs, dependencies, commands, and an account of expected outputs. Exact byte equality may be appropriate for some artifacts; other results need a stated tolerance or comparison rule.","reader_rule":"The reproduction claim should say which outcome is expected to agree.","reader_deep":"Randomness, external services, and environment differences can affect runs. Making those dependencies explicit helps a reader distinguish an actual discrepancy from an allowed variation."},{"object_id":"term:research_shift","preferred_label":"research shift","reader_preview":"A research shift is a bounded period of work on a specific, inspectable mathematical task.","reader_card":"The project's relay workflow starts from an identified public revision, selects a question, and returns a result with the source and checks needed for review. A person may use a coding agent to carry out parts of that work.","reader_rule":"The return should distinguish a proof, a failed route, an experiment, and an unresolved proposal.","reader_deep":"Attribution and exact starting state make the contribution easier to assess and combine with other work. Completing the shift does not imply solving the whole problem."},{"object_id":"term:residue_class","preferred_label":"residue class","reader_preview":"A residue class modulo m consists of all integers congruent to one another modulo m.","reader_card":"For example, the class of 2 modulo 5 contains \u2026, \u22128, \u22123, 2, 7, 12, \u2026 . Any of these integers can represent the class, while 2 is its usual least nonnegative representative.","reader_rule":"The modulus is part of the class's identity.","reader_deep":"The papers use classes to describe repeated arithmetic conditions across infinitely many integers. Proving a statement for a representative requires showing that the property is preserved for the other members of its class."},{"object_id":"term:result_record","preferred_label":"result record","reader_preview":"A result record stores what a particular check or run examined and what it reported.","reader_card":"It identifies the tested claim, relevant inputs or source version, outcome, and limits. Plectis keeps these records so an execution can be inspected after its temporary terminal output is gone.","reader_rule":"A record describes that run, not every later run of the same command.","reader_deep":"Its reliability depends on the recording process and links to evidence. If the source or inputs change, a new run may be needed before the earlier result can support a current claim."},{"object_id":"term:reviewer_brief","preferred_label":"reviewer brief","reader_preview":"The reviewer brief is a compact generated packet for someone assessing the public work.","reader_card":"It gathers the main orientation, claims, source routes, and stated limits so a reader or assistant can identify what deserves closer inspection. It is a starting point for review rather than a substitute for the papers and code.","reader_rule":"The brief's version determines which public state it describes.","reader_deep":"A useful brief separates evidence classes and open questions. That lets a reviewer choose an appropriate check instead of treating every item in the packet as equally established."},{"object_id":"term:rigidity","preferred_label":"rigidity","reader_preview":"A rigidity result shows that stated conditions leave an object unusually little freedom, often determining it up to a specified equivalence.","reader_card":"The conclusion may force one exact form, a small family, or a structure preserved under allowed transformations. The hypotheses explain which apparent alternatives have been excluded.","reader_rule":"Rigidity does not always mean literal uniqueness of one object.","reader_deep":"For example, a geometric result may determine a shape only up to rotation or scaling. Reading that qualification is essential before interpreting a classification as saying that there is exactly one possible instance."},{"object_id":"term:root_multiplicity","preferred_label":"root multiplicity","reader_preview":"The multiplicity of a root measures how many copies of its linear factor occur in a polynomial.","reader_card":"For a nonzero polynomial, a root a has multiplicity k when p(z) = (z \u2212 a)\u1d4fq(z) with q(a) \u2260 0. A simple root has multiplicity one; a multiple root has larger multiplicity.","reader_rule":"Counting with multiplicity differs from counting distinct locations.","reader_deep":"For example, (z \u2212 1)\u00b2(z + 2) has three roots counted with multiplicity but only two distinct roots. The distinction matters when a geometric argument chooses paths between root points. Repeated occurrences share one location and cannot be separated geometrically just by assigning them different labels."},{"object_id":"term:routing","preferred_label":"routing","reader_preview":"Routing chooses the next relevant destination or action from a question, input, or current state.","reader_card":"In Plectis, a route may lead to a source file, a navigation collection, a governing skill, or the workflow responsible for a change. Its purpose is to reduce guesswork before deeper inspection.","reader_rule":"The route must match the task's actual subject and authority requirements.","reader_deep":"A plausible keyword match can still lead to the wrong owner. A useful route therefore supplies enough identity and explanation for the reader to recognise and correct a mismatch."},{"object_id":"term:row_job","preferred_label":"row job","reader_preview":"A row job specifies one bounded operation on an identified record or small artifact selection.","reader_card":"It states the target, intended change, permitted scope, expected result, and required check before execution. Plectis uses this structure to turn a broad request into work that can be assigned and assessed.","reader_rule":"The job description is a plan; a separate result records what actually happened.","reader_deep":"Fixing the scope in advance makes it easier to detect unintended changes or unmet requirements. The worker still needs to inspect the target and stop if the assumed conditions do not hold."},{"object_id":"term:rowability","preferred_label":"rowability","reader_preview":"Rowability is Plectis's term for whether a collection can be reliably browsed as identifiable, source-linked entries.","reader_card":"Each item needs a stable identity, useful summary, route to detail, and appropriate status information. Merely having files somewhere in the repository does not supply that browsing interface.","reader_rule":"The collection should acknowledge missing or unsupported views.","reader_deep":"This property matters when an agent must choose among many artifacts without guessing their names. A rowable collection turns discovery into selecting known options while retaining access to the source needed for the chosen task."},{"object_id":"term:run","preferred_label":"run","reader_preview":"A run is one execution of a command, program, test, or experiment.","reader_card":"It has particular inputs, a source version, an environment, and an outcome. Plectis records those details when they are needed to support a claim or repeat the work.","reader_rule":"Two runs of the same command need not be identical if their conditions differ.","reader_deep":"A run starting successfully is also different from completing successfully. The final status and relevant outputs determine what happened, while a readable record lets later work assess it without relying on a session summary."},{"object_id":"term:runnable","preferred_label":"runnable","reader_preview":"Runnable means the material can be executed with the stated code, dependencies, inputs, and instructions.","reader_card":"For a public component, the reader should be able to obtain the required files and perform the advertised command in a supported environment. Missing private setup would limit that claim.","reader_rule":"Being runnable does not mean that the run succeeds or establishes the intended property.","reader_deep":"Execution results and checks provide the next layer of evidence. The published instructions should say where the output appears and what outcome counts as success for the example."},{"object_id":"term:running_lcm","preferred_label":"running least common multiple","reader_preview":"A running least common multiple takes the least common multiple of all terms up to the current position in a positive-integer sequence.","reader_card":"For 2, 3, 4, the running values are 2, 6, 12. Each new value is the least common multiple of the previous running value and the next term.","reader_rule":"It changes only when the next term supplies a prime factor at a higher exponent than was previously required.","reader_deep":"The Erd\u0151s #269 work studies reciprocal sums involving these cumulative values. Their behaviour depends on the specified input sequence, not merely on how many terms have been included."},{"object_id":"term:runtime","preferred_label":"runtime","reader_preview":"A runtime is the software support and execution context used while a program runs.","reader_card":"The word can refer to language machinery, loaded code, active processes, or the current state of a workflow. Plectis distinguishes this live behaviour from static source files and documentation about intended behaviour.","reader_rule":"The relevant environment and version determine which runtime is being described.","reader_deep":"Inspecting source can explain what a program is meant to do, while runtime records show what a particular execution did. Both may be needed to understand an error or validate a fix."},{"object_id":"term:saddle_connection","preferred_label":"saddle connection","reader_preview":"A saddle connection is a trajectory connecting saddle-type singular points, possibly returning to the same one.","reader_card":"The trajectory is regular away from its endpoints, while the endpoints are understood through limiting behaviour or a separately defined extension. Such a connection depends on the global flow, not just the local shape of each saddle.","reader_rule":"The vector field need not be defined at the endpoints under the same formula used between them.","reader_deep":"For Newton flow, polynomial values along a regular trajectory differ by positive real scale factors. This constrains which nonzero critical values can occur at its limiting endpoints."},{"object_id":"term:schema","preferred_label":"schema","reader_preview":"A schema defines the expected structure and constraints of data.","reader_card":"It can specify required fields, permitted types, allowed values, and relationships between fields. Plectis uses schemas so tools can validate structured records and interpret them consistently.","reader_rule":"Valid structure does not ensure that the recorded facts are true.","reader_deep":"For example, a result record can contain all required fields while reporting the wrong outcome. Schema validation should therefore be paired with checks appropriate to the contents and the claim for which the record is used."},{"object_id":"term:scope","preferred_label":"scope","reader_preview":"Scope states the cases, objects, actions, and conditions to which something applies.","reader_card":"For a theorem, scope comes from its quantified variables and hypotheses. For a test, it includes the inputs and behaviour examined. For a task, it includes the authorised changes and intended result.","reader_rule":"Evidence and permission should not silently extend beyond their stated scope.","reader_deep":"Plectis records these boundaries so partial work can be useful without appearing universal. A clear scope also makes it easier to identify which additional evidence or authorisation would support a broader action."},{"object_id":"term:scope_limit","preferred_label":"scope limit","reader_preview":"A scope limit states where a claim, permission, or piece of evidence stops applying.","reader_card":"For a component, it may restrict the supported inputs, checked behaviour, or conclusions allowed from a result. For mathematical work, it may identify a finite range or an unresolved hypothesis.","reader_rule":"The limit should name the actual boundary rather than offer a generic disclaimer.","reader_deep":"For example, a recorded test can establish that a specific forged note was rejected. That does not by itself establish that every forgery or every misleading report would be detected."},{"object_id":"term:semantic_naming","preferred_label":"semantic naming","reader_preview":"Semantic naming gives an identifier or label enough meaning to help a reader recognise the object's role.","reader_card":"Plectis distinguishes stable identity, storage path, display label, and aliases so those functions can be maintained separately. A useful name can indicate a component's job without forcing the reader to open it first.","reader_rule":"A name suggests meaning; the source record establishes the full role, authority, and current status.","reader_deep":"Encoding changeable status into a permanent name can make it misleading. Explicit fields and detail routes are often better places for information that needs to change over time."},{"object_id":"term:separatrix","preferred_label":"separatrix","reader_preview":"A separatrix is a distinguished trajectory, or invariant boundary, separating regions with different dynamical behaviour.","reader_card":"Near a planar saddle, special incoming and outgoing trajectories divide families of nearby trajectories. They help describe which starting points can approach which limiting objects.","reader_rule":"The precise definition depends on the dynamical system and the role of its singular points.","reader_deep":"In the Newton-flow material, these paths are used to organise regions around roots and critical points. Their local directions do not alone prove the existence or shape of a global connection between two distant endpoints."},{"object_id":"term:sequence","preferred_label":"sequence","reader_preview":"A sequence is an indexed family of terms, usually ordered by natural-number positions.","reader_card":"For example, a\u2099 = 1/n for n \u2265 1 gives 1, 1/2, 1/3, \u2026 . The index identifies each position, and repeated values are allowed.","reader_rule":"The starting index and formula or recurrence are part of the definition.","reader_deep":"A sequence lists terms; a series adds them. That distinction matters because a sequence tending to zero can still have a divergent sum, as happens with the harmonic sequence and harmonic series."},{"object_id":"term:series","preferred_label":"series","reader_preview":"A mathematical series is a sum of terms from a sequence, often extending over infinitely many indices.","reader_card":"The expression \u2211 a\u2099 is understood through its finite partial sums. When those partial sums approach a finite limit, the series converges and that limit is its sum.","reader_rule":"A time series is a different use: a sequence of observations indexed by time, not necessarily added together.","reader_deep":"The papers specify their summation indices and convergence conditions. Omitting the initial index or changing the order can matter, especially when terms are undefined or convergence is not absolute."},{"object_id":"term:series_tail","preferred_label":"series tail","reader_preview":"The tail of a series is the part remaining after a chosen finite number of terms.","reader_card":"For \u2211 a\u2099 starting at 1, the tail after N is \u2211 over n > N of a\u2099. When the series converges, this equals the full sum minus its Nth partial sum.","reader_rule":"A convergent series has tails tending to zero, although multiplying them by a growing factor may change that behaviour.","reader_deep":"The papers often study scaled tails to expose integer or divisibility conditions. Those arithmetic properties require more than the fact that the unscaled tail becomes small."},{"object_id":"term:set","preferred_label":"set","reader_preview":"A set is a collection determined by which elements belong to it.","reader_card":"Order and repetition do not change a set: {1, 2} and {2, 1, 1} describe the same set. The notation x \u2208 A says that x belongs to A.","reader_rule":"A sequence or multiset keeps information that an ordinary set discards.","reader_deep":"This distinction matters in the papers when selecting exponents or counting roots. A polynomial's roots as a set ignore repeated occurrences, while roots counted with multiplicity carry additional information."},{"object_id":"term:shared_path","preferred_label":"shared path","reader_preview":"The shared path is the common way Plectis presents a component's claim, check, recorded result, and limits.","reader_card":"Different components have different jobs, but readers can examine them through the same sequence: understand the assertion, inspect the test and inputs, read the output, and identify its scope.","reader_rule":"The common presentation makes comparisons easier without making the components interchangeable.","reader_deep":"Each component still needs evidence appropriate to its own mechanism. A shared card layout cannot substitute for a missing check or make one component inherit another's verified behaviour."},{"object_id":"term:simple_critical_point","preferred_label":"simple critical point","reader_preview":"A simple critical point of a complex polynomial p is a simple root of its derivative: p\u2032(c) = 0 and p\u2033(c) \u2260 0.","reader_card":"Near such a point, the first nonconstant term in the polynomial's local expansion is quadratic. This gives a specific local branching structure that differs from a higher-multiplicity critical point.","reader_rule":"When p(c) is nonzero, the associated modulus geometry has the ordinary nondegenerate saddle form.","reader_deep":"If p(c) = 0, the modulus instead vanishes there, so it should not be described by that same saddle claim. The papers' extra hypotheses distinguish these cases."},{"object_id":"term:skill","preferred_label":"skill","reader_preview":"In this system, a skill is a reusable set of instructions for carrying out a recognisable kind of task.","reader_card":"It states when it applies, the steps to follow, relevant constraints, and routes to governing material. Agents can discover and reuse it instead of reconstructing the workflow from scratch.","reader_rule":"A skill is procedural guidance; a component is implemented machinery that may carry out part of it.","reader_deep":"The skill should stay aligned with the tools and rules it names. An instruction pointing to an obsolete command needs repair even if its general advice remains sound."},{"object_id":"term:slice","preferred_label":"slice","reader_preview":"A slice is a selected part of a larger collection or system.","reader_card":"Plectis uses the word for bounded sets of files, records, or components included in a task or public release. Naming the selection makes it clear what can be inspected or changed.","reader_rule":"The selection's boundary should be explicit rather than inferred from its title.","reader_deep":"A public slice can be independently runnable while the wider system remains unpublished. Evidence about that slice applies to its included material and tested interactions, with any broader conclusions requiring additional support."},{"object_id":"term:smoke_check","preferred_label":"smoke check","reader_preview":"A smoke check is a quick test for major failures in basic operation.","reader_card":"It might confirm that an application starts, an example command runs, or a key page loads. The aim is to catch obvious breakage before investing in more detailed checks.","reader_rule":"A smoke check deliberately has limited coverage.","reader_deep":"Its success is a useful first signal, not a comprehensive assessment of correctness. Plectis should identify the basic behaviour exercised so readers can tell which further checks are needed for a particular change."},{"object_id":"term:smooth_number","preferred_label":"smooth number","reader_preview":"A positive integer is y-smooth if none of its prime factors exceeds y.","reader_card":"More generally, it is smooth over a specified set of primes if all its prime factors belong to that set. For example, 72 = 2\u00b3\u00b73\u00b2 is 3-smooth, while 70 has prime factors 2, 5, and 7.","reader_rule":"The bound or allowed prime set is part of the definition.","reader_deep":"The number 1 qualifies because it has no prime factors. The papers use smoothness to restrict which integers enter a sequence or reciprocal sum, often producing a structured but sparse collection."},{"object_id":"term:software","preferred_label":"software","reader_preview":"Software consists of programs and related data used by computers to perform tasks.","reader_card":"On this site, the software section presents Plectis's reusable research components and the machinery supporting its workflow. It is distinguished from the mathematical reading routes, although formal proofs also use software tools.","reader_rule":"Each published component needs its own description, dependencies, and evidence.","reader_deep":"A repository can make implementation available without demonstrating every advertised use. The source, runnable example, and recorded check together help a reader assess the particular behaviour being claimed."},{"object_id":"term:source","preferred_label":"source","reader_preview":"A source is the original or controlling material from which a particular claim or artifact is drawn.","reader_card":"Here it often means code, a structured record, a theorem declaration, or a document linked from a generated page. Which source matters depends on the question being asked.","reader_rule":"Source for implementation and source for human intent need not be the same material.","reader_deep":"A reliable explanation identifies the relevant source precisely, including a version where necessary. Opening it lets a reader check the detail that a summary may simplify or omit."},{"object_id":"term:source_authority","preferred_label":"source authority","reader_preview":"Source authority identifies which record or implementation controls a particular fact when other views disagree.","reader_card":"For example, a registry may define an object's identity, while an execution record describes a run and source code defines an implementation. A generated page presents selected information from those sources.","reader_rule":"Authority is specific to the question; one artifact does not settle every kind of claim.","reader_deep":"Plectis preserves these distinctions so a convenient summary cannot silently override its inputs. Resolving a mismatch requires identifying the relevant owner and checking what the source actually establishes."},{"object_id":"term:source_body","preferred_label":"source body","reader_preview":"A source body is the actual content of the code or document being referenced.","reader_card":"In a component bundle, it can be the full function or module implementation, as distinct from its signature, description, path, or digest. Including the body makes direct inspection possible.","reader_rule":"The body needs an identifiable version and context to explain which material it represents.","reader_deep":"A function may also depend on imports, configuration, or other files outside its displayed body. The bundle should make those dependencies clear if readers are expected to reproduce its behaviour."},{"object_id":"term:source_file","preferred_label":"source file","reader_preview":"A source file contains material from which another artifact is built or defined.","reader_card":"Source files can be written by people or generated by tools. Their role as source is relative to the build or transformation being discussed.","reader_rule":"Being a source file does not automatically make it the editable authority for every workflow.","reader_deep":"Plectis identifies the owning source and builder so generated material can be refreshed correctly. Editing a generated source copy may be overwritten by the next update unless its upstream owner is changed."},{"object_id":"term:source_slice","preferred_label":"source slice","reader_preview":"A source slice is the explicitly selected source material included in a task, package, or public release.","reader_card":"It can contain files and dependencies needed to support particular pages, components, or results. Plectis publishes bounded slices so readers can inspect relevant machinery without receiving the entire private working repository.","reader_rule":"The inventory determines what the slice includes and excludes.","reader_deep":"Its claims should fit that selection. If an example relies on omitted material, the dependency must be disclosed or included before the example can be described as independently reproducible."},{"object_id":"term:span","preferred_label":"linear span","reader_preview":"In linear algebra, the span of a set of vectors is the set of all finite linear combinations of them.","reader_card":"A linear combination scales vectors by allowed scalars and adds the results. For example, two nonparallel vectors in the ordinary plane span the plane; one nonzero vector spans a line through the origin.","reader_rule":"A spanning family may contain redundant vectors and need not be a basis.","reader_deep":"The underlying field and vector space matter. Claims about dimension concern the amount of independent structure in the span, not merely how many vectors were listed."},{"object_id":"term:sparse_checkout","preferred_label":"sparse checkout","reader_preview":"A sparse checkout limits which tracked files Git places in the working directory.","reader_card":"It lets someone work on selected directories while the repository's history and object database may still cover a larger project. This can make a large checkout easier to navigate and use.","reader_rule":"Sparse checkout alone does not mean that only those files were downloaded.","reader_deep":"Reducing downloads is a separate capability of partial or filtered clones. The public instructions should distinguish those operations so readers understand which files are present locally and which data may still need to be fetched."},{"object_id":"term:specification","preferred_label":"specification","reader_preview":"A specification states the requirements an implementation or result is expected to satisfy.","reader_card":"It can define behaviour, data structure, interfaces, inputs, outputs, or permitted assumptions. A test or formal verification then checks some relationship between the implementation and those requirements.","reader_rule":"The specification must itself express the intended task accurately.","reader_deep":"A perfectly checked implementation of the wrong requirement can still fail the user's goal. Plectis therefore links requirements, source, and evidence so reviewers can examine both the check and the meaning of what it checks."},{"object_id":"term:squarefree","preferred_label":"squarefree","reader_preview":"A positive integer is squarefree if no square of a prime divides it.","reader_card":"Equivalently, every prime in its factorisation occurs with exponent at most one. For example, 30 = 2\u00b73\u00b75 is squarefree, while 12 = 2\u00b2\u00b73 is not.","reader_rule":"The number 1 is squarefree because it has no prime factors.","reader_deep":"Squarefree is different from prime: a squarefree integer can have several distinct prime factors. The property is useful when a divisibility or counting argument depends on the presence of prime factors but excludes their repetition."},{"object_id":"term:standard","preferred_label":"standard","reader_preview":"A standard specifies requirements that items or processes are expected to meet.","reader_card":"Plectis stores standards as structured contracts covering fields, meanings, authority, validation, and maintenance. They give people and tools a common basis for writing and checking particular artifact kinds.","reader_rule":"A standard states the requirement; evidence shows whether an item satisfies it.","reader_deep":"The applicable version matters when rules change. A record valid under an earlier contract may need migration or a documented compatibility rule before it can be treated as valid under the current one."},{"object_id":"term:statement_node","preferred_label":"statement node","reader_preview":"A statement node is one identified mathematical statement in a graph of results and their relationships.","reader_card":"The graph can connect it to formal declarations, evidence, implications, and open obligations. Several declarations may support the same mathematical statement when they express equivalent versions or serve different implementation roles.","reader_rule":"Grouping statements requires a justified correspondence, not just similar wording.","reader_deep":"Plectis uses this distinction to make a large proof corpus easier to interpret. The number of declaration names need not equal the number of distinct claims or research ideas represented in the graph."},{"object_id":"term:stationary_bootstrap","preferred_label":"stationary bootstrap","reader_preview":"The stationary bootstrap resamples a time series in blocks of randomly chosen lengths to retain some of its temporal dependence.","reader_card":"In the standard construction, block lengths have a geometric distribution and starting points are sampled from the observed series, with circular continuation at its end. Joining such blocks creates a resampled series.","reader_rule":"Block length choices and assumptions about dependence affect the method's validity.","reader_deep":"It preserves local stretches of observations rather than exactly preserving every dependence in the original data. Statistical conclusions still require the sampling assumptions appropriate to the analysis."},{"object_id":"term:stub","preferred_label":"stub","reader_preview":"A stub is a placeholder representing an item or interface whose full contents or behaviour are not yet supplied.","reader_card":"It may allow navigation, compilation, or planning to proceed while showing where further work belongs. In testing, a stub can provide a simplified replacement for a dependency.","reader_rule":"The placeholder's limitations should be explicit wherever its output is used.","reader_deep":"A stub appearing in a catalog does not establish that the intended mechanism exists. Replacing it with an implementation requires the same source, interface, and validation checks as other substantive work."},{"object_id":"term:sublevel_set","preferred_label":"sublevel set","reader_preview":"A sublevel set consists of points where a real-valued function is below, or at most, a specified threshold.","reader_card":"The strict version is {x : g(x) < c}; the non-strict version is {x : g(x) \u2264 c}. For a continuous function, these give an open and a closed set respectively.","reader_rule":"Whether equality is allowed matters for paths and boundary points.","reader_deep":"The Erd\u0151s #1041 material studies paths in the open region |p(z)| < 1. A path that only remains in the closed region |p(z)| \u2264 1 does not automatically meet that requirement."},{"object_id":"term:subseries","preferred_label":"subseries","reader_preview":"A subseries is formed by selecting terms from a series and keeping them in their original order.","reader_card":"For example, retaining only the even-indexed terms of \u2211 a\u2099 gives a\u2082 + a\u2084 + a\u2086 + \u22ef . Its value, when defined, is a subsum of the original series.","reader_rule":"Selection differs from rearrangement: a rearrangement keeps all terms but changes their order.","reader_deep":"The Erd\u0151s #257 work asks about sums selected by infinite sets of exponents. The arithmetic pattern of the selected indices matters even when every such subseries converges."},{"object_id":"term:substrate","preferred_label":"substrate","reader_preview":"Here, substrate means the underlying files, stored state, and tools on which the system operates.","reader_card":"It includes the concrete material an agent can inspect or change, beneath the pages and summaries that describe it. Plectis uses the term when distinguishing direct access to working material from access to a copied explanation.","reader_rule":"The source's role determines what it can authoritatively establish.","reader_deep":"A webpage can accurately describe a file without granting access to that file or permission to change it. Type A and Type B describe this access distinction in the project's actor vocabulary."},{"object_id":"term:subsum","preferred_label":"subsum","reader_preview":"A subsum is a sum obtained by retaining selected terms of a series and omitting the others.","reader_card":"If A is the selected index set, the expression is \u2211 over n in A of a\u2099. The resulting sequence of retained terms forms a subseries, whose sum must be defined when the selection is infinite.","reader_rule":"Convergence of the original series alone need not guarantee convergence of every subseries.","reader_deep":"For absolutely convergent series, every subseries converges. For nonnegative convergent series, subsums lie between zero and the full sum; their possible values can form an interesting set."},{"object_id":"term:sum","preferred_label":"sum","reader_preview":"A sum is the result of adding quantities together.","reader_card":"A finite sum adds a specified number of terms. An infinite series uses the limit of finite partial sums when that limit exists. The symbol \u2211 abbreviates a sum with its index and range stated alongside it.","reader_rule":"The range and order of summation can be part of the mathematical claim.","reader_deep":"The papers distinguish exact finite arithmetic from conclusions about infinite sums. Passing from one to the other requires convergence and, when terms are rearranged, the appropriate justification for that rearrangement."},{"object_id":"term:summability","preferred_label":"summability","reader_preview":"Summability means that a family of terms has a sum under a specified definition of infinite summation.","reader_card":"For a nonnegative sequence, it means the partial sums are bounded and hence converge to a finite total. For real or complex families indexed without an order, the usual unconditional notion corresponds to absolute summability.","reader_rule":"The terms, weighting, and summation convention are part of the claim.","reader_deep":"In Lean, `Summable` has a formal meaning that should be checked for the relevant index and space. A convergent ordered series need not be summable in every stronger, order-independent sense."},{"object_id":"term:superior_predictive_ability","preferred_label":"superior predictive ability","reader_preview":"Hansen's superior predictive ability test assesses whether any candidate forecasting method has lower expected loss than a benchmark.","reader_card":"It compares a collection of methods jointly rather than treating the best observed performer as if it had been selected in advance. The procedure accounts for sampling variation and the comparison across candidates under its assumptions.","reader_rule":"The loss function, candidate set, dependence model, and resampling choices affect the inference.","reader_deep":"A rejection provides evidence against the benchmark's equal-or-better expected performance in the tested setting. It does not prove a particular method will outperform forever or explain why a difference occurs."},{"object_id":"term:support","preferred_label":"support","reader_preview":"Support can mean evidence for a claim or, in mathematics, the positions where a function or sequence is nonzero.","reader_card":"For a selected reciprocal sum, the papers also call the set of included indices its support. When terms are represented by zero-or-one selection coefficients, these two mathematical uses agree.","reader_rule":"The surrounding definition determines whether support refers to evidence, nonzero values, or selected indices.","reader_deep":"In analysis, the support of a function often means the closure of its nonzero set. That convention matters in topological settings and should not be silently substituted for an index set."},{"object_id":"term:sylvester_sequence","preferred_label":"Sylvester's sequence","reader_preview":"Sylvester's sequence starts with 2, and each later term is one plus the product of all preceding terms.","reader_card":"It begins 2, 3, 7, 43, 1807. With a\u2080 = 2, the same rule can be written a\u2099\u208a\u2081 = a\u2099(a\u2099 \u2212 1) + 1, so each next term can be calculated directly from the previous one.","reader_rule":"The starting value and indexing convention should be checked when comparing formulas.","reader_deep":"Its terms are pairwise coprime and grow rapidly. Reciprocal identities associated with the sequence make it useful in questions about unit fractions and the values of structured infinite sums."},{"object_id":"term:synth_seed","preferred_label":"synth seed","reader_preview":"A synth seed is a concise, replaceable brief for the current phase of work.","reader_card":"It gathers the goal, reasons for the work, success conditions, relevant sources, and next planned activity. Plectis derives it from durable intent and current state so an agent can start without rereading all earlier material.","reader_rule":"It describes the current phase rather than preserving the operator's original words.","reader_deep":"As the work changes, the brief can be replaced while the underlying source and historical records remain. Its usefulness depends on staying aligned with the live objective and evidence."},{"object_id":"term:synthetic","preferred_label":"synthetic","reader_preview":"Synthetic data is deliberately generated rather than directly observed from the real situation being studied.","reader_card":"It may model typical inputs, isolate an edge case, or contain a known fault. Plectis uses synthetic fixtures to make particular checks repeatable and their expected outcomes explicit.","reader_rule":"Generated data is useful when its relationship to the intended behaviour is clear.","reader_deep":"A successful synthetic test establishes what happened under the constructed conditions. Extending that result to real use requires evidence that the relevant conditions and failure mechanisms have been represented adequately."},{"object_id":"term:telescoping_identity","preferred_label":"telescoping identity","reader_preview":"A telescoping sum is one in which cancellations reduce a long sum to a few endpoint terms.","reader_card":"For example, (b\u2081 \u2212 b\u2082) + (b\u2082 \u2212 b\u2083) + \u22ef + (b_N \u2212 b_N\u208a\u2081) equals b\u2081 \u2212 b_N\u208a\u2081. The intermediate terms cancel in this finite identity.","reader_rule":"For an infinite series, the remaining endpoint term must have the appropriate limit.","reader_deep":"This method turns certain factorial fractions or recurrence differences into exact formulas. Writing down the finite cancellation first prevents an infinite argument from assuming, without proof, that its final boundary term disappears."},{"object_id":"term:terminal","preferred_label":"terminal","reader_preview":"A terminal provides a text interface for interacting with programs, commonly through a command-line shell.","reader_card":"It displays a prompt where commands can be entered and shows their output. Development workflows use it to run builds, tests, scripts, and version-control operations.","reader_rule":"The terminal is the interface; the shell interprets the command and starts the requested process.","reader_deep":"The site's runnable instructions use this route so readers can repeat named operations. A command's exit status and saved outputs help establish what happened beyond the text displayed while it ran."},{"object_id":"term:test","preferred_label":"test","reader_preview":"A test examines whether something behaves as expected under specified conditions.","reader_card":"Software tests supply inputs or setup, execute code, and compare the result with an assertion or other expectation. Plectis publishes tests alongside components to make the advertised behaviour inspectable.","reader_rule":"A passing test covers its particular conditions and assertions.","reader_deep":"Formal proof checking is a different form of verification and can establish a quantified statement under explicit assumptions. Both are valuable, but their guarantees should be described according to what the underlying method actually checks."},{"object_id":"term:theorem","preferred_label":"theorem","reader_preview":"A theorem is a mathematical statement established by proof within stated assumptions and definitions.","reader_card":"Its conclusion applies to every case covered by those assumptions. The word describes a proved result; importance, difficulty, and originality are separate questions.","reader_rule":"Read the theorem together with its hypotheses and the evidence for its proof.","reader_deep":"In Lean, a declaration labelled `theorem` can still depend on admissions or added axioms unless these are audited. The keyword alone is therefore insufficient evidence that the intended result has been fully proved on the permitted foundations."},{"object_id":"term:toolchain","preferred_label":"toolchain","reader_preview":"A toolchain is the collection of tools used to turn source material into a built, runnable, or checked result.","reader_card":"It may include a compiler, interpreter, package manager, document builder, and verifier. Their versions and configuration determine how the process behaves.","reader_rule":"Reproducing a result often requires the same supported toolchain or a demonstrably compatible one.","reader_deep":"The formal repositories identify Lean and dependency versions so proofs can be checked in the intended environment. A toolchain update can require source changes or a fresh validation even when the mathematical statement is unchanged."},{"object_id":"term:topological_closure","preferred_label":"closure of a set","reader_preview":"The smallest closed set containing a given set.","reader_card":"The closure of the open interval (0,1) is [0,1]: it adds the two boundary points approachable by sequences from inside. Points already in the set remain.","reader_rule":"A point lies in the closure exactly when every neighbourhood of it meets the original set. Closure is not the same as solving or completing an argument.","reader_deep":"Density and lower-semicontinuity arguments prove results first on generic polynomial configurations, then extend them to the closure containing limiting nongeneric cases."},{"object_id":"term:totient","preferred_label":"totient","reader_preview":"Euler's totient \u03c6(n) counts the integers from 1 through n that are coprime to the positive integer n.","reader_card":"For example, \u03c6(6) = 2 because only 1 and 5 are coprime to 6 in that range. The definition gives \u03c6(1) = 1; for n greater than 1, the qualifying integers are all strictly smaller than n.","reader_rule":"The input must be a positive integer in this definition.","reader_deep":"The papers use the totient in arithmetic series. Its values encode how prime factors restrict coprimality, linking multiplicative structure with questions about sums and digit expansions."},{"object_id":"term:transcendental","preferred_label":"transcendental","reader_preview":"A number is transcendental if it is not a root of any nonzero polynomial with integer coefficients.","reader_card":"Equivalently, it is not algebraic over the rational numbers. Familiar examples include \u03c0 and e. Every real transcendental number is irrational, but some irrational numbers, such as \u221a2, are algebraic.","reader_rule":"The polynomial must be nonzero: the zero polynomial vanishes at every number.","reader_deep":"Proving irrationality alone therefore does not establish transcendence. A transcendence argument must rule out every nonzero integer-coefficient polynomial relation, which is a stronger requirement than ruling out fractions."},{"object_id":"term:transcript","preferred_label":"transcript","reader_preview":"A transcript is a textual record of speech or an interaction.","reader_card":"It can preserve what was said, typed, or reported during a session, sometimes with timings and speaker labels. Plectis uses transcripts as inspectable records and source material for later explanation.","reader_rule":"The recording method determines what the transcript captures and where errors may occur.","reader_deep":"Speech recognition can mishear words, and an interaction transcript may omit events outside the captured channel. Important quotations or claims should therefore be checked against the appropriate original recording or execution evidence."},{"object_id":"term:truth_maintenance","preferred_label":"truth maintenance","reader_preview":"Truth maintenance tracks claims together with their reasons and dependencies so they can be reconsidered when evidence changes.","reader_card":"In AI and knowledge systems, this concerns managing beliefs and justifications. Plectis applies the idea to source records, checks, uncertainty, and decisions that depend on particular claims.","reader_rule":"Tracking a belief is not a guarantee that the belief is true.","reader_deep":"The useful behaviour is responding to a failed assumption or contradictory observation: identify affected conclusions, change their status, and review actions that relied on them rather than preserving an unsupported conclusion unchanged."},{"object_id":"term:trying","preferred_label":"trying","reader_preview":"Trying means attempting an action whose outcome is not yet established.","reader_card":"In research, an attempt can produce a proof, counterexample, useful failure, or no decisive result. Plectis keeps relevant evidence from attempts so later work can assess what was learned.","reader_rule":"The account should distinguish effort from the outcome actually obtained.","reader_deep":"A failed route is useful when its failure is explained precisely enough to guide another decision. Merely recording that an agent spent time on a task does not establish progress on the mathematical or software question."},{"object_id":"term:type_a","preferred_label":"Type A","reader_preview":"Type A names an actor with direct access to the working system and permission to perform specified operations on it.","reader_card":"Such an actor can inspect current files, run available tools, and make authorised changes. It must still respect the task's scope, ownership constraints, and validation requirements.","reader_rule":"The label describes access and authority, not intelligence or model quality.","reader_deep":"A delegated agent can be Type A if it has that live access. An actor with read or write tools elsewhere is not automatically Type A for this particular repository or system."},{"object_id":"term:type_a_judgment","preferred_label":"Type A judgment","reader_preview":"Type A judgement is the practical reasoning an agent uses when acting on the live system.","reader_card":"It includes noticing a missing connection, checking current evidence, choosing the appropriate owner, and deciding the smallest useful change within the task. Plectis expects the agent to use context rather than wait for every routine step to be prescribed.","reader_rule":"That discretion remains bounded by permissions, source authority, and consequences.","reader_deep":"For example, an agent can repair a directly affected instruction while preserving unrelated edits. A broader rule change needs evidence that the local lesson actually generalises."},{"object_id":"term:type_a_metabolism","preferred_label":"Type A metabolism","reader_preview":"Type A metabolism names the maintenance work that requires judgement about the system's structure or rules.","reader_card":"It addresses situations where an established routine is insufficient, such as resolving a contradiction, defining a missing object, or changing how a standard applies. The result should make later work more explicit and repeatable.","reader_rule":"This is a workflow category rather than a measure of an actor's intelligence.","reader_deep":"Because the work changes guidance that other actions rely on, its evidence, scope, and validation matter. A local ambiguity should lead to the narrowest justified structural refinement."},{"object_id":"term:type_a_operating_register","preferred_label":"Type A operating register","reader_preview":"The Type A operating register describes the conduct expected of an agent working directly on the system.","reader_card":"It includes understanding the user's aim, caring for the material touched, checking outcomes, preserving unrelated work, and recording useful lessons where future work will find them.","reader_rule":"\u201cRegister\u201d here means a style of working, not a database or processor register.","reader_deep":"The named traits, such as care and common sense, should produce observable decisions and follow-through. They do not replace explicit instructions, permissions, or evidence that a task has actually been completed."},{"object_id":"term:type_b","preferred_label":"Type B","reader_preview":"Type B names an actor reasoning from supplied or externally available context without direct authority over the working system.","reader_card":"It can analyse, research, critique, or draft candidate material, depending on its available tools. A Type A actor must perform any actions that require access to the live repository it cannot reach.","reader_rule":"The distinction concerns access to this system, not the actor's general abilities.","reader_deep":"A Type B actor may have web tools or its own execution environment. Those capabilities do not by themselves grant access to the private files or permission to change the system being discussed."},{"object_id":"term:type_b_metabolism","preferred_label":"Type B metabolism","reader_preview":"Type B metabolism names the routine upkeep of structures whose rules, inputs, and expected outputs have already been defined.","reader_card":"Examples include refreshing an index, filling a prescribed summary field, or checking that references still resolve. The process follows an established contract and records whether its result satisfies the required checks.","reader_rule":"This workflow label should be distinguished from the Type B actor-access label.","reader_deep":"Any actual write still requires an actor or tool authorised to make it. Repeated work can be bounded and checkable without every operation being deterministic or safe to repeat under changed inputs."},{"object_id":"term:typed_refusal","preferred_label":"typed refusal","reader_preview":"A typed refusal is a structured result saying that an operation could not proceed, with a specific reason.","reader_card":"It distinguishes cases such as missing evidence, unsupported input, failed validation, or timeout. Plectis uses these categories so later work can choose a suitable response instead of receiving a plausible-looking invented value.","reader_rule":"The refusal reports the operation's limitation; it need not refute the underlying claim.","reader_deep":"A timeout, for example, means the check did not finish within its budget. It is different from the checker finding a counterexample or completing with a failure verdict."},{"object_id":"term:typed_relation","preferred_label":"typed relation","reader_preview":"A typed relation is a connection that states how two identified items are related.","reader_card":"In a mathematical map, the type might be implication, equivalence, finite instance, or obstruction. In system records, it might identify implementation, ownership, or a source relationship.","reader_rule":"The relation type and direction are part of the information being recorded.","reader_deep":"Two statements merely sharing a topic do not establish an implication between them. Plectis's mathematical edges need supporting evidence so navigation does not silently promote an association into a logical result."},{"object_id":"term:unconditional_result","preferred_label":"unconditional result","reader_preview":"An unconditional result needs no additional unresolved hypothesis beyond its stated mathematical setting.","reader_card":"It still has definitions, domain restrictions, and logical foundations. The word distinguishes it from a result whose intended application depends on an open conjecture or other unestablished condition.","reader_rule":"Unconditional does not mean assumption-free or that the original research problem is fully solved.","reader_deep":"A proved equivalence or a theorem about a special case can be unconditional while the broader question remains open. The papers should say both what the result establishes and how it relates to the original target."},{"object_id":"term:uniform_convergence","preferred_label":"uniform convergence","reader_preview":"Functions converge uniformly when one sufficiently late stage makes the error small simultaneously at every point of the stated domain.","reader_card":"For every positive tolerance \u03b5, there must be an N such that |f\u2099(x) \u2212 f(x)| < \u03b5 for all n \u2265 N and all x in the domain. The same N works for every x.","reader_rule":"Pointwise convergence instead allows the threshold N to depend on x.","reader_deep":"Uniform convergence preserves continuity of continuous functions in the usual metric setting. Passing strict inequalities or derivative bounds to a limit needs additional care; uniform convergence alone does not preserve every property."},{"object_id":"term:uninterpretable","preferred_label":"uninterpretable","reader_preview":"Here, uninterpretable describes work for which readers do not yet have an adequate account of what it says or why it works.","reader_card":"A formal proof can check while its main idea, relationship to prior work, or intended meaning remains poorly explained. The missing task is to make that content understandable at an appropriate level of expertise.","reader_rule":"The term describes a current gap in understanding, not a proof that understanding is impossible.","reader_deep":"Useful exposition states the exact result, exposes the decisive argument, and preserves attribution and limits. Community understanding and use require further engagement beyond a machine's acceptance."},{"object_id":"term:unit_disc","preferred_label":"unit disc","reader_preview":"The open unit disc is the set of complex numbers z with |z| < 1.","reader_card":"It consists of points at distance less than one from the origin in the complex plane. The closed unit disc uses |z| \u2264 1 and includes its boundary, the unit circle |z| = 1.","reader_rule":"Open and closed versions differ precisely at that boundary.","reader_deep":"The lemniscate papers use conditions such as |p(z)| < 1. That says the polynomial's value lies in the open unit disc; it does not necessarily say the input point z lies there."},{"object_id":"term:up_propagate","preferred_label":"up-propagate","reader_preview":"To up-propagate is to place a reusable lesson in the shared source that should guide future relevant work.","reader_card":"Plectis uses the move when a local correction reveals a missing rule, instruction, or relationship. The lesson is expressed with its evidence and limits, then attached to an existing owner such as a skill or standard.","reader_rule":"The appropriate destination is the narrowest one that can carry the supported lesson.","reader_deep":"This prevents repeated rediscovery without converting every local fix into a system-wide rule. The changed source should be discoverable through the workflow that needs it."},{"object_id":"term:validator","preferred_label":"validator","reader_preview":"A validator checks whether supplied material satisfies a declared set of requirements.","reader_card":"It can examine a schema, input constraints, relationships, or other properties and report a pass, failure, warning, or inability to complete. Plectis connects these outcomes to the rules being applied.","reader_rule":"Validation is only as broad as those rules and the evidence examined.","reader_deep":"A file may be structurally valid while containing a false claim, and a blocked validation may simply lack a dependency. Clear result categories help distinguish these situations and identify the next appropriate action."},{"object_id":"term:variable","preferred_label":"variable","reader_preview":"A variable is a symbol or name whose value is supplied, quantified, or allowed to vary within a stated setting.","reader_card":"In mathematics, n may range over natural numbers while x ranges over real numbers. In a program, a variable names a value held or computed during execution.","reader_rule":"The name alone does not determine its meaning or permitted values.","reader_deep":"This matters when reading formulas across papers: the same letter can denote different objects in different sections. Use the local definition and hypotheses rather than assigning a universal meaning to a familiar symbol."},{"object_id":"term:vibe_mathing","preferred_label":"vibe-mathing","reader_preview":"\u201cVibe-mathing\u201d is this project's informal name for accepting AI-generated mathematical reasoning on plausibility without adequate independent checking.","reader_card":"Intuition and conversation can help generate useful ideas, but a fluent argument may hide an invalid step. The failure occurs when generating a candidate is treated as establishing it.","reader_rule":"A proof needs verification appropriate to its statement, whether formal or carefully examined mathematical reasoning.","reader_deep":"The project's response is to keep claims, evidence, explanations, and unresolved steps distinct. Formal checking helps with encoded inference; it does not replace understanding the intended result or explaining its significance."},{"object_id":"term:wave","preferred_label":"wave","reader_preview":"A wave is one bounded pass of work within a larger phase.","reader_card":"It has a stated objective, execution approach, permitted scope, and expected result. Plectis uses waves to organise progress into units that can be inspected and incorporated into the continuing programme.","reader_rule":"The live work record determines which wave is active or complete.","reader_deep":"A wave's closeout should say what changed, what was checked, and what remains. The organisational label does not itself establish progress; the useful outcome and its evidence do."},{"object_id":"term:whisper","preferred_label":"Whisper","reader_preview":"Whisper is OpenAI's speech-recognition model family for converting audio speech into text.","reader_card":"It can support transcription and related language tasks through suitable software. The surrounding recording workflow determines which timestamps, speaker information, or other metadata are supplied with the text.","reader_rule":"Do not assume that every transcript has accurate word-level timings or speaker labels.","reader_deep":"Recognition can make errors, especially with unfamiliar names, technical notation, or unclear audio. Plectis should preserve the source recording where exact wording matters and check important passages before treating them as authoritative quotations."},{"object_id":"term:whole_system_correctness","preferred_label":"whole-system correctness","reader_preview":"Whole-system correctness means satisfying a complete, stated specification for an entire system.","reader_card":"Such a claim must cover the relevant components, interactions, inputs, and environment. A collection of correct parts can still interact incorrectly or omit a requirement the overall task needs.","reader_rule":"Individual component checks do not automatically establish this broader property.","reader_deep":"Plectis uses the phrase to mark that evidence boundary. Its public examples establish particular behaviours or properties; any claim about the whole system would need a suitable specification and evidence covering the composition as well."},{"object_id":"term:witness","preferred_label":"check result","reader_preview":"A mathematical witness is an object showing that an existence claim holds; in Plectis's software records, the word can also mean evidence of a check.","reader_card":"For example, n = 3 witnesses that some positive integer has square 9. A software witness may instead be a saved verification record tied to a particular execution.","reader_rule":"The surrounding claim determines which kind of witness is required.","reader_deep":"Supplying an object is useful only when it satisfies the claimed property. Likewise, a record supports an execution claim only within what its source and recording process can establish."},{"object_id":"term:work_landing","preferred_label":"work landing","reader_preview":"Work landing is the point at which an attempted change becomes a recorded, accepted part of the system.","reader_card":"It connects the actual change with ownership, validation, and closeout information. In a repository workflow, that may include a scoped commit and the checks supporting it.","reader_rule":"Acceptance concerns the stated scope and criteria, not every possible use of the change.","reader_deep":"An unfinished draft or unrecorded success has not completed this transition. A useful landing record lets later work identify the resulting artifact, inspect its evidence, and understand any remaining obligations."},{"object_id":"term:working_record","preferred_label":"working record","reader_preview":"A working record is the detailed account of research on one problem, preserving results, attempts, evidence, and unresolved questions.","reader_card":"The mathematical records here are available as long-form papers or reading views. They retain reasoning and failed approaches that a short problem note may omit.","reader_rule":"Different passages can have different evidence status, so the record should identify what is proved, computed, cited, or open.","reader_deep":"Its purpose is to help another reader understand and continue the work. Preserving an attempted argument does not endorse it once a later correction or counterexample has been recorded."},{"object_id":"term:workingness","preferred_label":"workingness","reader_preview":"Workingness is Plectis's term for whether its evidence, beliefs, uncertainty, and permitted actions remain coherently connected.","reader_card":"It asks what the system currently claims, what supports those claims, what could invalidate them, and how decisions respond to missing or contradictory evidence. Passing tests are one input to that assessment.","reader_rule":"The term names a proposed evaluative idea, not a universal measure of reliability.","reader_deep":"Its value depends on concrete records and behaviour: an unsupported claim should change status, and an action depending on it should be reconsidered rather than silently proceeding."},{"object_id":"term:workingness_instrument","preferred_label":"Workingness Instrument","reader_preview":"A Workingness Instrument is a proposed structured record connecting one outcome to its evidence, dependencies, and permitted next actions.","reader_card":"It describes the claim, current status, possible invalidation conditions, unresolved disputes, and relevant repairs. The purpose is to make the consequences of a result explicit instead of leaving them in informal judgement.","reader_rule":"The record's existence does not establish that every described control has been implemented.","reader_deep":"For an implemented instance, source and execution evidence must show how changes in the record affect the actions that depend on it. The governing material identifies the current design and supported scope."}]};
